regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1074:
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1075:#  *** THE WIDTH BLIND SPOT.  Every #462 store to +0x18 was 32-bit, so a repair
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1076:#  conditioned on access width -- `if (len == 4) idata = 0;` -- passed the whole
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1077:#  table.  This tree ALREADY established, in the `gdromdmawidth` assessment,
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1078:#  that mainline Linux BYTE-writes this register, so that mutant is not
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1079:#  hypothetical: it breaks the one guest whose behaviour the round documented.
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1080:#
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1081:#  *** AND THIS ROW ALSO CARRIES THE TRIGGER-VALUE CLOSE. ***  A seat observed
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1082:#  that EVERY +0x18 trigger in every other row stores the value 1, so a repair
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1083:#  written `idata &= ~1u;` or `idata -= 1;` -- both plausible as "clear the
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1084:#  trigger bit" -- passed the whole table while any guest trigger >= 2 stuck.
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1085:#  The arm fires on any nonzero and the diagnostic does not print the trigger,
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1086:#  so triggering with 0x5a costs nothing and closes that family here.
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1087:#
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1088:#  NOTE the poison preset is 0xa5, NOT 0x5a as the other rows use.  The seat's
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1089:#  suggestion was to trigger with 0x5a while keeping the 0x5a preset -- but then
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1090:#  "the mutant left the trigger stuck" and "the load never executed" both read
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1091:#  back 0x5a and the row could not tell them apart.  Trigger and poison must
regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1092:#  DIFFER, which is the same three-outcome discipline `accept data` uses.
