1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
2:"""DETECTOR for gdromdefault, carrying the round's witness measurement.
3:
4:THE DEFECT: dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c registers a 0x100 window at 0x005f7000 and
5:its switch implements EIGHT offsets -- 0x18, 0x80, 0x84, 0x88, 0x8c, 0x90,
6:0x94, 0x9c.  Every other address in that window falls to `default:`, which
7:prints and then runs exit(1), for a READ or a WRITE, at any width.
8:
9:*** ONE PREDICATE, ROUGHLY 248 OF 256 BYTE OFFSETS LETHAL. ***  A driver or a
10:diagnostic that scans the device's register space -- an ordinary thing to do
11:before you know what is there -- ends the host process on its first unhandled
12:address.  That is a wider blast radius than the whole GDROM_DATA arm #460
13:fixed, which needed a specific register and, for two of three sites, specific
14:device state.
15:
16:CENSUS, measured on an unmodified `-E dreamcast -x`, one 32-bit guest load per
17:offset: 21 sampled, 19 DIED via this arm, 2 survived (0x90 and 0x94, both
18:implemented).  32-bit was chosen deliberately -- it reaches `default:` for an
19:unhandled offset WITHOUT first tripping GDROM_DATA's own width guard, so a
20:death is attributable to this arm and not to another.
21:
22:THE LATCH SHAPE IS THE ROUND'S DESIGN QUESTION, and #443 already answered the
23:same question in this tree: `uint32_t pcic_reported[NCLASS][(N_REGS+31)/32]`,
24:a PER-WORD BITMAP keyed by fault class, with its own comment recording why --
25:"A per-device flag would hide it ... 137 named flags would be absurd.  This is
26:five words."  A single flag here would let ONE unimplemented access silence
27:the other 247; a named constant per offset is absurd at this width.
28:
29:*** DREAMCAST NEEDS -x OR IT NEVER REACHES THE DEBUGGER PROMPT. ***  Two
30:console inputs, and console.c:994-1007 makes that fatal without allow_slaves.
31:Without it a session dies for a CONFIGURATION reason and prints the same
32:"died" the defect prints -- that cost #459 a false positive.
33:
34:Rung 3 by the witness ladder: real guest instructions through real address
35:decode on an UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description.
36:
37:CONTROLS RUN IN THEIR OWN SESSIONS AND NEVER TOUCH AN UNIMPLEMENTED OFFSET,
38:so a red trigger row cannot be confused with a broken session.
39:
40:*** CONTRACT (vacuity floor clause (e)) -- WHAT THESE ROWS ARE DESIGNED TO
41:CATCH, WHAT THEY ARE NOT, AND WHAT IS KNOWN TO ESCAPE THEM. ***  A detector
42:that says so is falsifiable; one claiming completeness is not, and a later
43:escape then becomes an argument about overclaiming instead of a contract
44:question with an answer.
45:
46:SCOPE.  This contract covers the #462 `case 0x18` DMA rows added below.  The
47:#461 `default:`-arm rows are governed by the census header above; an escape
48:against one of THOSE is adjudicated there, not here.
49:
50:DESIGNED TO CATCH -- each backed by a mutant that measurably reddens a row,
51:not by intent:
52:
53:  * reinstatement of either `exit(1)`                        (Mb1, Mb2)
54:  * loss, RELOCATION, CONDITIONING or LATCH-SCOPING of the
55:    `idata = 0` shadow clear, at either declining arm or at
56:    the success arm                          (M1, M3, M6, M7, M12)
57:  * collapse of the two warn-bit latches onto ONE bit (M2), or
58:    loosening of the ARMING latch into a counter (M4)
59:  * a completion event SET on a declined transfer            (M8, M9)
60:  * outward growth of the arming test                        (M10)
61:  * an operand swap in the no-data guard                     (M11)
62:    *** #490: THIS CLAIM IS EXPIRED AND THE MEASUREMENT SAYS SO.  M11 IS NOW
63:    EQUIVALENT -- NO ROW KILLS IT, BECAUSE THERE IS NOTHING LEFT TO KILL. ***
64:      artifacts/gdromexitsix/m11_rescore_20260828/m11_58row.txt
65:      (a 58-row measurement: these are the counts AS MEASURED THEN, not today's
66:       denominator.  The probe has since grown and the gate pin is authoritative;
67:       rewriting these to match it would falsify the record they exist to preserve.)
68:        fixed  GDROM_DEF_RESULT=58/58   nodata stale/stale says/stale idle all ok   [58-row denominator, as measured then]
69:        M11    GDROM_DEF_RESULT=58/58   ... byte-identical   [58-row denominator, as measured then]
70:
71:    THE CHRONOLOGY, from git rather than memory, because every record here was
72:    CORRECT WHEN WRITTEN and one of them was overtaken:
73:      2026-08-25  abf665c  M11 measured DETERMINISTICALLY LETHAL -- five runs, five
74:                           SIGSEGVs, written out in words rather than as a ratio
75:                           because an N/N in this docstring is indistinguishable
76:                           from a stale ROW SCORE against the gate's pin of 58.
77:                           (The round that first measured it recorded exactly this
78:                           remedy; I wrote the ratio anyway and section U caught it.)
79:                           Preceded by
80:                           `Transfering 408 bytes to 0x00000000` -- a real NULL read.
81:                           That round recorded, honestly, that A RE-SCORE WAS OWED.
82:      2026-08-26  4d05cf0  #465 `data and data_len were released apart` -- free_data
83:                           now clears BOTH.  THE CRASH BECAME IMPOSSIBLE.
84:      2026-08-28           the owed re-score, finally run: EQUIVALENT.
85:    `git merge-base --is-ancestor` confirms the fix is NOT an ancestor of the SIGSEGV
86:    measurement, so the two results do not contradict -- they are one day apart.
87:
88:    *** THE LESSON IS NOT ABOUT M11.  A KILL RECORD IS TIME-INDEXED EXACTLY AS AN
89:    ABSENCE RECORD IS. ***  The carrier already requires that an absence record NAME
90:    ITS WARRANT and that any edit touching that warrant RE-RUNS the death measurement.
91:    Nothing said the same about a claim that a mutant IS caught -- so #465 edited the
92:    warrant, the owed re-score was written down the day before, and three rounds ran
93:    with this contract asserting coverage it no longer had.
94:
95:    KEPT, NOT DELETED: the mutant is still worth listing.  A future edit that
96:    re-separates the two fields makes M11 lethal again, and this entry is where a
97:    reader learns that -- which a deleted line could not do.
98:  * REFUSAL OR CORRUPTION OF A LEGAL TRANSFER -- legal arming still enters
99:    the path, the completion still fires, and the device's own TOC byte
100:    still lands                                              (M5)
101:
102:*** THE `idata = 0` VERB LIST IS DELIBERATELY WIDE. ***  The mutants do not
103:merely DELETE that clear: M3 relocates it after the `break`, M6 conditions it
104:on fresh-device state, M12 scopes it inside the latch.  A contract saying only
105:"loss" would let a future conditioning escape be argued outside it.
106:
107:*** AND THE LATCH CLAUSE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. ***  M4 is anchored on
108:`GDROM_W_DMAARM`, so only the ARMING latch is measured; NO mutant loosens the
109:no-data latch alone.  Claiming "either latch" would be an overclaim inside a
110:contract, which is worse than not writing one.
111:
112:KNOWN TO ESCAPE -- measured, filed, and named here so a rediscovery can be
113:adjudicated instead of re-argued:
114:
115:  * `gdromdmaclear` -- a completion event CLEARED on a decline.  The SET
116:    direction is caught (M8/M9); the CLEAR direction escapes every row.
117:  * `gdromdmaguard` -- `count == 1` widened to `count <= 1`, so `(0,1)` is
118:    accepted.  `arm halfarm` cannot see it.
119:  * `gdromdmasilent` -- `if (idata != 0)` narrowed to `if (idata == 1)`.
120:
121:*** THAT LIST IS THE HALF THAT DOES THE ADJUDICATING. ***  Without it, the
122:next reader to rediscover `gdromdmaclear` cannot tell a FLOOR BREACH (L2, fix
123:in-round) from FILED COVERAGE DEBT -- which is exactly the distinction clause
124:(e) exists to make mechanical.
125:
126:NOT CAUGHT, structurally:
127:
128:  * *** THE SURVIVAL ROWS DETECT PROCESS TERMINATION ONLY. ***  `arm survive`,
129:    `nodata live` and `nodata stale` compute `"survived" if alive else "died"`,
130:    and `alive` is False ONLY when a read raises OSError or returns empty INSIDE
131:    a budget window -- so they are true of an emulator that is HUNG, one that is
132:    FROZEN, and, measured, one that has ALREADY BEEN KILLED.
133:
134:    *** THIS PARAGRAPH SAID "False ONLY on EOF ... true of an emulator that is
135:    HUNG but has not exited", AND BOTH HALVES ARE MEASURABLY WRONG. ***
136:
137:      * `rd()` returns False on `except OSError` as well as on EOF, and on Linux
138:        a pty-master read after the child dies gives EIO -- so the OSError arm is
139:        the USUAL death path, not an exotic one.  "Only on EOF" mislabels the
140:        common case.
141:      * The defect is not on either False path.  It is on the THIRD path: the
142:        loop is `while time.time() < end:`, so it returns True when the budget
143:        expires having read nothing.  **A death that lands after the last budget
144:        check is never read.**  Measured on a rig built from this file's own
145:        session()/rd(): a process SIGKILLed just before its session's `quit`
146:        scores `alive == True`.
147:
148:    So `alive` does not mean "hung but not exited".  It does not even mean
149:    "not exited".  It means: no EOF and no OSError were observed inside the
150:    sampled windows.  A red `alive == died` also cannot distinguish a clean exit
151:    from a pty error.
152:    Hang-shaped regressions are the IDLE rows' subject, not theirs.
153:
154:  * The `absent` path is DESIGNED to fire when a readback produces no output
155:    (e.g. a wedged handler).  It HAS fired once --
156:    `regress/artifacts/kill462/table3_58rows/probe_M11.raw`, row `stale idle`
157:    -- and that firing's CAUSE IS UNATTRIBUTED: a hang and a session
158:    outrunning its spin budget are both consistent with it -- AND SO IS A THIRD
159:    CAUSE THIS LIST OMITTED, WHICH A CLEAN REBUILD REPRODUCES DETERMINISTICALLY:
160:    A HOST DEATH THIS PREDICATE MISSED.  The M11 session was re-run five times
161:    on a tree synced byte-identical to SEC, and EVERY ONE OF THE FIVE gave a SIGSEGV
162:    -- written out rather than as a ratio, because an `N/N` in a probe docstring
163:    is indistinguishable from a stale ROW SCORE, and the score-claims gate
164:    correctly flagged the ratio form against this probe's pin of 58.  Preceded by
165:    `Transfering 408 bytes to 0x00000000 (guest asked for 0)`.  The shipped
166:    table3 entry records `survived` for that session.  Detection is proven;
167:    diagnosis is not claimed -- and the leading diagnosis is now the one the
168:    original list did not contain.
169:
170:  * No row observes interrupt ASSERTION.  The completion rows observe the
171:    SYSASIC pending register, which is a different channel.
172:
173:Filed for the gaps rather than left implicit: `probetimebased` carries the
174:liveness predicate's soundness defect and its fix shape (a three-state
175:predicate -- terminated / responsive / unresponsive), and `lethalonly` carries
176:the one row whose only kill is lethal.
