1:# GXemul 0.7.0 hardened fork — standing instructions (every session)
2:
3:`GXEMUL-SEC/` is the git repo. *** OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-08-22: LOCAL GIT ONLY -- DO
4:NOT PUSH. *** Commit as normal; do not run `git push`, and do not treat an unpushed
5:commit as an exposure (the older "an unpushed round is the largest single exposure"
6:line below is superseded for as long as this holds). The remote is left configured and
7:untouched -- it is the owner's plumbing, and nothing here removes or rewrites it.
8:(Historic note, still true if pushing ever resumes: push from Windows git-bash, never
9:WSL.) `est/` is its
10:non-git twin; `build/` (pmax) and WSL `/tmp/gxsec-build` (arc) are in-place build trees
11:with **NO VPATH** — propagate every edit into them or you measure a stale binary.
12:
13:**First stop each session:** `git -C GXEMUL-SEC log --oneline -5` (memory summaries go
14:stale; the repo is the truth), then `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` (in-flight uncommitted
15:work + **build-tree dirtiness** — see below), then `GXEMUL-SEC/OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` (the
16:"OPEN LIST" head plus the dated tail entries = the live queue) and the harness TaskList.
17:
18:**Checkpoints (standing directive, 2026-08-11): survive power/Windows outages.**
19:1. `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` is OVERWRITTEN (never appended) at every round boundary
20:   and before/after any build-tree mutation. It records only what git cannot: uncommitted
21:   files, in-flight measurements, and which build trees are dirty and how to restore
22:   them. Its own staleness rule: if its HEAD line disagrees with `git log`, re-derive
23:   and rewrite it. (RESUME.md once went stale for 26 rounds — a checkpoint that grows or
24:   lingers is worse than none.)
25:2. **Any script that mutates a build tree MUST drop a sentinel first** —
26:   `build/.MUTANT: <file> <what>` — and remove it only after restore is VERIFIED by
27:   `cmp` against GXEMUL-SEC (the repo source, NEVER the script's own backup: a crashed
28:   run once left the tree mutant, so the next run's "orig" backup captured the mutant
29:   and its restore restored it). A fresh session finding a `.MUTANT` sentinel restores
30:   from SEC (`rm src/cpus/*.o`, rebuild) before trusting ANY measurement.
31:   > ### *** AND THE RESTORE IS NOT FINISHED UNTIL THE BINARY IS REBUILT. THE SENTINEL
32:   > ### PROTECTS THE SOURCE; THE THING MEASURED IS THE BINARY. Measured 2026-08-27. ***
33:   >
34:   > ### *** AND IT IS NOT ONLY A MUTATION HAZARD. MEASURED AGAIN 2026-08-27, WITH NO
35:   > ### MUTANT ANYWHERE IN THE STORY. ***
36:   >
37:   > `build/gxemul` was found at `05:52:15` sitting on a `dev_wdc.c` of `14:56:12` -- nine
38:   > hours older than the only file that had changed, so it predated three shipped rounds.
39:   > **No script mutated anything.** A round propagated its edit into `build/src/` (as the
40:   > no-VPATH rule requires) and nobody rebuilt.
41:   >
42:   > **So part 1 passed and part 2 failed on a tree that was never mutant**, and the
43:   > SENTINEL DISCIPLINE CANNOT CATCH THIS CLASS AT ALL -- sentinels are dropped around
44:   > mutations, and there was no mutation. `CHECKPOINT.md` asserted `build/ CLEAN`,
45:   > written that same afternoon by the same loop that had done the propagating.
46:   >
47:   > > **THE TWO-PART CLEARANCE IS A ROUND-BOUNDARY OBLIGATION, NOT A MUTATION-RESTORE
48:   > > ONE. RUN IT; DO NOT ASSERT IT.** The checkpoint's build-tree section records the
49:   > > OUTPUT of the check, never a remembered state:
50:   > >
51:   > >     cmp -s build/src/devices/<edited>.c GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/<edited>.c
52:   > >     [ build/gxemul -nt build/src/devices/<edited>.c ]
53:   >
54:   > ### *** AND "ALL FOUR TREES" IS A CATEGORY ERROR: ONLY THREE ARE BUILD TREES, AND ONE
55:   > ### OF THOSE WAS FIVE HOURS STALE WHILE A COMMIT MESSAGE SAID OTHERWISE.  Measured
56:   > ### 2026-08-31 by the `#499` pass-2 seat. ***
57:   >
58:   > The propagation rule names four trees, and a reader takes that to mean four two-part
59:   > clearances.  Measured:
60:   >
61:   >     build/              BUILD tree  -- owes both parts
62:   >     /tmp/gxsec-build    BUILD tree  -- owes both parts  (the arc rig)
63:   >     build-asan/         BUILD tree  -- owes both parts  (configured, 224 objects)
64:   >     est/                SOURCE tree -- NO BINARY EXISTS.  Part 2 is INAPPLICABLE,
65:   >                                        not merely unchecked.
66:   >
67:   > `#499`'s commit message asserted *"All four trees now satisfy BOTH parts"*.  **False in
68:   > two different ways**: `build-asan`'s binary was from `02:20` under a source from `07:34`
69:   > -- a five-hour stale trap for the next ASAN sweep -- and `est/` has no binary at all, so
70:   > the claim was category-wrong there rather than merely untrue.
71:   >
72:   > **The sharp part is that the CORRECT sentence exists one commit later.**  `a481d8b` says
73:   > "BOTH build trees" and is right; `9f666a8`'s stood uncorrected, and `build-asan` stayed
74:   > stale for the ~5 hours between them.  A propagation that copies a source into every tree
75:   > and rebuilds only the ones you happen to measure leaves the rest holding new source over
76:   > old objects -- which is the two-part rule's own failure mode, reached by obeying half of
77:   > it.
78:   >
79:   > *Propagate to all four.  Run part 1 on all four.  Run part 2 on the THREE that have a
80:   > binary, and say `est/: source tree, part 2 N/A` rather than leaving a blank -- an unrun
81:   > check and an inapplicable one must not render the same.*
82:   >
83:   > ### *** THAT `cmp` CANNOT BE RUN ON THE SIX DECLARED DIVERGENCE PATHS, AND NOTHING SAID
84:   > ### SO UNTIL 2026-08-31.  Measured, not reasoned. ***
85:   >
86:   > `build/` matches **`est/`**, not `GXEMUL-SEC/`, for every path in
87:   > `tools/est_divergence.txt`.  Measured by running the recipe against all six:
88:   >
89:   >     src/devices/.index          DIVERGENT      src/devices/dev_jazz.c      DIVERGENT
90:   >     src/devices/Makefile.skel   DIVERGENT      src/devices/dev_ne2000.c    ABSENT in build/
91:   >     src/devices/autodev.c       DIVERGENT      src/machines/machine_arc.c  DIVERGENT
92:   >
93:   > and confirming the direction on one of them: `cmp build/src/devices/dev_jazz.c
94:   > est/src/devices/dev_jazz.c` is CLEAN while the same file against SEC differs.  `build/`
95:   > is the **pmax** tree and the ARC/Jazz layer is the SEC-only addition, so a pmax build has
96:   > no use for it -- but **whether that is by design or by history is NOT established here**,
97:   > and this note claims only what was measured.
98:   >
99:   > **THE TRAP, and it is the dangerous direction:** a round editing one of those six in SEC
100:   > runs the clearance, reads `DIVERGENT`, and concludes the tree is MUTANT -- then "restores"
101:   > `build/` from SEC and silently converts the pmax build tree into something neither tree
102:   > was.  *The recipe's own failure mode is to prescribe the corruption it exists to prevent.*
103:   >
104:   > **SO: the clearance applies to SHARED files only.  For a divergence-set path there is no
105:   > two-part clearance -- compare against `est/`, or do not compare.**  Derive the set, never
106:   > quote it: `grep -vE '^\s*(#|$)' GXEMUL-SEC/tools/est_divergence.txt`.
107:   >
108:   > *Latent rather than live: every file this session touched (`dev_sgi_re.c`, `dev_wdc.c`) is
109:   > shared, so the recipe was correct every time it was run. It was found by reading a
110:   > measuring seat's `BASELINE.out`, which listed the clone's divergences from SEC and made
111:   > the pattern visible -- the seat had already noticed and named it, and I checked it.*
112:   >
113:   > Found by a rung-3 witness seat that noticed the impossible timestamp pair and
114:   > measured against its own `/tmp` tree instead, saying so plainly. **Nothing in the
115:   > harness would have reported it** -- and a `build/` measurement taken in that window
116:   > would have tested pre-`#481` code against a tree that `cmp`s clean.
117:   >
118:   > *Note the checker to build here is NOT another sentinel. It is two lines in the
119:   > checkpoint step, because the failure is a habit -- propagate, forget to rebuild --
120:   > and the carrier's own finding is that a rule fires when it lives where the decision
121:   > happens.*
122:   >
123:   > A mutant script restored `dev_dreamcast_asic.c`, `cmp`'d it clean against SEC, and
124:   > cleared the sentinel — exactly as this rule says. **It left a MUTANT `gxemul` on
125:   > disk.** The tell was an impossible pair of timestamps: binary `05:47:23`, restored
126:   > source `05:51:44` — *the binary was OLDER than the source it was supposedly built
127:   > from.*
128:   >
129:   > A later probe would then have measured the mutant against a tree that `cmp`s clean
130:   > and carries no sentinel — **the stale-binary trap reached from the other side, and
131:   > with the sentinel discipline fully obeyed.**
132:   >
133:   > **THE CLEARANCE CONDITION IS TWO-PART: source `cmp`s clean against SEC AND
134:   > `[ binary -nt source ]`.** Rebuild inside the restore, and leave the sentinel in
135:   > place if the rebuild does not produce a fresh binary — a sentinel that outlives its
136:   > mutant costs one restore; one cleared over a mutant binary costs a wrong
137:   > measurement nobody suspects.
138:3. Commit+push at every green gate — an unpushed round is the largest single exposure.
139:4. *** KILL TABLES RUN IN A SCRATCH CLONE BY DEFAULT. Mutating `build/` is the EXCEPTION and
140:   carries its stated reason in the checkpoint. Owner decision 2026-08-28, on measured
141:   practice rather than on proposal. ***
142:
143:   `#489`'s pass-2 seat ran a nine-log table in `/tmp/gxopus489` (a relocatable `cp -a` of
144:   `build/`) **CONCURRENTLY with the main loop's own table holding `build/.MUTANT`**, wrote
145:   into no project tree, and left its scratch `cmp`-clean. **Two tables at once, zero commits
146:   blocked, zero restores owed.** The relief is measured.
147:
148:   > **The obvious objection is backwards, and it is worth stating because I raised it: a
149:   > mutant table NEVER measures the battery's binary.** Every measurement is of a MUTANT
150:   > binary built over baseline sources. The load-bearing identity is SOURCE identity with the
151:   > intended baseline, which `cmp` establishes in a clone exactly as in `build/` -- and
152:   > `build/`'s own binary has twice been caught STALE over clean sources. *A clone that
153:   > rebuilds its baseline before mutant 1 is more trustworthy than an in-place run inheriting
154:   > whatever binary sat there.*
155:
156:   **The recipe that makes the default safe, each a checkpoint line:** (1) **NAME THE
157:   BASELINE** -- HEAD or the round's post-fix working set -- and `cmp` the files-to-be-mutated
158:   against it before mutant 1, since a clone inherits whatever `build/` held mid-round;
159:   (2) **rebuild the baseline binary IN THE CLONE** and check `[ binary -nt source ]` -- the
160:   base-table run doubles as the freshness proof; (3) clone-local sentinel plus the
161:   frozen-copy driver rule, unchanged; (4) the volatile-path rule **with extra force** -- /tmp
162:   evaporates, and copying any number a durable record will cite into `regress/artifacts/`
163:   comes BEFORE the record is written.
164:
165:   **For the `build/`-mutating exception nothing is overturned: TOTAL blast radius, two-part
166:   clearance.** Narrowed in population, unchanged in scope. *No checker -- there is no
167:   mechanical observable for "should have cloned", and a check that cannot fail is worse than
168:   none.*
169:
170:## *** WHERE A LESSON GOES: STRUCTURE FIRST, PROSE ONLY AS FALLBACK. ***
171:## Owner-adopted 2026-08-26. Read this before adding anything to this file.
172:
173:> **Every incident must first propose a STRUCTURAL landing spot — a check, a gate section, a
174:> brief-template line, a script practice. A carrier paragraph is the FALLBACK, used only when
175:> no structure can hold the lesson. And a rule that earns its SECOND INSTANCE while sitting in
176:> this file has thereby proven it was mis-filed as prose.**
177:
178:**The evidence is one day's own record, and it falls on both sides.** What changed outcomes was
179:structure: `check_seats_read.py` was taught ONE filename mapping and surfaced three unread
180:reviews the same day; `panel.sh` gained a fourth Ollama seat and it has answered in every panel
181:since; a kill table now runs from a frozen copy. What failed *while written down here* was prose:
182:the frozen-copy rule was violated by its own author hours after he wrote it, the
183:guest-drivable-`fatal()`-needs-a-latch rule went unconsulted by a round that debated exactly that
184:question, the unread-review class reached its third instance, and the frozen-number class its
185:fourth generation.
186:
187:> **THIS FILE IS NOT FAILING AS MEMORY. IT IS FAILING AS A TRIGGER.** The frozen-copy rule was
188:> *cited by the person breaking it*, on a different object, in the same hour. Knowing a rule and
189:> having it fire on the right object are different problems, and only the second one is solved by
190:> writing more.
191:
192:**The carve-out is real and must not be eroded:** judgment rules — option-set completeness,
193:adjacency, severity ranking — cannot be mechanised and belong here. But note *why* the
194:premise-audit paragraph is the one piece of prose that demonstrably works: it lives in the BRIEF
195:TEMPLATE, the artifact present at the moment of decision. **That is structure in the sense that
196:matters — text located where the decision happens, not text located where the reasoning was
197:recorded.**
198:
199:Ethos (non-negotiable): terse portable C99, minimal surgical changes, `/* #NNN: */` tags,
200:a CHANGELOG.md round block per correction (REVIEW_FINDINGS.md is frozen at #290 — see its
201:scope banner, added by #385; rows #291+ live in the CHANGELOG round blocks), honest
202:"assessed, not changed" records. **Test-first:** reproduce on the committed build before
203:any edit — where *reproduce* is defined by the WITNESS LADDER below; a defect
204:with no witness gets documented instead. No session URLs
205:in commit messages (keep `Co-Authored-By`).
206:
207:## SEAT LEDGER — which seats were USED, which were NOT, and why (keep this current)
208:
209:**Record it every round.** A seat that did not answer is a SEAT FAILURE, never agreement, and a
210:seat that was deliberately not fired is a different thing again — conflating the two is how a
211:two-seat review gets reported as a panel. State both, by name, in the round record.
212:
213:*** DO NOT LOOK FOR SEAT STATUS IN THIS FILE. *** Live status is `panel.sh`'s seat check and
214:the ledger; both are generated and neither can go stale. **Two status snapshots used to live
215:here — an 08-19 table headed "CURRENT" and an 08-14 one below it — and by 2026-08-26 the first
216:was wrong about every seat it named while still calling itself current.** A status line in an
217:auto-loaded file is a wrong record waiting for a calendar. The 08-14 table is kept below for its
218:REASONING only; read no status out of it.
219:
220:**THE ONE LESSON WORTH THE SPACE, and it cost a real panel to learn: VERIFY THE CONTENT, NEVER
221:THE SIZE.** Codex once returned the **LARGEST file in its panel — 26,992 bytes** — and it was the
222:echoed brief followed by "You've hit your usage limit", twice. *A size check scores that the best
223:answer of the nine.* The failure mode is a big file, which is why every seat check in this
224:project greps the content and the TAIL rather than measuring bytes.
225:
226:### *** A WALL IS A MOMENT, NOT A STATE — MEASURED 2026-08-23, and it CONTRADICTS the
227:### "never by a date" line in the Fable row below. ***
228:
229:*** AND THE SAME DAY, THE OTHER HALF OF THAT RULE WAS CONFIRMED FOR THE THIRD TIME:
230:AN ACCOUNT-STATE CHANGE REVIVED A SEAT THAT TWO FIRINGS HAD FOUND WALLED. ***
231:
232:The Fable seat failed twice on 2026-08-23 with `You've reached your Fable 5 limit` — the
233:second firing deliberate, per the re-fire-a-walled-seat-once rule. **The owner then ran
234:`/login`, and the next firing returned BOTH liveness tokens** (the prompt nonce and a
235:verbatim quote from `CHECKPOINT.md`), so prompt delivery and file access were proven
236:rather than assumed. It then produced the round's adjudication: seven wrong records and
237:one undischarged obligation.
238:
239:**So both halves now have measured instances:** Kimi revived across a date boundary with
240:no account change (cause NOT established), and Fable has now revived twice on a `/login`.
241:The operational rule is unchanged and is the cheap one: **TEST THE SEAT, do not reason
242:about why it might be back.** One Agent call with a liveness token settles it in seconds.
243:
244:**AND THE HOLD WAS WORTH IT, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING.** The round was held
245:uncommitted for this seat by owner decision across two failures. Waiting cost nothing —
246:the work was checkpointed and unrelated work was split into its own commit — and the
247:seat that eventually answered found the round's one L3 obligation, which no other seat
248:had named across two full panels.
249:
250:
251:Kimi walled mid-panel at 23:01 with a `403 usage limit for this billing cycle`. **One hour
252:later, across the date boundary, a liveness test returned its token on the first try.** No
253:account-state change was made. So the sentence below — *"Both 'dead' Anthropic-side states
254:this project has seen were revived by ACCOUNT-STATE CHANGES … never by a date"* — has a
255:counter-example. **Cause is NOT established** (a billing cycle rolling at midnight is a
256:guess, not a measurement); what is established is that the seat was walled and then was not.
257:
258:**The ADVICE in that row was right even though its stated reason was wrong**, which is worth
259:keeping separate: *"DO NOT ASSUME A CALENDAR DATE RESTORES A SEAT — TEST IT"* is exactly what
260:settled this, in one invocation and about four seconds.
261:
262:**Two harness consequences, both applied:**
263:* **A WALLED SEAT IS RE-FIRED ONCE before the round closes.** It costs a minute and recovers
264:  a seat the round would otherwise silently lose. The owner caught this one only because the
265:  seat showed as available in their own UI — which is a human noticing, i.e. the thing this
266:  harness exists to stop depending on.
267:  > *** THE RULE IS SOUND AND "BOTH APPLIED" WAS FALSE ABOUT THIS HALF. Measured 2026-08-26
268:  > by an audit of this file. *** `panel.sh` printed **"re-firing once (a wall can be minutes
269:  > long) ..."** and then `return 2` — and every caller is a bare `check "name" "$file"` whose
270:  > exit status is discarded, with no seat command re-invoked anywhere afterwards. **Nothing
271:  > was ever relaunched.**
272:  >
273:  > **That is worse than a stale citation.** A stale citation misleads a reader who follows
274:  > it; this printed a claim of ACTION to the operator at the moment a seat was lost, so the
275:  > one person who could re-fire it by hand was told it had already been done. It is the
276:  > failure `panel.sh` itself warns about sixty lines earlier — *"a documented check that
277:  > cannot fail, which is worse than no check because it reassures."*
278:  >
279:  > The message is now an INSTRUCTION, not a false report. **Automatic re-firing is FILED,
280:  > not done**, because it is a behaviour change to the script every panel runs and it owes
281:  > two negative controls: prove the retry fires on a walled seat, and prove a seat that
282:  > answered is NOT retried.
283:### *** THE CHECK-THAT-CANNOT-FAIL: TWO INSTANCES IN ONE DAY, AND THE HUNT FOR A THIRD
284:### CAME BACK EMPTY IN THE PLACE YOU WOULD EXPECT IT. Measured 2026-08-26. ***
285:
286:Two shortcuts were found inert on the same day, and both had the same shape -- **a cheap
287:substitute for a real test, which returns the same answer whether or not the thing it checks
288:is true**:
289:
290:* `panel.sh` printed **"re-firing once (a wall can be minutes long) ..."** and re-fired
291:  nothing; every caller discards the `return 2`.
292:* The seat table called `grok models` **"itself a cheap tier check"** for whether the paid
293:  subscription is live. Running it prints **`You are not authenticated.`** and lists both
294:  models anyway -- the list is served without auth, so it answers identically in both states.
295:
296:**THE HUNT FOR A THIRD WENT TO THE GATES AND FOUND NOTHING, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH
297:RECORDING.** 251 `check` rows were scanned for a want that is literally the got expression.
298:Twenty matched -- and **all twenty are the GUARDED parse idiom**, where an `if` arm emits
299:`"no" "yes"` and the `else` arm emits `"yes" "yes"` for the same label, so the row fails
300:loudly when parsing fails. **Zero unguarded.** A row of mine that looked self-comparing
301:(`"${wfres%/*}" "${wfres#*/}"`) was checked too: the two halves are the numerator and
302:denominator of one probe string -- different quantities -- and it reds whenever the score is
303:below its own denominator, with a companion row pinning that denominator to a literal.
304:
305:> **SO THE CLASS LIVES IN PROSE AND SHORTCUTS, NOT IN CHECK ROWS.** The gates are written by
306:> people thinking about failure; the shortcuts are written by people trying to AVOID running
307:> something. **A check that cannot fail is almost never a badly-written assertion here -- it
308:> is a claim that some cheap observable stands in for an expensive one.** Ask of any such
309:> shortcut: *what does it print when the thing is FALSE?* Both instances above answer "the
310:> same thing", and neither had ever been asked.
311:
312:*Both were found by the exemplar audit, not by a gate, and neither could have been: one is a
313:`say` in a shell script and the other is a sentence in a table.*
314:
315:* ***`panel.sh` COULD NOT SEE THIS WALL AT ALL.*** Its check only caught the ECHO-ONLY shape
316:  (brief echoed, tiny payload — the codex signature). Kimi's wall was **36 KB of real content,
317:  98% novel, ending mid-sentence with the notice**: payload huge, echo absent, invisible to
318:  every test it had, and printed as a healthy answer. It now also checks the TAIL, using the
319:  same POSITION discriminator `seat_verdicts.py` already used — which is why the extractor
320:  caught what the panel script missed.
321:
322:### *** A FOURTH CLASS, MEASURED 2026-08-24: ONE SHARED SERVICE BEHIND TWO SEATS.
323:### THE ROSTER SAYS NINE; AVAILABILITY SAYS EIGHT. ***
324:
325:Opus 5 failed three consecutive times on `API Error: 529 Overloaded`. Fable 5 was
326:then asked to do its job -- it is the only other seat with build and execute access
327:-- and failed on **the same error**. Meanwhile all seven CLI/cloud seats answered
328:normally inside the same window.
329:
330:So the diagnosis is not *"the Opus seat is overloaded"*. **The Agent-tool path is
331:overloaded, and both Anthropic seats sit behind it.**
332:
333:It is none of the three classes already recorded: no quota text, so the wall rule
334:passes it; substantive partial answers on two of the four attempts, so the stall rule
335:passes it; and the CLI seats share no transport with these, so the daemon rule does
336:not apply.
337:
338:> **THE CONSEQUENCE, which matters more than the diagnosis: the two Anthropic seats
339:> are independent for REASONING and correlated for AVAILABILITY.**  They are the only
340:> two that compile and run. A panel that counts nine seats and loses both to one
341:> outage has lost **all** of its compile-and-measure capacity, and a round whose
342:> remaining obligation is a MEASURED KILL then has no fallback anywhere in the
343:> roster.
344:
345:That happened on `#462`: everything was measured except the mutant table, and the
346:only seats that could produce one went down together. **A held round is the correct
347:response** — the owner chose it — but the roster should not be read as nine
348:independent chances at a measurement. It is seven readings and one measuring
349:capability with two front ends.
350:
351:*** AND IT CAN. ESTABLISHED 2026-08-24, DURING THE OUTAGE THAT PROMPTED THE
352:QUESTION. ***
353:
354:`agy` was given a bounded capability test -- build the tree unmodified, then build
355:ONE mutant and score it -- with an explicit instruction to say so plainly and stop
356:if it could not. It produced `/tmp/gxagy/gxemul` (6.1 MB), applied the mutant
357:correctly (2 `idata = 0;` against the repo's 3), and ran the 44-row probe against
358:its own binary. **It builds and it measures.**
359:
360:> **So the roster has THREE compile-and-measure front ends, not two, and only two of
361:> them share the Agent-tool service.**  `agy` is on an entirely separate path, which
362:> is exactly the property the fourth failure class showed was missing.
363:
364:Two cautions, both from how this test was set up rather than from anything agy did:
365:* **It has no sandbox.** `codex` is read-only and `grok` is plan-mode by
366:  construction; `agy` runs with `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, so the BRIEF is the
367:  only thing keeping it out of the repo. Spell out "copy the tree, build the copy,
368:  never write to GXEMUL-SEC" every time, and verify afterwards rather than trusting.
369:* **Give it the build traps explicitly.** `-d Gentoo` (the default distro has no
370:  `make`), `rm` the `.o` first, and check `[ binary -nt source ]` because `MAKE_RC`
371:  has been observed reporting 0 on a failed build. It followed all three when told;
372:  none of them are guessable.
373:
374:*The capability question sat unasked for months and cost this round its fallback.
375:It took one bounded invocation to answer. Ask the cheap question early.*
376:
377:### *** A THIRD SEAT-FAILURE CLASS, MEASURED 2026-08-22: THE TRANSPORT, NOT THE SEAT. ***
378:
379:All THREE Ollama seats returned 155 bytes each in one firing —
380:`ConnectionRefusedError(10061)` on `localhost:11434`. **The daemon was not running at all.**
381:Not a wall, not a stall, not a brief defect: the models were available the whole time and one
382:`ollama serve` recovered every one of them into the same panel dir.
383:
384:**It defeated every rule this file had.** It carries no quota keyword, so the wall rule passes
385:it. It is 100% novel against the brief, so the echo rule passes it. It is short — but so is a
386:stall, and this file already warns that short does not mean dead. `seat_verdicts.py` therefore
387:filed all three under *"answered, NO VERDICT LINE (format miss)"*: **three DOWN seats recorded
388:as live seats with bad formatting**, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape in
389:its purest form.
390:
391:Fixed in both places, and the two fixes are different on purpose: `seat_verdicts.py` reports
392:`SEAT DOWN` and **separates "daemon refused the connection" from "transport/model error"
393:because the remedies differ**, and `panel.sh` now PRE-FLIGHTS the daemon and starts it, since
394:starting it is cheaper than losing three seats. If it still will not come up the script says
395:so loudly rather than leaving it to be read out of three error files.
396:
397:**The general rule: a WALL is the seat refusing and you wait it out; a REFUSED CONNECTION is
398:the transport being absent and one command fixes it. Reporting the second as the first
399:silently gives up seats that were available all along.** Check the daemon before blaming the
400:seat — `curl -s localhost:11434/api/tags` is the whole test.
401:
402:### *** A SIXTH CLASS, MEASURED 2026-08-25: ONE TRANSPORT WALLED, ANOTHER LIVE — AND THIS IS
403:### THE ONLY CLASS WHERE THE SEAT IS AVAILABLE THE WHOLE TIME AND NOBODY ASKS. ***
404:
405:*(Numbering, because a reader counting headings in this file finds third, fourth, then sixth:
406:**the FIFTH is `seatthinkburn` in the ledger — an empty response wrapped in 260 KB of thinking,
407:which every size check scores as the best answer in the panel — and it was never written up
408:here.** The hole is in this file, not in the count. Filed, not lost.)*
409:
410:### *** THE FIFTH CLASS, WRITTEN UP AT LAST — AND THE 2026-08-26 INSTANCES SHOW IT IS DRIVEN BY
411:### THE BRIEF, NOT BY THE SEAT. ***
412:
413:`deepseek-v4-pro:cloud` returns `"response": ""` inside a JSON object whose `thinking` field is
414:hundreds of KB, with `done_reason: "length"` and `eval_count: 65536`. **It spends its entire
415:output budget on hidden reasoning and never emits an answer.**
416:
417:> ### *** THAT SIGNATURE IS ONE INSTANCE OF THE CLASS, NOT THE CLASS. A BURN CARRYING
418:> ### `done_reason: "stop"` AND A **NULL** `eval_count` WAS MEASURED 2026-09-06 ON glm. ***
419:>
420:> 130,056 bytes, `response` EMPTY, `thinking` 124,789 characters -- and `done_reason` is the
421:> value a HEALTHY seat returns. So the two fields the paragraph above offers as the signature
422:> both read normal, and only the empty `response` is diagnostic.
423:>
424:> **THE TOOL WAS ALREADY RIGHT AND ONLY THIS FILE WAS WRONG**, which is why nothing was built:
425:> `seat_verdicts.py` does not test `done_reason == "length"`. It tests for an empty `response`
426:> FIRST and uses `done_reason` only to decide what to do next -- on `"length"` the answer is
427:> gone, on `"stop"` the model FINISHED and the answer may be sitting in `thinking` behind a
428:> channel marker, so it looks. It looked here, found no marker, and reported SEAT DOWN rather
429:> than recovering an answer that did not exist. *A checker that had been written from this
430:> paragraph's signature would have scored a 130 KB burn as a healthy seat.*
431:>
432:> **THE RULE: an empty `response` is the diagnostic; `done_reason` decides the DISPOSITION, not
433:> the detection.** `"length"` = the budget ran out and the answer is lost; `"stop"` = the model
434:> finished, so read `thinking` for a channel-marked block before declaring the seat down.
435:>
436:> **AND READ THE THINKING EVEN WHEN THERE IS NO MARKER.** glm's burn carried a real challenge to
437:> the round's packet -- an unevidenced universal claim about the CPU model -- which was checked
438:> in one grep and turned out true-but-unsupported. *An answer that was written and never read is
439:> an UNREAD REVIEW, and this project has three times found real defects in one.* The burn costs
440:> the VERDICT, not necessarily the finding.
441:>
442:> *This file's own exemplar rule, landing on this file again: a rule minted from ONE instance
443:> carries that instance's shape as if it were the class. The instruction survived contact; the
444:> signature did not.*
445:
446:
447:It is none of the other four classes: no quota text (not a WALL), nothing echoed (not the
448:echo-only shape), the daemon served it for minutes (not a REFUSED CONNECTION), and the other
449:cloud seats answer normally in the same firing (not the shared-transport outage).
450:
451:*** AND IT DEFEATS EVERY SIZE-BASED CHECK: the burn is one of the LARGEST files in the panel. ***
452:
453:**THE CORRELATION, measured across four firings in one day:**
454:
455:    090604    4,032 b   normal      simple brief
456:    190055    2,845 b   normal      simple brief
457:    222557  142,525 b   BURNED      three-claim band-arithmetic audit
458:    230207  222,981 b   BURNED      five questions across two rounds
459:
460:**Same seat, same transport, same day — the burns track BRIEF COMPLEXITY.** That is the
461:carrier's oldest packet-fed lesson recurring: their behaviour is governed by the brief, and this
462:project once spent DAYS misdiagnosing a brief defect as a seat outage.
463:
464:**WHAT IS LOST IS NOT NOISE.** The second burn ends mid-sentence — *"So verdict likely NOT
465:RATIFY due to #467. But maybe the question is from a panel"* — **it had reached a verdict and was
466:second-guessing itself when the budget ran out.** On that same panel the other dissenting seat
467:found a live defect in code shipped an hour earlier, so a lost dissent is real cost.
468:
469:> **DETECTION IS SOLVED — `seat_verdicts.py` keys on `done_reason`, not size, and named both
470:> burns correctly. THE MITIGATION IS THE BRIEF: keep a packet-fed brief to ONE question with its
471:> facts inlined.** Splitting a complex audit across two firings costs one extra invocation and
472:> preserves the answer.
473:
474:The kimi CLI returned `provider.auth_error: 403 You've reached your usage limit for this billing
475:cycle`. That is a genuine wall, correctly detected, and it was recorded as a down seat.
476:
477:**kimi was never down.** `curl -s localhost:11434/api/tags` lists **`kimi-k3:cloud`** alongside
478:the three named Ollama seats, and three earlier panel dirs each hold a healthy `ollama_kimi.txt`
479:(17,684 / 17,382 / 9,227 bytes) — so the substitute was being fired BY HAND and then quietly
480:stopped being. `panel.sh` launched exactly one kimi seat, the CLI, with its Ollama seats hardcoded
481:to glm / deepseek / minimax.
482:
483:**FIXED IN THE SAME COMMIT THAT SHIPPED THIS PARAGRAPH (`faffad7`), AND THE PARAGRAPH DID NOT SAY
484:SO — which made it a wrong record from the moment it landed.** `panel.sh` now fires `OLLAMA4 =
485:kimi-k3:cloud` as a fourth Ollama seat, and its seat check reports a walled CLI with a live
486:substitute as ONE SEAT COVERED rather than one lost. It has answered in every panel since.
487:`check_seats_read.py` and `seat_verdicts.py` both learned the mapping in the same commit, so a
488:substitute answer is no longer invisible to section G. *A present-tense description of a tool,
489:written in the commit that changes the tool, is stale before anyone reads it.*
490:
491:> **WHEN A SEAT WALLS, ASK WHETHER THE MODEL IS REACHABLE BY ANOTHER TRANSPORT BEFORE RECORDING
492:> IT DOWN.** The wall rule says wait it out; the daemon rule says one command fixes it. This
493:> class says **neither applies — the seat is answerable right now, on a different road.**
494:
495:**Why it is worse than an undercount, which is how the ledger row first described it.** A
496:mis-tallied seat is a reporting defect a reader can correct. Here the roster records no failure
497:to investigate at all: every panel since the CLI wall ran one seat short while a working
498:transport sat idle. *That is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape with an extra step,
499:because the missing seat never even appears as missing.*
500:
501:**The cheap check is the same one the daemon rule already mandates**, read for a different fact:
502:`api/tags` answers "is the transport up?" AND "which models can I reach?" — it was being used for
503:only the first. A walled CLI seat costs one `grep` against that list to recover.
504:
505:*Recovered the same minute by hand onto the live panel dir. The `panel.sh` fix was deliberately
506:NOT applied while a panel was running: bash reads a script incrementally, so editing a running
507:script is its own defect class.*
508:
509:
510:**A SHORT ANSWER AND A DEAD SEAT ARE NOT THE SAME THING.** Grok's file was 119 bytes when first
511:sampled — opening line only, "I'll read the full offloaded packet first" — and looked exactly
512:like the minimax stall this file spent days mis-attributing. It was MID-ANSWER. Sample twice
513:before calling a seat dead; the size check cannot distinguish slow from dead.
514:
515:**SEAT RANK DOES NOT SETTLE FACT — measured twice today, in both directions.** The flagship seat
516:corrected a false "strict superset" claim about header closures and its correction was ALSO
517:false; `gcc -E -H` settled it (two exceptions, not the one either of us named). And a reading
518:seat's claim that production `fatal()` exits — which would have made a shipped correction into
519:guest-reachable process death — was refuted by opening `src/core/debugmsg.c:384`. **Both were
520:settled in under two minutes by looking.**
521:
522:*** AND THE REFUTATION'S REASON WAS ITSELF FALSE, corrected 2026-08-21 after TWO seats
523:challenged it independently and a direct reading settled it. *** This paragraph said `fatal()`
524:is "byte-for-byte `debug()`". **It is not.** `debug()` (`:365-381`) computes
525:`ss = single_step || about_to_enter_single_step`, decrements `v` when `emul_executing`,
526:increments it when stepping, and then **`if ((quiet_mode && !ss) || v < 0) return;` at `:375`**.
527:`fatal()` (`:384-390`) has **no early-out at all** — it goes straight to `va_debug`. The
528:conclusion held (neither exits), but the reason given was wrong, and a wrong reason gets reused:
529:it was cited a second time before anyone opened the file.
530:
531:**Two live consequences, both measured:** `fatal()` cannot be silenced by `-q`, which is why a
532:guest-drivable `fatal()` needs a latch and not a verbosity flag; and **a probe CAN distinguish
533:the two, contrary to the older rule 6** — free-running gives `v < 0` so `debug()` returns while
534:`fatal()` prints, whereas single-stepping raises `debug()` above the threshold and both print.
535:**That distinction is conditional on `verbose == 0`**: run with `-v` (or `-i`/`-r`, which imply
536:it) and free-running gives `v == 0`, `debug()` prints, and any row resting on the difference
537:goes quiet without saying so.
538:
