553:*** ALL SEVEN SCRIPTABLE SEATS ANSWERED THE #420 BRIEF — the first time that has happened.
554:The variable was the BRIEF, not the roster. *** A brief that inlines every fact and says
555:plainly "do not plan to read files" got 12 KB out of minimax and 5.6 KB out of deepseek, both
556:of which had been recorded as broken. **Two of the nine "dead" seats were alive the whole
557:time.** Before pruning any seat, re-run it against an inlined brief — a stall from a
558:file-reading instruction is indistinguishable from a quota wall in the output, and this
559:project mis-attributed it for days.
560:
561:**THE CLOUD SEATS CANNOT READ FILES, AND THE BRIEF DECIDES WHETHER THEY ANSWER — this is a
562:PROMPTING defect, not a seat outage, and it was misdiagnosed as one for days.** On the #419 pass 1
563:**deepseek stalled at 136 bytes with minimax's exact signature**, having answered 7 KB the round
564:before. The difference was not the seat: #418's brief INLINED its measurements, #419's told the
565:seats to read the source. glm survived only by answering from the prompt alone and said so —
566:*"no files read; no boots executed; every line citation in this review is the prompt's, re-stated,
567:not independently verified."* That is the honest form and the right way to count it: a READING.
568:**RULE: inline every fact a cloud seat needs. A brief containing "read the source" guarantees a
569:stall from deepseek and minimax, and it will look exactly like a quota failure.**
570:| **Kimi 3** | *** BACK — USED AND ANSWERING, 2026-08-14 ~21:0x *** | Quota-dead since ~08-12 (403, 328-byte error file). On the #121 pass 1 it returned **128 KB** ending in a real verdict, and **it RAN ITS OWN PROBE** (`/tmp/t121`, `_scratchpad/t121_probe.c`) while re-checking `git status --porcelain` empty afterwards — so it is a MEASURING seat, not only a reading one. **It beat the main loop twice in that single pass:** it found the self-invalidating occurrence count at a SECOND site (`CHANGELOG.md:4580`) that a `src/`-only grep missed, and it caught that `prefix_d` is absent from the `prefix_i+prefix_f+prefix_s > 1` guard at `diskimage.c:1763`, so `ds:` is accepted and a naively-placed `d:` assignment would silently override an explicit `s:`. **A 128 KB substantive answer IS the health test.** Wire it back into panels AND into the #124 adjudication. |
571:| **Fable 5** (Agent, `model: "fable"`) | *** ALIVE — REVIVED BY THE 2026-08-15 /login, NOT BY THE CALENDAR *** | Six consecutive quota failures (the sixth deliberately after the date rollover, proving the reset is not at local midnight) — then the owner's `/login` at ~10:4x restored it. The post-login ECHO-TEST returned **both nonces** (prompt token + verbatim file quote from CHECKPOINT.md), so prompt delivery AND file access are proven, not assumed. **Both "dead" Anthropic-side states this project has seen were revived by ACCOUNT-STATE CHANGES (Kimi: billing cycle; Fable: login), never by a date.** The owner also set the session model to Fable 5 (max) — the flagship hat and the main loop are one model again. Its first act back: the FABLE half of the FABLE/KIMI adjudication, reviewing Kimi's docket verdict item by item. Health-tested **five times** across 2026-08-13/14, including on the day its reset was expected and again at ~22:2x on 08-14. DO NOT ASSUME A CALENDAR DATE RESTORES A SEAT — TEST IT; the test is one Agent call told to open with a liveness token (`FABLE_SEAT_ALIVE_<date>`), which costs one call and cleanly separates "dead" from "assumed dead". Kimi revived with no calendar signal after five failures, so keep testing this one too — but ONCE PER SESSION, not per round. |
572:
573:**Nine seats by design. `panel.sh` fires EIGHT scriptable transports as of 2026-08-25 (the
574:eighth is `kimi-k3:cloud`, the kimi SUBSTITUTE -- see the sixth seat-failure class). The
575:count below is the 2026-08-14 snapshot: SEVEN answering, `panel.sh` firing seven scriptable
576:seats, only ONE of which (minimax) now produces nothing.** Kimi came back with no warning and no
577:calendar signal — which is the general lesson: **a dead seat is dead until a real invocation says
578:otherwise, and it may revive the same way. Fire the full roster and let the seat-check sort it;
579:do not prune a seat out of the script on the strength of a previous failure.** The only seat still
580:down is Fable, and the ADJUDICATION (see below) is no longer blocked, because Kimi can hold it alone.
581:
582:### *** A FABLE/KIMI PASS IS THE ADJUDICATION FOR FIXES (standing user directive, 2026-08-14) ***
583:
584:When Fable and Kimi come back, their pass is **not another review** — it is the **adjudication of
585:the fix list**. Give them the candidate fixes and residuals a round has produced and ask which are
586:worth making, in what order, and which should be filed or dropped. That is a different question
587:from "is this diff correct", which the reading and measuring seats already answer.
588:
589:Until they return the adjudication degrades to the main loop, which is the documented fallback and
590:has worked — but the rule that made it work is unchanged and applies to whoever holds the hat:
591:**anything checkable in under about two minutes gets CHECKED, not judged.** Every useful drop on
592:2026-08-13 was settled by a grep, a run, or a five-rig spelling check.
593:
594:*** BATCHING THE FLAGSHIP SEAT IS RIGHT FOR CLOSURES AND WRONG FOR RULE QUESTIONS —
595:measured 2026-08-22 on a 46-row batch, by the seat that was being batched. ***
596:
597:Its finding, and the evidence is inside the batch: **the closures lost nothing by waiting.**
598:Every row was gate-green with its record written, and five days of delay produced exactly one
599:hold text overtaken by events (`sgiarcbiosoob` still said "not committed yet" after its oracle
600:landed) and one state-vs-substance mismatch (`es438` marked "dropped" on shipped, witnessed,
601:reviewed work — *a future reader who trusts states reads that as "not done", which is false*).
602:
603:**But the RULE questions did not wait safely.** #451 had to answer its own accept-row question
604:locally, by building both mutant forms, because the seat that adjudicates that rule was
605:batched; and the failability rule was minted, corrected and re-corrected across #448/#449 with
606:the flagship signature arriving days later. Nothing broke — the measuring seats covered it —
607:but *that is the panel silently absorbing a missing seat's function*, which is the thing the
608:seat ledger exists to refuse to count as agreement.
609:
610:> **SPLIT THE QUEUE BY QUESTION TYPE.** CLOSE / KEEP-OPEN sign-offs on shipped work batch
611:> freely; weekly is fine, and a 46-row batch proved it. Rows raising a **RULE QUESTION** get
612:> the seat at the next round boundary, cap about a day — they change how future rounds run, so
613:> every day they wait is rounds executed under an unadjudicated rule. The cost is small: rule
614:> questions were 6 of 46, and they are the short ones.
615:
616:> **A STAGE HELD *FOR* A SEAT MUST CARRY THE QUESTION IT IS HELD FOR.** Three of the four
617:> meta-rows in that batch carried identical boilerplate with no question in them, and the
618:> question had to be reconstructed from the row's subject. The two rows that named their
619:> question explicitly were the easiest to answer *accurately*. One habit, and it makes the
620:> batch both faster and safer.
621:>
622:> ### *** THAT HABIT WAS AT **ZERO** ON 2026-08-26, WHICH IS WHY THE RULE IS NO LONGER PROSE.
623:> ### It is now `check_stage_panels.py` + a `hold_question` field. Owner-decided the same day. ***
624:>
625:> **THE NUMERATOR IS THE CLAIM AND IT IS ZERO. THE DENOMINATOR IS DELIBERATELY NOT A NUMBER
626:> HERE, because writing one is what went wrong.** This clause read "0 FOR 18" and named no
627:> UNIT -- and when the exemplar audit challenged it, FOUR counting methods gave FOUR answers:
628:> the audit seat 21 stages / 18 rows; a bare entry-grep of mine 30 / 26 (it counted ENTRIES,
629:> so two markers in one stage counted twice); the checker's phase rules 26 / 22; and its full
630:> rule set, excluding closed rows, 24 / 21. **I could not reproduce the seat's figure, so I
631:> am not writing any of them down** -- that is precisely how the original number got there.
632:>
633:> To re-derive it at any commit: `git show <rev>:tools/pipeline/ledger.json` and count held
634:> markers **one per (row, phase), recognised phases only, excluding closed rows** -- which is
635:> what `check_stage_panels.py` does. **STAGES AND ROWS ARE DIFFERENT UNITS and a count that
636:> names neither cannot be checked.**
637:>
638:> Every held stage then in the ledger carried a DISPOSITION note where a
639:> question belongs -- *"FILE - no measurable effect until a caller survives"*, *"a policy, not a
640:> round"*, *"FILE - offer as a rider in b120u's first panel"*. Each is a true statement about
641:> the row. **None is a question a seat can answer.**
642:>
643:> **This is the carrier diagnosing itself, exactly as the structure-first rule at the top of
644:> this file predicts.** The rule was written down, it was minted from a measured incident, it
645:> sat in an auto-loaded file -- and the very next population of held rows scored zero. *Knowing
646:> a rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems, and only the second is
647:> solved by writing more.*
648:>
649:> **Why it is worse than untidy:** a seat fired against a disposition note has to GUESS what it
650:> is being asked, and a guessed question yields an answer to the wrong question **that still
651:> looks like a discharged stage**. That is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape moved
652:> one step earlier -- earlier, and therefore harder to see, because the stage shows a real
653:> answer from a real seat.
654:>
655:> **The check is dated-cutoff, the same shape `check_bugfile_sync.py` uses**: stages whose
656:> newest entry postdates `HOLDQ_CUTOFF` FAIL; the eighteen older ones are reported every run
657:> and not failed. That is deliberate and it is not timidity -- a rule that retro-fails
658:> eighteen rows blocks the next commit on reconstruction nobody scheduled, and **a rule that
659:> does that gets switched off**, which is worse than a rule that warns. Negative-controlled in
660:> both directions on the day it landed: injecting a post-cutoff hold with no question turns it
661:> red and names the row; adding the question turns it green again.
662:
663:*** SUPERSEDED 2026-08-16 BY OWNER DIRECTIVE: FULL PANEL ON EVERY STAGE. ***
664:
665:**THE RULE NOW: every stage — assess, research AND review — runs all nine seats before the round
666:moves on. If a seat cannot be run, STOP AND ASK THE OWNER.** Do not degrade quietly; that is
667:exactly what this replaces. `regress` remains FABLE-ONLY (separate standing directive), and stages
668:that ran before 2026-08-16 are grandfathered forward-only and carry an explicit annotation entry so
669:their blanks do not read as seat failures.
670:
671:*** EVERY LEDGER ENTRY WRITTEN FROM A PANEL MUST NAME ITS PANEL DIR. One field, and it
672:is what makes section G work at all. Learned 2026-08-24 by breaking it for a whole day. ***
673:
674:Seven panels ran that day, all answering. The ledger recorded two or three seats per stage;
675:about **forty seat-entries were missing**, and one seat's 7.6 KB answer had never been
676:opened at all -- only its verdict line read.
677:
678:**The gates caught ONE of the seven, and the reason is structural.** Section G's scope
679:trigger is an entry that CITES A PANEL DIRECTORY. The ledger cited six `panel_20260823_*`
680:dirs and **zero** `panel_20260824_*` dirs -- so G never looked at any of that day's panels.
681:Section H compounds it: it counts the per-stage seat union ACROSS DATES, so a new panel on
682:a row that already had seats from an earlier one folds in invisibly.
683:
684:> **The defect was the PRACTICE, not the gates.** The day before, every panel got cited and
685:> G enforced the rest mechanically. Nothing in the tooling changed.
686:
687:Each time the citation was added, that panel came under G's scope in one step -- and on the
688:last one G immediately reported *"1 seat answers exist on disk and are recorded NOWHERE"*.
689:Its wording is the rule worth keeping: **a seat that answered and was not recorded is NOT a
690:seat failure and NOT agreement -- it is an UNREAD REVIEW**, and this project has twice found
691:real defects sitting in one.
692:
693:**And a counting trap that costs exactly two seats every time:** the two Agent seats leave
694:NO FILE in the panel dir. Any check that counts files under-counts by two, and they are
695:precisely the two most likely to have found something.
696:
697:Mechanically enforced by `GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_stage_panels.py`, wired into `precommit_check.sh` as a
698:hard section H. Note it complements rather than duplicates section G: **G verifies that a seat which
699:ANSWERED was recorded; H verifies the seat was FIRED at all.** Neither catches the other's failure.
700:
701:> *** H's GUARANTEE IS NARROWER THAN "FIRED AT ALL", and the overclaim was measured
702:> 2026-08-26. *** H reads LEDGER ENTRIES, not process execution. It **skips stages that
703:> never started**, treats a one-seat stage as a filing rather than a short panel, and
704:> accepts a seat entry without any proof a process ran. The honest statement is: **a
705:> STARTED, non-filing stage must carry nine recorded seats.** A stage that was never begun,
706:> and a seat entry written without firing anything, both pass. The policy is unchanged and
707:> right; the mechanical guarantee is smaller than the sentence claimed.
708:
709:*The superseded rule, kept because the reasoning is the part that expired, not the caution:* it said
710:ONE full panel per ROUND with pass 2 restricted to Codex + Opus, because the Ollama seats 429 if two
711:panels fire within ~20 minutes and Grok's free tier died after ~4 substantial invocations. **Grok is
712:paid now and Kimi is back**; all seven scriptable seats answered the last three panels, so the only
713:live constraint is the ~20-minute Ollama spacing — which real stages already exceed. A policy
714:written around two dead seats outlived both of them by a week.
715:
716:## The multi-LLM panel — EVERY task, research and review (standing user directive)
717:
718:Every code round runs the panel **twice**: pass 1 on the design brief BEFORE any edit,
719:pass 2 on the actual diff AFTER.
720:
721:> ### *** `git show <sha> -- <file> | head -N` INLINES THE COMMIT MESSAGE, NOT THE DIFF.
722:> ### Three panels were blinded by this in one session before the cause was found. ***
723:>
724:> `git show` prints the FULL COMMIT MESSAGE first.  A 50-line message and `head -60` yields
725:> **zero diff lines** -- measured: `git show 938b328 -- src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c | head -60`
726:> gives 0 hunks, while the whole diff has 20.  An earlier brief in the same session got 34
727:> hunks from the identical command with `sed -n '1,200p'` instead, which cleared the message.
728:> **Same command, different cut, opposite outcome** -- which is why it read as intermittent
729:> rather than as a bug.
730:>
731:> **USE `git show --format= <sha> -- <file>`** (suppresses the message entirely) or
732:> `git diff <sha>^ <sha> -- <file>`.  Never a line-count cut on `git show`, because the cut
733:> has to be larger than a message whose length you do not control -- and this project writes
734:> long messages by policy.
735:>
736:> *** THE WORSE HALF, AND IT IS A DISCIPLINE FAILURE NOT A TOOL ONE: THE CHECK FIRED AND I
737:> IGNORED IT. ***  The brief-building step printed `diff hunks inlined: 0` in its own output,
738:> immediately above the line that launched six seats.  I read past it.  Two seats then said
739:> plainly they could not settle the questions from the packet -- and found the defect anyway.
740:>
741:> **A count printed beside an action is not a check unless something refuses to proceed on
742:> it.**  The denominator rule already says a number makes a search checkable; this adds that
743:> a checkable number nobody gates on is decoration.  When a brief claims to inline a diff,
744:> the launcher should REFUSE at zero hunks rather than report it.
745:
746:> ### *** AND A REPAIR MADE IN RESPONSE TO A PASS 2 OWES ITS OWN PASS 2.  Adjudicated
747:> ### 2026-08-31 with a named stop condition, and SETTLED EMPIRICALLY IN THE SAME HOUR. ***
748:>
749:> The question is real, because this project's `#392` chain ran SIX follow-up passes without
750:> converging and the stopping rule exists to end exactly that.  Two seats ruled opposite ways:
751:>
752:> * **NO** -- *the pass 2 that produced the repair IS the pass 2 on the repair*, stopping
753:>   unless the repair changes product code or adds a detector axis the pass did not cover.
754:> * **YES** -- **the obligation follows the MATERIAL, not the narrative.**  An exemption keyed
755:>   to provenance (*it is only a repair*) is the same scope-rot that let a PRETABLE block go
756:>   stale by binding to vintage rather than to what shipped.
757:>
758:> **THE STOP CONDITION THAT MAKES *YES* TERMINATE, and it is the whole answer:** one pass per
759:> floor-bound DIFF, scoped to that diff; **the chain closes on the first pass that finds zero
760:> floor breaches** -- no clean-sweep mutant, no vacuous row, no non-varying channel.  Pass N+1
761:> targets only `diff(repair N)`, so the chain is infinite only if EVERY repair is defective --
762:> in which case the alternative ships every one of those defects.
763:>
764:> **`#392` is not evidence against passing repairs; it is evidence the repairs kept being
765:> bad.**  A *first clean pass closes* rule would have ended it at the first good fix.
766:>
767:> *** THE MEASURED CASE, and it is why the NO ruling is rejected rather than merely
768:> outvoted: `#499`'s L2 repair WAS DEFECTIVE, and the pass the NO rule would have skipped is
769:> the one that found it. ***  The repair closed a width-axis gap at the address the mutants
770:> used and left the identical gap at the other component -- three cells of a two-by-two.  A
771:> guard re-added at the catch-all would have been caught; one re-added and CONDITIONED on the
772:> first component would not.  **The original breach, reproduced inside its own repair, by
773:> someone who had just spent an hour understanding it.**
774:>
775:> *The gate already enforced this -- it refused the next commit with NO PASS-2 RECEIPT on the
776:> repair.  What was missing was the warrant and the stop condition, so a future round meeting
777:> that red would know whether to satisfy it or argue with it.* Research/triage tasks get at least one pass. Launch all
778:seats **concurrently** (independent processes / one Agent batch), then collect.
779:
780:| seat | invocation |
781:|---|---|
782:| Codex GPT-6 Astra (xhigh) -- *** MIGRATED FROM 5.6-SOL BY OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-09-04 ("GPT 6 Astra came out; use extra high effort"), VERIFIED LIVE BEFORE THE EDIT: `-m gpt-6-astra -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh"` returned the liveness token, named itself `GPT-6`, and quoted the first line of CHECKPOINT.md verbatim.  THE MODEL WAS REACHABLE ONLY AFTER A CLI UPGRADE THAT THE CLI COULD NOT DO ITSELF: 0.148.0 answered `The 'gpt-6-astra' model requires a newer version of Codex`, `codex update` failed twice with `Downloaded Codex package archive did not contain the expected package layout` (its staging dir was EMPTY -- the extraction, not the archive: the 0.153.4 tar.gz fetched from GitHub by hand had exactly the 0.148.0 layout), so the archive was unpacked into `~/.codex/packages/standalone/releases/0.153.4-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc/` and `current` re-pointed by junction, with 0.148.0 kept as `current.0.148.0-bak` for a one-command rollback.  `codex --version` now says 0.153.4.  EFFORT RAISED TO `max` BY OWNER DIRECTIVE THE SAME EVENING: accepted by the CLI (header `reasoning effort: max`, token returned) and PROVEN VALIDATED by the grok discriminator -- a bogus value errors with `Supported values are: 'none', 'minimal', 'low', 'med...`, so acceptance proves the tier; `panel.sh` carries `CODEX_EFFORT="max"`.  Re-check the model name after any further CLI upgrade -- a wrong name is a SEAT FAILURE. *** | `codex exec --sandbox read-only --skip-git-repo-check -m gpt-6-astra -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh" "$(cat brief)" < /dev/null` (the 5.6-SOL form until 2026-09-04) — the `< /dev/null` is required. Windows argv caps ~32 KB. *** `panel.sh` NOW HANDLES THIS ITSELF, as of 2026-08-30 -- do NOT hand-trim a brief for it. *** Above `ARGV_CAP` (30000) codex and kimi are fed a POINTER (`PTR_PROMPT`: read `$BRIEF_ABS` and review it as its own Questions section directs) instead of the contents; below it the inline form is byte-unchanged. Both are FILE-READING seats, so this costs nothing -- and grok (`--prompt-file`) and agy (`--add-dir`) were already immune, leaving these two as the only argv-fed seats. **The block previously WARNED and then launched anyway**, which cost both seats on a 38 KB brief and is the check-that-cannot-fail family's sibling: *a message reporting an ACTION the code does not take*, exactly like the re-firing message. `PANEL_ARGV_CAP` overrides the cap **only so the branch is testable in both directions** without keeping a 38 KB fixture; it is not a tuning knob. Controlled by extracting the real decision block out of `panel.sh` and `eval`ing it -- 6/6 rc=0 fixed, EXTRACT FAILED rc=9 pre-fix. **A HAND-WRITTEN BRIEF STILL NEEDS TO BE READABLE**: the pointer form only helps seats that can open the file, so it is not licence to grow briefs without limit -- the packet-fed Ollama seats still get the whole thing POSTed and still burn budget on it. Never the trailing-`-` stdin form (died silently once). |
783:| agy 3.7 (high) | `agy --dangerously-skip-permissions --add-dir <dir> --print-timeout 30m --model gemini-3.7-flash-high --effort high --print "<short prompt pointing at brief file>"` — reads files via `--add-dir`; no stdin prompt mode. **Model set by user directive 2026-08-13.** PASS BOTH `--model` and `--effort`: the effort tier is baked into the model name (`-high`/`-medium`/`-low`) AND there is a separate `--effort` flag, so naming only one leaves the other at its default. Before this, `panel.sh` passed NEITHER while printing "agy(high)" — the banner was an overclaim. **`agy models` is the authority on valid names** (it lists gemini-3.7/3.6/3.5-flash and 3.1-pro tiers, plus claude-sonnet-4-6 and claude-opus-4-6-thinking); a wrong name is a SEAT FAILURE, so re-check after any CLI upgrade.  *** AND IT HAS A QUOTA.  MEASURED 2026-08-30, THE FIRST agy WALL ON RECORD, AND IT ARRIVED WHILE THE SEAT WAS BEING DELIBERATELY PUSHED. ***  `Error: Individual quota reached. Please upgrade your subscription to increase your limits. Resets in 1h56m37s.` -- **111 bytes, on stderr, with the process exiting immediately**, so the `.out` file is ZERO BYTES and nothing is written to the panel dir but that one line.  **THE ROSTER ASSUMPTION IT OVERTURNS:** the owner's own read was *"seems like there is limitless agy use"*, and this project had never seen it wall, so it was being farmed 3 jobs at a time.  Six substantial invocations inside about forty minutes reached the limit.  Treat it like Grok's paid cap: **not queryable, not unlimited, and a raised cap has the same failure mode further out.**  *** THE DETECTION IS EASY AND THE MISREADING IS NOT. ***  It states its own reset time, which no other seat here does -- so a walled agy tells you exactly when to re-fire, and the re-fire-a-walled-seat-once rule becomes a scheduled thing rather than a guess.  But **the zero-byte `.out` is the trap**: this file's own rule says a zero-byte agy log is the NORMAL mid-run state because agy does not stream.  Zero bytes plus a live process is mid-run; zero bytes plus 111 bytes on stderr is a WALL.  **Check the `.err`, not the size** -- `panel.sh` writes one per seat and this is what it is for.  *Recorded because a seat that walls with the machine otherwise healthy is the state this project most often misfiles: three jobs failed identically and instantly, which reads like a launcher bug and is not.* |
784:*** OWNER RULING 2026-08-30: **agy IS RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT AND HELD OUT OF ROUTINE
785:PANELS.** ***  Asked after its first quota wall proved the cap is real (~6 substantial calls
786:per window), and the question was forced rather than tidy: it cannot be a measuring front end
787:AND a panel seat on one budget.
788:
789:**THE WARRANT IS THE ROSTER'S ONE STRUCTURAL WEAKNESS, already measured.**  There are THREE
790:compile-and-measure front ends and TWO of them -- Opus 5 and Fable 5 -- sit behind the SAME
791:Agent-tool transport.  When that transport went down on `#462`, the panel lost **all** of its
792:measuring capacity at once while seven reading seats answered normally, and the round had to
793:be held with a measured kill outstanding.  **agy is the only measuring seat on an independent
794:road**, so spending its budget on reading is spending the roster's insurance on a job seven
795:other seats already do.
796:
797:**WHAT THIS COSTS, stated rather than glossed: routine panels now fire EIGHT seats, and every
798:one of them records agy as DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.**  That is a different thing from a seat
799:failure and from agreement, and conflating the two is exactly what the seat ledger exists to
800:refuse -- so it is written by name in the round record, every time.
801:
802:*The cost is real and today shows why it is worth paying: agy's READ-ONLY audits settled the
803:alignment premise path-by-path and found the debugger's `put d` exception that a clean
804:invariant claim would have missed.  Losing that from panels is not free.  But a reading is
805:recoverable from another seat, and a MEASUREMENT with both Anthropic seats down is not.*
806:
807:**IT IS STILL FIRED FOR READING WHEN NO MEASUREMENT IS IN FLIGHT** -- the reservation is about
808:not spending the budget where it is fungible, not about idling the seat.  And note the two
809:roles genuinely conflict in practice, not just on paper: for most of 2026-08-30 agy was given
810:reading precisely BECAUSE a concurrent build would have corrupted the measuring seat's
811:wall-clock oracles.
812:
813:| Kimi 3 | *** THE CLI IS THE SEAT AGAIN AS OF 2026-08-29, BY OWNER DIRECTIVE, AND THE WALL CLEARED BY TESTING RATHER THAN BY WAITING. ***  Its 403 was a **WEEKLY (7-day)** limit, not a billing cycle; a real invocation returned the liveness token, so the seat is **FILE-READING** again.  *** THAT IS A CAPABILITY CHANGE, NOT A TIDY-UP *** -- the `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute is PACKET-FED, so for the whole time it stood in, "the kimi seat" could not read the repo, and any answer credited to it as a REPO FACT was the conflation the doctrine forbids.  `panel.sh`'s `OLLAMA4` is now blank, and the launch, banner, seat check, verdict roster AND coverage note are all conditional on it -- **blanking the variable alone would have left `panel_ollama.py` running with an empty model name, writing an error file, and producing a PHANTOM seat that answers the seat check.**  Restoring the substitute is ONE LINE: `OLLAMA4="kimi-k3:cloud"`.  The two-transport history is kept below because it IS the recovery path. |
814:| *(kimi, historical)* | **TWO TRANSPORTS, AND THE SEAT WAS LIVE ON THE SECOND.** CLI: `kimi -p "$(cat brief)" -m kimi-code/k3` (rejects `--auto`/`--yolo` with `-p`) — **403-walled on its billing cycle; its ~348-byte error file is NOT a seat answer.** SUBSTITUTE: `kimi-k3:cloud`, fired automatically by `panel.sh` as `OLLAMA4` since `faffad7`, answering in every panel since. **Record it by transport (`kimi-k3:cloud`), never as "the kimi CLI seat".** *This row said QUOTA-DEAD / "do not wire it into panels" for days after the substitute was working — a FALSE ROSTER in the table a fresh session fires from. Fixed 2026-08-26.* |
815:| Grok 4.6 (xhigh) | `grok --prompt-file <brief> -m grok-4.6 --effort xhigh --permission-mode plan --cwd <proj> < /dev/null` — **xhigh set 2026-08-13 by user directive and VERIFIED to be a real tier, not assumed: passing a bogus value makes the CLI name its own list, `use one of: xhigh, high, medium, low`, which proves the flag is validated AND that xhigh is the top of it.** That discriminator is the general technique — if a bad value were silently ignored, acceptance would prove nothing and the seat would quietly run at the default, which is precisely the trap the agy seat sat in. **A bad effort value EXITS 0 while printing its error**, so exit status cannot catch it; `panel.sh`'s seat check greps for "unknown effort level"/"unknown model" alongside the quota patterns. — binary at `~/.grok/bin/grok.exe`, **added 2026-08-13 by user directive.** *** `--prompt-file` TAKES THE BRIEF FROM A FILE, so the ~32 KB Windows argv cap DOES NOT APPLY — this is the only CLI seat immune to the failure that killed Codex on a 36 KB brief today, so it is the seat to trust with a large inlined diff. *** `--permission-mode plan` keeps it READ-ONLY (verified: it read a repo file and answered correctly, without prompting or hanging) and `--cwd` lets it consult source like Codex and agy do. `grok models` is the authority on IDs. **MEASURED 2026-08-26 by running it: it lists TWO** -- `grok-4.6` (default) and `grok-4.5`. *** THIS CELL CARRIED BOTH "today it lists exactly one" AND "now lists TWO" AT ONCE, two present-tense claims that cannot both be true, until the exemplar audit found them. Settled by running the command rather than by choosing between them. *** **AND THE SAME RUN REFUTED THE TIER-CHECK CLAIM, which mattered more than the count.** This said the model list is "itself a cheap tier check" for whether the subscription is active. It is not: `grok models` printed **`You are not authenticated.`** and STILL listed both models -- so the list is served without auth and proves nothing about tier. **The seat was verified live in the same minute by a real invocation** (`--prompt-file`, xhigh, plan mode: it answered, read `CHECKPOINT.md`, and exited 0). **So the ONLY trustworthy health test is firing the seat.** That is the rule this file already states for walled seats -- *do not reason about whether a seat is back, test it* -- and the tier-check shortcut was a way of dodging it. The paid cap is still not queryable (no `usage`/`quota` subcommand; `du` is disk usage), so DO NOT ASSUME IT IS UNLIMITED — a raised cap has the same failure mode further out. `panel.sh`'s seat check scans any non-answering seat for quota/rate/auth text generically, so a wall is reported as an OPERATIONAL failure rather than counted as agreement. **FREE-TIER HISTORY, kept because it calibrates expectations: the free tier died after ~4 substantial invocations, mid-way through its first real review — and even so it found a genuine defect in that partial output (three surviving "un-fakeable" overclaims that two other seats had missed).** |
816:| Ollama cloud ×3 | POST `http://localhost:11434/api/generate` from **git-bash** python (WSL cannot reach the host API); write responses to utf-8 files, never `print()` (cp1252 crash). Three seats, verified live 2026-08-10: **`glm-5.3:cloud`** — *** MIGRATED TWICE BY OWNER DIRECTIVE: glm-5.2 -> glm-5.3-flash on 2026-08-28, then glm-5.3-flash -> **glm-5.3** on 2026-08-29. BOTH VERIFIED BEFORE THE EDIT, which is the discipline that matters here: `ollama pull glm-5.3:cloud` succeeded and an `api/generate` call returned the liveness token EXACTLY (`done_reason=stop`, eval_count 52, no thinking-burn). *** Verified BEFORE the edit rather than after: `ollama pull glm-5.3-flash:cloud` succeeded while the near-miss `glm-5.3flash:cloud` FAILED with `file does not exist`, and an `api/generate` call returned its liveness nonce exactly (`done_reason=stop`, 25 bytes, no thinking-burn). **The tag lives in TWO files and both were changed together** — `panel.sh`'s `OLLAMA1` and `adjudicate.sh`'s two loops, where the second loop must carry the `${m%%:*}` short form or the seat check hunts a filename the run never writes and reports a LIVE seat as missing. **The seat itself is keyed by FILENAME (`ollama_glm.txt`) everywhere else**, so `check_seats_read.py` and `seat_verdicts.py` needed no edit — record a seat by its transport, not its tag —, **`deepseek-v4-pro:cloud`** (DeepSeek is back — v3.2 was retired 2026-07-15 but v4-pro is live; the exact tag needs `-pro`), **`minimax-m3:cloud`**. All three are THINKING models — send NO `num_predict` cap or hidden reasoning eats the budget and the response is empty. Recheck: `curl -s localhost:11434/api/tags`. |
817:| Claude (Opus 5) | the Agent tool with `model: "opus"` — it has repo Read/Grep/Glob, and is the seat most likely to COMPILE AND MEASURE (it has overturned readings twice). Use plain bounds-/correctness-engineering framing, never offensive-security wording (refusal/flag risk); keep the adversarial instruction aimed at the engineering claim. There is no opus/fable CLI. |
818:| Claude (Fable 5) | the Agent tool with `model: "fable"`. **LIVE and answering** — it holds two hats and both are in use: this review seat, and (in the main loop) the ADJUDICATOR of the panel's suggestions. Same benign-framing rules as the Opus seat. Open every invocation with a liveness token plus a verbatim quote from a named file, so prompt delivery AND file access are proven rather than assumed. *This row read QUOTA-DEAD with three dated re-test failures long after the seat came back; the standing lesson survives and is the useful part — **a wall is a moment, not a state, and one Agent call settles it in seconds.** Do not reason about whether a seat is back; test it. Fixed 2026-08-26.* |
819:
820:*** THERE IS A FIFTH `src/` AT THE PROJECT ROOT AND IT IS PRISTINE UPSTREAM. A FILE-READING
821:SEAT POINTED AT THE PROJECT ROOT WILL READ IT AND BE CONFIDENTLY, DETAILEDLY WRONG.
822:Measured 2026-08-22. ***
823:
824:`C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\src\` is dated **April 2021**, is untracked, and is NOT the
825:hardened fork. The four fork trees are `GXEMUL-SEC/`, `est/`, `build/`, `build-asan/` (plus
826:WSL `/tmp/gxsec-build`).
827:
828:> #### *** AND ON 2026-08-31 IT CAUGHT THE MAIN LOOP, NOT A SEAT.  THIS WHOLE SECTION IS
829:> #### WRITTEN AS A WARNING ABOUT SEATS WITH `--add-dir`; THE PERSON IT ACTUALLY BIT WAS ME. ***
830:> 
831:> I "refuted" a flagship seat's claim that `memory_rw.c` pre-zeroes a device read buffer, by
832:> grepping `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` while the cwd had drifted to the PROJECT ROOT.  That resolved
833:> to the pristine 2021 copy -- **530 lines against the fork's 794** -- which genuinely does not
834:> have the `#95` pre-zero.  The fork does, at `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memset(data, 0, len);`
835:> immediately above `paddr -= mem->devices[i].baseaddr;`.  The seat was right, I shipped the
836:> refutation in a commit, and reported it to the owner as a finding.
837:> 
838:> > **THE TELL WAS IN MY OWN OUTPUT AND I DID NOT READ IT.  Every line number I quoted -- 161,
839:> > 222, 425, 437, 486 -- is below 530.  The fork's are 215, 267, 492, 671, 683, 732.  The
840:> > section three paragraphs down already says a seat reading another copy "reports line numbers
841:> > off by hundreds"; that is exactly what I did, to myself.**
842:> 
843:> **The cheap defence is not vigilance, it is the PATH.**  `grep -n ... GXEMUL-SEC/src/...` from
844:> the project root, or an absolute path, cannot resolve to the wrong tree.  A bare `src/...`
845:> depends on a cwd that this file elsewhere records as DRIFTING for whole rounds.
846:
847:> #### *** AND IT IS NOT THE ONLY ONE. `./src/` IS THE ONE THIS PARAGRAPH NAMES; THERE ARE
848:> #### **THREE** PRISTINE-UPSTREAM COPIES UNDER THE PROJECT ROOT. Derived 2026-08-27 by
849:> #### running the command below, not transcribed -- and two of them were undocumented. ***
850:>
851:> **THE DERIVER, because a hand-maintained inventory of files is a wrong record waiting for
852:> someone to add a directory** -- this is the same failure the `_scratchpad/*.pdf` list made
853:> twice:
854:>
855:> ```
856:> find . -name 'dev_wdc.c' | while read f; do echo "$(md5sum "$f" | cut -c1-8) $f"; done
857:> ```
858:>
859:> On that date it printed **four fork copies sharing one hash** and **three upstream copies
860:> sharing another**, the extra two living under `_archive/audit-results-*/builds/`
861:> (`est-asanonly/` and `orig-asan/`). A seat given `--add-dir` at the project root can reach
862:> all of them.
863:>
864:> **THE SAVING GRACE, and it is worth knowing rather than relying on: these are PRISTINE
865:> 2021 upstream, not partially-patched fork copies.** A seat reading one reports line
866:> numbers off by hundreds and arms whose existence differs -- loud, and detectable by the
867:> rule two paragraphs down. *A nearly-right stale FORK copy would be far worse and would
868:> shift lines by tens.* So the hazard is bounded by luck, not by design; do not assume the
869:> next stale copy will be as obvious.
870:>
871:> *Surfaced by the codex seat while answering an unrelated design brief, as a held queue
872:> item (`stalecopies`). Its count was right and its characterisation -- "pre-#337 copies" --
873:> was not; they predate the fork entirely. Re-derived here rather than transcribed.* A brief that says only *"project root: C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL"*
874:lets a seat with `--cwd` there resolve `src/cpus/cpu_sh.c` to the 2021 file.
875:
876:**What that cost:** on the `sh4tlbmulti` pass 1, Kimi returned **80 KB, internally consistent,
877:with a DO-NOT-SHIP verdict and a dramatic finding — that the defect family was far larger than
878:the brief said, with `EXPEVT_RES_INST` itself ending in `exit(1)` and the `SLOT_INST`,
879:`FPU_SLOT_DISABLE` and `ADDR_ERR_LD/ST` arms simply absent.** Every word of it is TRUE OF
880:UPSTREAM and FALSE OF THIS FORK: `RES_INST` was fixed in **#217** and carries a comment saying
881:so, and all four arms exist at `cpu_sh.c:780-796`. Its own line numbers were the tell — it
882:reported the raise site at `dev_sh4.c:700-702` where the fork has it at `:1159`, and it said
883:so out loud, attributing the difference to "maybe GXEMUL-SEC or est".
884:
885:**Two rules follow, and the second is the cheap one:**
886:1. **NAME THE TREE IN THE BRIEF**, not just the project root: *"the tree under review is
887:   `GXEMUL-SEC/src/`; ignore any other `src/` you find."*
888:2. **A SEAT REPORTING LINE NUMBERS THAT DISAGREE WITH THE BRIEF HAS PROBABLY READ ANOTHER
889:   COPY** — check that before crediting or dismissing the finding. Drift from an intervening
890:   commit shifts lines by tens; reading a different tree shifts them by hundreds and changes
891:   which arms exist.
892:
893:**Do not score this as a seat failure.** The seat did exactly what it was asked, found real
894:defects in the file it was given, and flagged its own uncertainty about which tree it had.
895:The defect is in the BRIEF, and it is the same shape as the "inline every fact" rule below:
896:what the cloud seats cannot read, the file-reading seats can read too much of.
897:
898:### *** AND A WRONG FACT IN A BRIEF COMES BACK CONFIRMED. Measured 2026-08-24: six of
899:### seven seats ratified a census that was wrong by 4x, including one that verified every
900:### other claim against the source. ***
901:
902:The `asicbadaddr` pass-1 brief stated that **sixteen** offsets reach a host kill at
903:`(relative_addr / 4) & 3 == 3`. The true number is **sixty-four**, because `relative_addr`
904:is not necessarily word-aligned -- a BYTE access at `+0x0d` gives `0x0d / 4 == 3` and dies
905:exactly like `+0x0c`. The sixteen cited were the word-aligned quarter.
906:
907:*** THE TALLY WAS REPORTED THREE TIMES BEFORE THE PANEL HAD FINISHED ANSWERING, AND ALL
908:THREE WERE WRONG. ***  "One seat of seven", then "two of nine", then "three of eight" --
909:each stated as fact while seats were still returning. **That is the frozen-number class
910:wearing a different hat: a count written before the thing it counts has finished.** The
911:score-claims rule already says a number names the artifact that produced it; the same
912:applies to a tally of seats, and the artifact is *the completed panel*.
913:
914:Final, measured by grepping all nine answers for any engagement with alignment:
915:
916:| seats | what they did |
917:|---|---|
918:| **codex, grok, fable5, opus5** | **CORRECTED it** -- each derived that `relative_addr` need not be word-aligned and the killing set is 64 byte addresses, not 16 |
919:| **agy** | **silently NARROWED the claim** to "Exactly 16 *word-aligned* offsets", inserting a qualifier the brief did not have. It verified every other claim against source, in detail and correctly |
920:| **kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax** | did not engage with alignment at all |
921:
922:*** THE agy CASE IS THE ONE WORTH KEEPING, because it is neither agreement nor
923:correction. ***  A locally-true restatement repairs the sentence and hides the defect: a
924:reader sees a verified claim, and the fact that the true set is four times larger appears
925:nowhere. **That is harder to catch than a plain wrong answer**, because it survives every
926:check except asking what the qualifier was doing there.
927:
928:*** AND ONE OF THE FOUR CORRECTORS PUT THE WRONG NUMBER IN ITS VERDICT LINE. ***  Its
929:verdict ratified "the sixteen word-aligned killing offsets" as verified; eight paragraphs
930:down it derived 64 and called the brief's framing an understatement. **This project's
931:policy is VERDICT LINE FIRST so the answer survives truncation** -- and here the surviving
932:half would have been the wrong half. A verdict line is a summary written against the
933:brief's own framing, so it is the LEAST likely part of an answer to hold the correction.
934:Read the body of any seat whose verdict merely agrees.
935:
936:**And the strongest corrector gave the reason the enumeration could not:** `r == 3` is
937:exactly `(relative_addr & 0x0c) == 0x0c` -- *the fourth word of every 16-byte group* --
938:while all twelve implemented cases are three CONSECUTIVE words at group bases
939:`0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30`. **A structural invariant tells a future edit what it must not do;
940:an enumeration only tells you today's answer.** It also checked the one thing the brief
941:asserted implicitly and nobody else tested -- the DOMAIN of `relative_addr` -- confirming
942:from `memory_rw.c` and `core/memory.c` that it is exactly `[0, 0x100)` with no page
943:rounding and no fast path.
944:
945:> **A STATED PREMISE IS LOAD-BEARING FOR EVERY SEAT, AND AGREEMENT WITH IT IS NOT
946:> EVIDENCE.** The existing rule says packet-fed seats can only know what the brief inlines.
947:> The sharper form is that they cannot know what it inlines WRONGLY -- and neither, in
948:> practice, do the file-reading seats, because a brief that presents a derivation directs
949:> them to check the arithmetic instead of the premise.
950:
951:**Two things follow, and the second is the cheap one:**
952:* **Unanimity on a brief-supplied fact is worth nothing.** Score agreement only where a seat
953:  says it re-derived the fact from something the brief did NOT give it. This is the same
954:  distinction the seat ledger already draws between a READING and a MEASUREMENT, applied one
955:  level up -- to the brief itself.
956:* **State premises as questions where they are cheap to check.** Writing *"I believe these
957:  are the only reachable offsets -- check whether `relative_addr` can be unaligned"* costs
958:  one clause and converts a ratification into a test. The seat that found this was the only
959:  one whose answer engaged with alignment at all.
960:
961:*Consequence in this instance, and it is why the class matters rather than being a tidy-up:
962:a detector built from the wrong census walks sixteen addresses, covers a QUARTER of the
963:defect, and reports full coverage.*
964:
965:### *** THE REMEDY MEASURED: PUT THE PREMISE-AUDIT INSTRUCTION IN THE BRIEF. Second
966:### trial, 2026-08-24, and it found FOUR wrong premises in one brief. ***
967:
968:The next brief opened with: *"please do not simply ratify — a recent panel had seven of
969:nine seats confirm a premise this brief stated wrongly. **Check the premises, not just the
970:derivations.**"*  Four of its load-bearing premises were then refuted, each by a different
971:seat, and **not one of them was refuted by argument — every refutation was a file opened
972:or a command run:**
973:
974:| my premise | how it died |
975:|---|---|
976:| *"Site A is already half-fixed, so someone decided the kill was wrong"* | `git log -L 676,700:<file>` returns ONE commit — the upstream import. The fork never touched those lines |
977:| *"quoted verbatim from"* over a condensed comment | the real comment is **thirteen lines**; the brief showed two, with no ellipsis |
978:| *"nobody noticed"* | `#461`'s census had already counted the three disabled exits, and another row named the live one by its diagnostic |
979:| *"removing the guard deletes a tripwire that announced itself loudly"* | the `fatal()` REMAINS — only process termination goes. The tripwire still fires, it just stops shooting |
980:
981:**And the corrections were BETTER ARGUMENTS than the claims they replaced**, which is the
982:part worth internalising. The upstream-residue reading (*whichever exit the author happened
983:to HIT while developing against one driver*) is a stronger warrant for de-fatalising than
984:"someone decided", because it explains why the live kill survived: **that driver never
985:wrote the bad value — not that such writes are unthinkable.**
986:
987:> **THE RULE: state a brief's load-bearing premises AS QUESTIONS, and say plainly that
988:> ratification is not wanted.** Seats that would otherwise verify a derivation will
989:> verify the assumption under it. Two trials, two catches, and the second found four.
990:
991:*Cost: one paragraph. It also produced the session's only finding that would have hidden a
992:defect outright — a queue id covering TWO defects, where closing it on one would have
993:buried the other.*
994:
995:Panel rules, learned the hard way:
996:- *** A READING SEAT BUYS THE CLASS; A MEASURING SEAT BUYS THE INSTANCE — AND THE CLASS ARRIVES
997:  FIRST, FOR FREE. *** On #434's pass 1 a reading seat wrote that the planned detector was *"too
998:  clean and will green-light state-dependent mutants that still purge on the only path a guest
999:  takes."* **Nobody read it for a day.** Over the next two rounds measuring seats independently
1000:  BUILT that exact class twice — value guards (`&& idata`, `odata != 0`, a UAPR-only equality
1001:  guard) and selector guards (`&& d->cmmu_nr == 0`) — each discovery costing a full pass-2
1002:  cycle. The measuring seats were necessary to PROVE the instances; the reading seat had already
1003:  named the family. **So when a reading seat names a FAMILY of mutants, hand that sentence to
1004:  the measuring seat as its next assignment rather than waiting to rediscover the members.**
1005:  Honest limit, because one instance cannot settle it: whether that prediction was load-bearing
1006:  or merely lucky is not decidable from a single case. It is recorded as a heuristic worth
1007:  acting on, not as a law. *(From ledger row `m8invpred`. The lesson was moved here FIRST and the row's closure held
1008:  behind a seat — an earlier draft of this line said "dropped", which ran ahead of the gate
1009:  that had refused the closure. The adjudicator caught it. Record the move, not the outcome.)*
1010:- **Verify every seat actually answered** — one 246 KB "answer" was the echoed prompt.
1011:- **Disagreements are settled empirically** (instrument and boot), never by vote — the
1012:  majority has been wrong on mechanism repeatedly (≥6 recorded cases; round 100's Opus
1013:  seat overturned a confident measured reproduction by *compiling* variants).
1014:- Seat health drifts. If a CLI wedges, re-test before dropping the seat.
1015:
1016:## *** THE HARNESS MOVED INTO GIT ON 2026-08-17 — EVERY PATH BELOW CHANGED ***
1017:
1018:It lived at the project root, **outside version control**, until the owner was asked and chose to
1019:track it. It is now `GXEMUL-SEC/tools/` — `precommit_check.sh`, `nightly_battery.sh`,
1020:`nightly_check.sh`, `panel.sh`, `adjudicate.sh`, `panel_ollama.py`, and `tools/pipeline/` with the
1021:ledger, `gen_dashboard.py`, `check_seats_read.py`, `check_stage_panels.py` and `fable_queue.md`.
1022:See `GXEMUL-SEC/tools/README.md`, which records which failure each gate was built after.
1023:
1024:Scripts now DERIVE the project root from their own location (`$GXROOT` overrides). They used to
1025:hardcode one machine's absolute path, which is only half-tracked. **The migration got that wrong
1026:once and the dead-man switch caught itself** — a dangling `[ -d "$ROOT" ] ||` swallowed the new
1027:assignment, `ROOT` resolved empty, and `nightly_check.sh` reported FAIL rather than passing.
1028:
1029:The scheduled task `GXemul-weekly-battery` was repointed at `tools/nightly_battery.sh` and then
1030:TRIGGERED to prove it fires. Its `LastRunTime` had read `11/30/1999` for its whole first week: it
1031:had never run, and nothing said so.
1032:
1033:**Two gates were added the same week and they are NOT redundant** — `precommit_check.sh` section
1034:**G** verifies that a seat which ANSWERED was recorded; section **H** verifies the seat was FIRED
1035:at all. Neither catches the other's failure. Two more followed, and the same warning applies:
1036:**I** checks that flagship work which is OWED is written down (H reads a HELD marker as "waiting",
1037:and waiting is indistinguishable from forgotten), and **J** checks the carrier copies below.
1038:
1039:### THIS FILE IS TRACKED BY COPY, AND SECTION J IS WHY THAT IS SAFE
1040:
1041:Owner decision 2026-08-17: `CLAUDE.md` and `PIPELINE.md` are tracked. They cannot be tracked
1042:*where they sit* — **the git root is `GXEMUL-SEC/`, one level BELOW the project root**, and the
1043:carrier has to sit at the project root to be auto-loaded. So the tracked form is a copy at
1044:`GXEMUL-SEC/tools/CLAUDE.md` and `tools/PIPELINE.md`.
1045:
1046:**A copy nothing checks is the antipattern the owner had just rejected** (a dated
1047:`CLAUDE.md.snapshot_20260817` was deleted rather than committed, for the same reason RESUME.md
1048:went stale for 26 rounds). So `precommit_check.sh` section **J** hard-fails on byte-divergence,
1049:using check A's `cmp` idiom. **The root file is the live one; after editing it, re-copy.** An
1050:absent root original is NOT a failure — on a fresh clone the repo copy is the only one there is.
1051:
1052:`tools/` and not `GXEMUL-SEC/` deliberately: the cwd DRIFTS (it has sat in `GXEMUL-SEC/regress`
1053:for whole rounds), and a copy at `GXEMUL-SEC/CLAUDE.md` would auto-load *alongside* the root
1054:original, putting two copies of the carrier in one context -- **151 KiB** as of 2026-08-26, and
1055:this line said 38 KB, which was true when it was written and has quadrupled since.
1056:
1057:## Tooling that makes the panel automatic (the "always" layers)
1058:
1059:1. **Carrier** — this `CLAUDE.md`, auto-loaded every session.
1060:1a. **THE PIPELINE DOCTRINE** — `PIPELINE.md` (project root): the owner's portable multi-LLM
1061:   fix-pipeline methodology, supplied 2026-08-15 and ADOPTED (pasted into the loop five times —
1062:   that is a directive). Two seat CLASSES recorded on every dispatch: FILE-READING (Codex, agy,
1063:   Grok, Opus, Fable — facts about the repo) vs PACKET-FED (the Ollama transports —
1064:   attack reasoning only; their blindness is a feature; NEVER equivalent votes on repo fact).
1065:   *** KIMI IS CLASSIFIED BY ITS TRANSPORT, NOT BY ITS NAME, and this line listed it as
1066:   FILE-READING until 2026-08-26. *** The CLI is file-reading; the live substitute
1067:   `kimi-k3:cloud` goes through `panel_ollama.py` and is a **FOURTH PACKET-FED
1068:   transport**.
1069:   *** THIS SENTENCE SAID THE SUBSTITUTE GOES THROUGH `panel_ollama.py` "with
1070:   `PACKET_BRIEF`".  THERE WAS NO SUCH THING.  Measured 2026-08-27 by opening the
1071:   file: it was 24 lines and POSTed the brief VERBATIM, with no preamble of any
1072:   kind. ***
1073:   The cost was a lost seat.  A brief written for the file-reading seats carries
1074:   instructions a packet-fed seat cannot obey -- *"check the premises against the
1075:   source"*, *"grep the fork"* -- and a seat that TRIES to comply stalls.  On the
1076:   `wdcdataoutdrain` pass 1 the substitute returned **127 bytes** (*"I'll check the
1077:   premises against the actual tree ... Starting by locating"*) and stopped; with the
1078:   CLI 403-walled that lost the kimi seat entirely, and `seat_verdicts.py` filed it as
1079:   *"answered, NO VERDICT LINE (format miss)"* rather than as the stall it was.
1080:   **This is the carrier's own "it was never the seat, it was the BRIEF" recurring,
1081:   for the documented reason: the remedy lived in PROSE (remember to inline every
1082:   fact) instead of in the tool that fires every time.**  `panel_ollama.py` now
1083:   prepends a `PACKET_PREAMBLE` telling the seat it cannot read files and asking it to
1084:   name any premise it cannot settle from the packet -- so an uncheckable premise comes
1085:   back as an honest "not checkable" instead of silence.  Controlled in four states,
1086:   including the negative one (the pre-fix source must NOT carry the preamble).
1087:   *A tool's own source is the primary authority about the tool; this row asserted a
1088:   wrapper that a 24-line file plainly did not have.*
1089:   Since the CLI is 403-walled, **the kimi answers actually
1090:   arriving today are PACKET-FED** -- so crediting one as a repo-fact reading is exactly
1091:   the conflation this doctrine forbids.
1092:   Conflict-of-interest badges on every closure: `A` = author self-review disclosed, `s` =
1093:   same-model replication (weaker than two-family, never passed off as it). Evidence ladder
1094:   CONFIRMED > MANUAL > BELIEVED > UNKNOWN — an honest UNKNOWN outranks a confident guess.
1095:   VERDICT LINE FIRST in every requested seat output (truncation survives). Echo-test = nonce in
1096:   the prompt + nonce in a file, both required back. **The ledger**: `GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json` is
1097:   the single source of truth; `GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/gen_dashboard.py` renders **`matrix.html`** (published as
1098:   a claude.ai artifact; this line said `dashboard.html` until 2026-08-26 -- the
1099:   generator itself documents the stale name at its `OUT =`) — write the ledger, regenerate, republish, NEVER hand-edit the output.
1100:   Two divergences held deliberately per newer owner directives, recorded in PIPELINE.md's
1101:   adoption section: the loop does not halt at phase boundaries, and a dead seat degrades
1102:   (recorded by name) rather than pausing the fix.
1103:1b. **THE DEAD-MAN SWITCH** — `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/nightly_check.sh [max_age_h]`.
1104:   *** THE NIGHTLY BATTERY IS UNCONDITIONAL, YET NOTHING MADE THE ABSENCE OF A TERMINAL
1105:   RECORD A FAIL. *** Every threshold in this harness lives INSIDE the run, so none of them
1106:   can observe the run not happening — gate 5 cannot report that gate 5 never started. A run
1107:   that never fires (logged-out session, task unregistered, reboot) produced NO RED ROW
1108:   ANYWHERE. Named by an adjudicating seat on 2026-08-15 as the sharpest gap three rounds of
1109:   harness work had left. It separates NEVER RAN (no verdict file, or one older than the
1110:   window — the scheduled task is "Interactive only", so a sleeping machine skips it
1111:   SILENTLY) from RAN BADLY (a verdict whose four signals disagree), and it treats a STALE
1112:   verdict as the same failure as a missing one, because reading yesterday's green as today's
1113:   is the "a green row means nothing" class in its purest form. It also checks the HEAD stamp
1114:   and TREE_DIRTY: a verdict that does not name what it tested cannot be attributed.
1115:   Negative-controlled in both directions on the day it was written. **It generalises a check
1116:   that was being tracked by hand — and a check a human has to remember is a check that
1117:   eventually does not happen.**
1118:2. **One-command convening** — `./panel.sh <brief_file>` fires **EIGHT transports**
1119:   concurrently (seven logical scriptable seats, because the Kimi CLI and its
1120:   `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute are two transports for ONE seat), verifies each produced a
1121:   real answer, and reminds you to launch **Opus AND Fable** via the Agent tool. *This
1122:   read "five scriptable seats ... the sixth (Opus)" until 2026-08-26.* `panel_ollama.py` is its cloud-seat helper. Low
1123:   friction is what makes "always" actually hold. **ALWAYS invoke it as
1124:   `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh <ABSOLUTE brief path>`, never `./panel.sh`:**
1125:   the session cwd DRIFTS (it has sat in `GXEMUL-SEC/regress` for whole rounds), and from
1126:   there `./panel.sh` dies with "No such file or directory" — a SILENT zero-seat failure
1127:   that looks like a completed run, so you review the previous round's stale seat files by
1128:   mistake. After firing, confirm a NEW `_scratchpad/panel_<timestamp>/` dir exists and each
1129:   seat file is > a few hundred bytes before trusting any of it (a 152-byte file is a
1130:   truncated-thinking-model non-answer, not a review).
1131:2b. **One-command adjudication** — `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/adjudicate.sh
1132:   <finding_file> [tail_bytes]` fires the three Ollama cloud seats at ONE seat's finding
1133:   and asks them to REFUTE it, not summarise it. panel.sh convenes a panel on a *brief*;
1134:   this is the other direction, for when a single seat has produced research that needs
1135:   independent scrutiny before it is acted on. It INLINES the finding (the cloud seats
1136:   cannot read files) and takes only the TAIL by default, because Codex streams its
1137:   reasoning into the output file and the answer lives at the end — a Codex research run
1138:   here has exceeded 1 MB. Standing user directive (2026-08-13): **farm Codex 5.6-SOL at
1139:   xhigh out in parallel across independent read-only queue items — credits are plentiful
1140:   and slow runs are fine — then adjudicate what comes back with the Ollama seats.** Read
1141:   -only Codex jobs do not contend with the build trees or rigs, so many can run at once;
1142:   gates and probes still may not run while they do.
1143:
1144:3. **Deterministic reinforcement** (`.claude/settings.local.json` hooks →
1145:   `.claude/hooks/round_reminder.sh`): a **SessionStart** hook re-injects the panel
1146:   directive each session; a **TaskCompleted** hook fires the moment a task is marked
1147:   done and injects the end-of-round parallel-work step (harvest residuals → start the
1148:   next item's brief/panel while gates run). The hook triggers on the event, so it does
1149:   not depend on remembering.
1150:4. **One-keystroke round** — `/round` runs a whole correction round end-to-end
1151:   (reproduce → panel pass 1 → implement → panel pass 2 → gates → commit → parallel triage).
1152:
1153:The panel's *quality* — good adversarial review, honest empirical adjudication — is
1154:judgment and cannot be enforced; the layers above guarantee it is always *convened* and
1155:that the parallel-work step always *fires*, which is the enforceable part.
1156:
1157:## The self-sustaining monitor loop
1158:
1159:The queue is worked as a continuous loop, kept alive by three things together: the
1160:background-completion notifications (each finished panel seat / gate / battery re-invokes
1161:the main loop), the TaskCompleted hook (fires the end-of-task triage), and — for idle gaps
1162:where nothing is running — a `/loop` driver (self-paced; it re-invokes the main loop to
1163:start the next item). **The loop runs on THIS machine**, because the panel is local
1164:(localhost Ollama + Windows CLI binaries); a cloud/cron run cannot reach the panel.
1165:
1166:Each cycle of the loop, the main model (Fable) does:
1167:
1168:1. **Advance the in-flight task.** If a panel / gate / battery is running, do NOT start
1169:   contending work — wait for its notification. Never two gate/harness invocations at
1170:   once; before re-running a battery, explicitly stop any prior run (a rebuild does NOT
1171:   stop it). One gate run, one gate log. **"Contending" includes CPU-heavy work that
1172:   touches no tree at all** — panel seats, subagents, big greps. Gates carry wall-clock
1173:   oracles. *`gate_ab`'s 300 s WALL budget was REPLACED by an accounting of GUEST WORK
1174:   in #419 (`LUNA_BUDGET`, `LUNA_STALL`, `LUNA_BACKSTOP`); this line said "300 s against
1175:   a ~100 s normal" until 2026-08-26. The contention hazard is unchanged -- only the
1176:   oracle's shape, and it is still load-sensitive.* In
1177:   round 108 two research subagents plus interactive commands pushed HEAD past that
1178:   budget, producing a FAIL indistinguishable from a real capability regression (a bare
1179:   `ls` under WSL took >120 s in that window; re-run alone it passed 1:1:1). Serialize:
1180:   gate first, then panel. Docs, queue triage and brief-writing are the safe filler.
1181:2. **On task completion → panel review + suggestions.** Convene the full panel
1182:   (`./panel.sh` + Opus) to review the completed work AND to surface new tasks — residual
1183:   defects, sibling bugs, instrument gaps, follow-ups. After-panel findings have become
1184:   the next rounds repeatedly.
1185:3. **Fable adjudicates each suggested task.** For every new candidate the panel raises,
1186:   the main model decides: is it worth adding? (real defect / reachable / in-scope /
1187:   test-first-able vs. noise or duplicate.) Record the verdict; add the worthwhile ones
1188:   with TaskCreate, drop the rest with a one-line reason.
1189:4. **Assign parallelism.** For each added task, decide whether a subagent can start it
1190:   NOW in parallel: read-only work (source scoping, byte-order/idiom investigation,
1191:   reachability analysis, brief-drafting) parallelizes freely and should be handed to a
1192:   background subagent to de-risk the round before its panel convenes (as the round-101
1193:   byte-order subagent did). Tree-contending work (builds, probes, gates) is serial —
1194:   it waits its turn behind the single gate/rig/build-tree writer.
1195:5. **Claim the next task** and run the round: reproduce (test-first) → panel pass 1 →
1196:   implement → panel pass 2 → gate (single clean run) → docs → commit/push → back to 1.
1197:
1198:The loop ends only when the queue is empty or a task needs a human decision (destructive
1199:action, genuine scope change). It does not stop for a long session.
1200:
1201:**Panel roster for the loop — NINE seats by design, and as of 2026-08-26 ALL NINE ANSWER.**
1202:The kimi CLI is 403-walled and its seat is covered by the `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute, which
1203:`panel.sh` fires automatically and reports as COVERED rather than lost. *A dead seat is a SEAT
1204:FAILURE and is never counted as agreement — and never silently: say which seats answered in the
1205:round's record. But do not read a stale status line as a dead seat either; this paragraph
1206:claimed two dead seats for over a week after both were answering.*
1207:`bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh` fires EIGHT scriptable transports: Codex 5.6-SOL (xhigh —
1208:the CLI's "ultra" intent), agy 3.7 (`gemini-3.7-flash-high`, `--effort high`), Kimi 3 (max
1209:effort), **Grok 4.6 (high, `--prompt-file` so no argv cap)**, Ollama glm-5.3:cloud
1210:(migrated from glm-5.2 on 2026-08-28, pull- and liveness-verified),
1211:deepseek-v4-pro:cloud, minimax-m3:cloud. Two more are launched in the same turn via the
1212:Agent tool: **Opus 5** (`model: "opus"`) and **Fable 5** (`model: "fable"`). Fable also
1213:ADJUDICATES the panel's task suggestions in its main-loop role — seat and adjudicator are
1214:distinct hats; the adjudication happens in the main loop, not in the seat.
1215:
1216:**ADJUDICATION IS A MAIN-LOOP ROLE THAT FABLE HOLDS BY PREFERENCE, and that distinction is
1217:load-bearing: it DEGRADES rather than blocks.** Proved on 2026-08-13 when the Fable seat was
1218:genuinely walled: the adjudication still ran, in the main loop, and worked — 9 of 13 candidates
1219:dropped, folded or retired, two of which would have caused harm. The seat has since been live
1220:for days and holds both hats. What made the degraded form work was NOT the model: every one of
1221:the five drops was settled by MEASUREMENT — a grep, a run, a five-rig spelling check — not
1222:by judgement. **So the rule that earns its keep is: any candidate checkable in under about
1223:two minutes gets CHECKED, not judged.** Keep that whichever model holds the hat.
1224:
1225:## FAN OUT WIDE, CONVERGE ON ONE PANEL (standing user directive, 2026-08-13)
1226:
1227:**"Utilise tokens as much as we can, but circle back for a full panel review."** The two
1228:halves land on different resources, and that is the whole point:
1229:
1230:* **UNLIMITED — fan out hard.** Agent-tool subagents (`model: "opus"`) and farmed Codex runs
1231:  have no quota that has ever bitten. Read-only work parallelises freely: source tracing,
1232:  reachability analysis, round design, brief-drafting, records audits. Launch several in one
1233:  turn. This is where "use as many tokens as needed" applies, and today it was the single
1234:  highest-yield resource — the measure-seat overturned the main loop FOUR times.
1235:* **RATE-LIMITED — spend once, at the convergence point.** Measured 2026-08-13: the three
1236:  Ollama cloud seats return HTTP 429 if two panels fire within ~20+ minutes (21 was not
1237:  enough); Grok 4.6's free tier died after ~4 substantial invocations; Kimi is 403-dead.
1238:
1239:  *** ALL THREE OF THOSE ARE NOW STALE, AND THE FIRST WAS MEASURED FALSE ON 2026-08-20. ***
1240:  FIVE full panels fired in one evening, with gaps of 66, 33, 13, **5** and **11** minutes --
1241:  two of them far inside the "~20+ minutes" threshold -- and **all six non-codex seats
1242:  answered every one**, substantively:
1243:
1244:  | panel | agy | grok | kimi | glm | deepseek | minimax |
1245:  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
1246:  | 22:20 | 15k | 9k | 87k | 8k | 3k | 10k |
1247:  | 23:26 | 12k | 9k | 45k | 13k | 4k | 16k |
1248:  | 23:59 | 12k | 11k | 72k | 19k | 5k | 11k |
1249:  | 00:12 | 11k | 7k | 37k | 6k | 3k | 8k |
1250:  | 00:17 | 14k | 13k | 61k | 14k | 5k | 9k |
1251:
1252:  No 429, no degradation, no shrinking answers. Grok is PAID now and Kimi is back, so those
1253:  two clauses expired months ago; the Ollama one expired without anyone re-testing it. **The
1254:  "ONE full panel per ROUND" policy rested on this constraint, and the constraint is gone** --
1255:  which is the same failure this file already records one paragraph below: *"A policy written
1256:  around two dead seats outlived both of them by a week."* It happened again, to the paragraph
1257:  that says it.
1258:
1259:  **The rule that replaces it: fire a panel whenever there is a brief worth panelling, and let
1260:  the seat check report a wall if one comes.** Do not ration against a limit nobody has
1261:  observed. The one real cost is CONTENTION -- panel seats are CPU-heavy and must not run
1262:  during a gate, which is a scheduling constraint and not a quota.
1263:
1264:  **VERIFY CONTENT, NEVER SIZE.** Codex's wall, when it had one, was five echoed-brief-plus-429
1265:  files of 26,992 / 11,180 / 10,036 / 8,323 / 12,414 bytes — *a size check alone scores those the
1266:  largest answers in their panels.* That lesson is permanent; the outage was not.
1267:
1268:  *This block used to END with "So: ONE full panel per ROUND, not one per pass" — the SUPERSEDED
1269:  conclusion, sitting after the rule that replaced it and holding the closing position of
1270:  authority. Which is this file's own "a policy written around two dead seats outlived both of
1271:  them" failure, happening a second time, inside the paragraph that records it. **When a rule is
1272:  superseded, delete its conclusion; do not demote it to a footnote that still reads as the
1273:  verdict.***
1274:
1275:## THE VACUITY FLOOR AND THE DE-ESCALATION CLAUSE (flagship adjudication, 2026-08-21)
1276:
1277:**"Weak earns its own round; vacuous does not get to ship" IS A RATIONALISATION UNTIL A FLOOR IS
1278:ATTACHED** — the adjudicating seat said so about the main loop's own call, and it was right:
1279:*weak* has no lower bound, so any detector that kills one mutant can claim it.
1280:
1281:> **THE FLOOR.** A detector is VACUOUS — replace it IN-ROUND — unless it (a) fails on the
1282:> pre-fix build, (b) fails on a **VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT of the removed defect at EVERY fixed
1283:> site**, and (c) fails on a null medium (a stale copy, a comment-only file — i.e. it carries
1284:> identity rows). Below the floor, the round's own claim to have "shipped a detector with the
1285:> fix" is a WRONG RECORD, which the stopping rule already fixes in-round.
1286:>
1287:> ### *** CLAUSE (d) — NULL-REPAIR RED. Adopted 2026-08-23 by OWNER RULING, on the round
1288:> that measured its case. ***
1289:>
1290:> **(d) The detector must also fail when the fix is PRESENT but its REPAIR IS NULLED —
1291:> the guard kept, its effect removed.**
1292:>
1293:> **(b) DOES NOT IMPLY (d), and #461 measured both in the same hour on the same detector:**
1294:>
1295:> | mutant | shape | score |
1296:> |---|---|---|
1297:> | `m_dmarevert` | (b): reinstate the `exit(1)` verbatim | **22/31 — killed** |
1298:> | `m_align1` | (d): keep the guard, weaken `& 3` to `& 1` | **27/27 — ESCAPED** |
1299:>
1300:> The detector passed (a), (b) and (c) and was still blind to a ONE-CHARACTER edit that
1301:> re-opened the very defect the round had just closed. (b) restores the DEFECT; (d) neuters
1302:> the FIX. A detector can be sensitive to one and blind to the other, because they fail at
1303:> different sites: (b) fails where the old code was, (d) fails where the new code is.
1304:>
1305:> **HOW TO BUILD THE (d) MUTANT — and it is the same discriminator the accept-row rule
1306:> already uses.** Take each predicate the fix **DEPENDS ON** and weaken it to the
1307:> least-restrictive form that still *reads* like the same check: a mask narrowed
1308:> (`& 3` -> `& 1`), a condition qualified (`if (X)` -> `if (X && writeflag == MEM_WRITE)`),
1309:> a bound relaxed. **Not deletion** — deletion is (b) wearing (d)'s clothes and is usually
1310:> already covered. The test is whether a plausible future edit could produce it.
1311:>
1312:> **WHY THE ONE-CHARACTER FORM IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS.** `dma alias`, the row written
1313:> specifically to pin that repair, was **STRUCTURALLY BLIND** to it: it used an ODD address,
1314:> which both guard forms drop. *The row aimed at the defect could not see the defect return.*
1315:> A row written from the FIX's intent tests the intent, not the boundary — so (d)'s mutant
1316:> has to come from an adversary, which is exactly what the ask-again buys.
1317:>
1318:> **COST: one mutant per repair predicate**, the same order as the accept-row rule (#448 = 4,
1319:> #449 = 1, #461 = 4 across two arms). It is bounded by the diff, so it cannot become #392:
1320:> the predicates are the ones the fix ADDED, and that list is finite and visible.
1321:>
1322:> ### *** "THE PREDICATE THE FIX INTRODUCED" ENUMERATED THE EMPTY SET FOR MOST OF THIS
1323:> ### QUEUE, AND NOBODY NOTICED FOR THREE ROUNDS. Corrected 2026-08-24 -- clause (d) now
1324:> ### reads DEPENDS ON, not INTRODUCED. ***
1325:>
1326:> Clause (d) says *keep the guard, null its repair*. As written it enumerated only
1327:> predicates the diff ADDS -- and **the two commonest fix shapes here add none**:
1328:>
1329:> * a **pure deletion** introduces no predicate at all;
1330:> * a **DE-FATALISATION** introduces none either -- it changes what an EXISTING
1331:>   predicate's branch does. That is most of this project's queue.
1332:>
1333:> So for those rounds clause (d) enumerated **nothing**, and a round could satisfy the
1334:> floor while having built no (d) mutant whatever. `#461` measured (d) successfully only
1335:> because its fix happened to ADD a predicate (an alignment mask) for the mutant to weaken.
1336:>
1337:> **Reading it as DEPENDS ON fixes both cases at once**, and it is why this now replaces a
1338:> narrower deletion-only rule written earlier the same day: that rule was a special case of
1339:> this gap, and two overlapping statements of one rule is the accumulation this carrier is
1340:> supposed to refuse.
1341:>
1342:> **The two shapes, once the enumeration is right:**
1343:> * **fix ADDS a predicate** -> weaken the added one (unchanged; the original rule).
1344:> * **fix adds none** -> the (d) mutant reroutes an EXISTING guard's repair. For a
1345:>   de-fatalisation: **swap the guard's operand for a field the guarded BODY reads.**
1346:>   For a pure deletion: **re-introduce the removed behaviour under a DIFFERENT predicate**
1347:>   -- not the same one widened, which is (b) wearing (d)'s clothes.
1348:>
1349:> *** THE MEASURED CASE, and it is why this matters rather than being tidy: ***
1350:> `#462` de-fatalised two arms. Its guard reads `d->data`; the body it guards reads
1351:> `d->data` AND `d->data_len`; the two are set together in one place and cleared in two
1352:> places that touch only ONE of them. Swapping the guard's operand to `d->data_len == 0`
1353:> **scored a CLEAN SWEEP on a 52-row detector that had just passed twelve mutants**, and
1354:> restored a NULL-pointer read of 408 bytes. Under the old wording that was coverage debt;
1355:> under DEPENDS ON it is a **floor breach, L2, fix in-round** -- which is what the round
1356:> did, before the wording was corrected.
1357:>
1358:> #### *** THE BOUND, and it is not optional: "DEPENDS ON" INVITES TRANSITIVE DATA-FLOW
1359:> #### CHASING, which is unbounded and is how this becomes `#392`. Named 2026-08-24 by the
1360:> #### adjudicating seat, in the same breath as upholding the correction. ***
1361:>
1362:> > **DEPENDS ON enumerates exactly two things: (1) the predicates DOMINATING the diff's
1363:> > edited lines, walked up only to the ENCLOSING FUNCTION BOUNDARY -- never into callees
1364:> > or callers; and (2) for operand-swap forms, swaps drawn ONLY from fields the guarded
1365:> > BODY reads (the guard-field/body-field grep the pre-table check already mandates).**
1366:>
1367:> That is finite, derivable from the diff by reading, and **it provably contains the
1368:> measured case**: the `d->data == NULL` guard dominates the edited decline (clause 1), and
1369:> `data_len` is a body-read field (clause 2), so `M11` falls out mechanically rather than
1370:> by inspiration. It cannot become `#392` because the enumeration stays closed over the
1371:> diff's own hunks -- the same closure the INTRODUCED wording had, without the empty-set
1372:> defect that made INTRODUCED enumerate nothing for a de-fatalisation.
1373:>
1374:> #### *** JOINT CELLS -- adjudicated 2026-09-04 on `pcc2widthkills` pass 10 by the flagship seat, after
1375:> #### a measuring seat's CONJUNCTION of two body-read counts escaped 190/190 AND THE SAME SEAT ARGUED IT
1376:> #### WAS NOT A BREACH.  Both proposed bounds were rejected; the one adopted came from neither. ***
1377:>
1378:> > **DEPENDS-ON enumerates JOINT cells over the guard's operands and the body-read lvalues EXACTLY
1379:> > WHEN THE GUARDED BODY'S OWN CODE DISTINGUISHES THEM.**  For each lvalue take the PARTITION the
1380:> > body's own tests induce (`pending > 0` gives {0, >0}; `relative_addr == PCCTWO_T1ICR` gives
1381:> > {T1, T2}); the enumerated cells are the CROSS-PRODUCT of those partitions -- never arbitrary
1382:> > boolean functions of the read-set.  A mutant exempting a single cell whose reachability is
1383:> > MEASURED is a floor breach; close the whole cross-product in ONE diff and write into the
1384:> > contract which cells are rowed.  A value or combination the body never tests as a case
1385:> > (`idata == k`; a byte no conditional distinguishes; both counts >= 2 where the body tests `> 0`)
1386:> > is DISCLOSED under clause (e).
1387:>
1388:> **Why this form and not the two that were argued.**  The dissent's discriminator -- *a plausible
1389:> future edit* -- failed three ways: plausibility is not derivable by reading; the mutant was written
1390:> and measured, so it is an edit someone writes; and "single-operand is complete" is measurably
1391:> false, since `&& idata != 2` is single-operand and escapes.  The main loop's own bound -- *pairs of
1392:> body-read lvalues over a finite reachable space* -- was rejected as SELF-REFUTING: the counts' raw
1393:> value space is 2^32 apiece, so "finite" does no bounding work on its motivating case (a C `int` is
1394:> finite -- the first wording here said it was not; codex and glm caught it on pass 11); "reachable
1395:> guest state" would force rowing
1396:> `idata == 2` that the same record discloses; and a PAIRS cap misses `#499`'s four axes.  The
1397:> body-tested-partition form is finite (a product of small partition counts), derivable from the
1398:> diff by reading the body's own `if`s, and it enumerates `#499`'s width x component x direction x
1399:> position cells from `len`, `writeflag` and `relative_addr` -- which neither argued bound reached.
1400:>
1401:> **The measured case.**  The TxICR body tests both pending counts at `> 0` and the label at
1402:> `== T1ICR` / `T2ICR`, so the closure is {T2, T1} x {p1 = 0, > 0} x {p2 = 0, > 0} -- eight cells.
1403:> Pass 9 held one open cell (both counts, T2 label: a 190/190 escape, predicted by the Opus seat
1404:> before its build); pass 10 found the MIRROR (both counts, T1 label) and the (p2 only, T1 label)
1405:> cell open -- the `#499` shape reproduced inside the repair for it -- and the label x p1 x p2 x IEN
1406:> product (sixteen cells) closed in one diff of nine readbacks in existing sessions; the ICLR, INT and
1407:> 0xe0 partitions were disclosed by count, and pass 11 then found the ICLR x pending cell open and
1408:> in-rule (this paragraph said "eight cells ... three readbacks" until pass 11).  **The De Morgan form of the gap**, from the kimi
1409:> substitute: `!(p1 && p2)` is `p1 == 0 || p2 == 0`; neither disjunct is true of all its lvalue's
1410:> held values, the disjunction is true in every row, so a per-lvalue disclosure test can never fire
1411:> on it.  That is why the per-lvalue value-domain statement, correct as far as it went, could not
1412:> see this class at all.
1413:>
1414:> **Two corollaries from the same pass, both measured.**  The PRE-TABLE CHECK's SIBLING PARITY row
1415:> compares BLOCKS; at a block that serves TWO case labels the siblings are the LABELS, and a
1416:> parity census over the row names is what would have shown `pendboth` as the only T2-only cell
1417:> before its table ran (Opus, pass 10).  **AND THE JOIN ITSELF, adjudicated 2026-09-09 on `sgimtedepth4dead` 7g-1 after a MEASURED total escape: a dispatch LABEL is a floor axis of an edited guard when the selector's value FLOWS INTO an lvalue the guarded body reads or writes within the enclosing function (there, `dst_mode = (dst_tlb << 10) + ...` two lines above a repair of `dst_mode |= RGBA`), or when the body re-tests it after the join (TxICR); a label that reaches the edit and touches nothing the body reads is clause (e) (the `mode & COPY` shape).  A detector rowing one label of three was swept 14/14 by `&& dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A` while a guest-only control showed the closed defect live at the other two (`regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/killtable3_20260909/`).  The floor it adds is {labels} x the guard operand's body-tested partition -- six cells there, two rows owed -- never the labels' cross-product with everything.**  And the measuring seat's own bound -- the cross-product
1418:> of the value-CLASSES the rows establish, with the dispatch axes as full axes, demand
1419:> INCREMENTAL -- agrees with the body-tested form on every cell that mattered here and differs on
1420:> where the classes come from (the rows, or the body's tests); that residual difference is
1421:> carried to the next pass as a named question rather than settled by the author.
1422:>
1423:> #### *** THE LETTER OVER-DEMANDED, AND ITS OWN AUTHOR BOUNDED IT ONE PASS LATER (pass 11, 2026-09-04). ***
1424:>
1425:> Read literally, "the cross-product of the body-tested partitions" demanded ALL 128 cells at the TxICR
1426:> arm -- the body also tests `idata & IEN`, `idata & ICLR`, the register's INT bit and `idata & 0xe0` --
1427:> and it compounds as 2^k in the `if`-count across twelve arms: the `#392` shape wearing the rule's
1428:> clothes.  Three seats said the letter demands the remainder; the flagship seat, asked to be harder on
1429:> its own rule than on anyone, ruled AGAINST the letter:
1430:>
1431:> > **The rule ENUMERATES joint cells wherever the body distinguishes them; the FLOOR -- the cells a row
1432:> > is OWED for -- is the cross-product of the guard's dominating predicates with the device-STATE lvalues
1433:> > the fix's own repair path reads that are JOINTLY REACHABLE through its own data flow.**  The bits of
1434:> > the single value being written are single-operand MARGINALS, not floor; joint cells outside the floor
1435:> > are clause-(e) coverage debt, subject to the one ask-again.  A filter may reject or discharge a cell
1436:> > but may demand a new row only for a floor cell -- so it cannot grow unbounded.
1437:>
1438:> At TxICR the floor is {label} x {p1} x {p2}: one `pcc_timer_tick` callback increments both counts and
1439:> `reassert_interrupts` reads both, so the pair is a joint input of the model.  Eight cells, all rowed.
1440:>
1441:> #### *** THE FLOOR INCLUDES THE SINGLE-AXIS GENERATING SET, AND "DERIVED" STATE IS NOT DISPOSABLE.
1442:> #### Adjudicated 2026-09-04 by the fix review (Fable 5.1 adjudicating; GPT-6 Astra at max dissenting on the
1443:> #### bound and supplying the two findings that made the amendment measurable). ***
1444:>
1445:> > **At each floor cell, every idata bit the guarded body TESTS is written both clear and set.**  The bare
1446:> > state floor above does not catch `M_ICLR` -- an idata-bit x state joint -- and would grade the pass-11
1447:> > escape as debt while the CHANGELOG grades it L2; the generating set is what makes the two consistent.
1448:> > Multi-axis idata joints stay clause-(e) debt.  **The codex seat adopts the Opus strength-t covering
1449:> > array instead** (its `t` = the body's longest conjunction) and is RECORDED, not adopted -- for COST (seven
1450:> > binary axes at TxICR make a strength-4 array tens of pty cells per arm: the 2^k growth by another door) and
1451:> > because a covering array certifies that a tuple OCCURS, not that an observable differs, so it cannot be the
1452:> > floor without the CHANNEL obligation doing the work.  *This clause first said "`t` is a judgment input";
1453:> > the array's own author refuted that on pass 13 -- `t` is read off the body -- and supplied the two grounds.*
1454:>
1455:> **The two measured consequences, both found by a seat reading the ENUMERATION against the handler rather
1456:> than the tables against the enumeration:**
1457:>
1458:> * **The enumeration listed five arms as having NO `if` and each has one** -- VECBASE, SCCICR, SCCTX, SCCRX,
1459:>   SCSIICR test their HUH? bits on the composed idata.  The record then said every wide value the rows drove
1460:>   at them had those bits SET, so `&& (idata & ~mask)` would sweep the table.  **Measured FALSE the same
1461:>   evening (part 13, M_HUHV 249/251):** two announce-count rows (`latch all zero`, `latch all one`) already
1462:>   drove HUH?-clear values (0x0000 alone at VECBASE, whose mask is 0xf0; the six `latch all*` rows at the
1463:>   0x1f/0xdf arms -- 244/251 there), and their counts drop when the write lands -- the partition was HELD by a channel
1464:>   the enumeration did not know it had.  *An enumeration is checked against the BODY and against the ROWS
1465:>   before a breach is declared; "it would sweep the table" is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference -- and the
1466:>   seat that raised the finding had said so ("existing rows may discharge their partitions; the enumeration
1467:>   must actually show that") while the main loop wrote the sweep as a fact.*
1468:> * **The INT bit was dismissed as "derived from the count" and it is not**: `reassert_interrupts()` only SETS
1469:>   it, the body clears it only on ICLR, and an IEN-clear byte write zeroes the count -- so (INT set, count 0)
1470:>   is a reachable joint state of two body-read lvalues that no row held.  **Reachability of a joint cell is
1471:>   derived from the WRITERS of each lvalue, never from a steady-state invariant** -- "INT follows the count"
1472:>   was true between ticks and false across the one write the guest driver makes to disable a timer.
1473:>
1474:> *Both went to pass 13 as floor cells (nine rows, seven mutants).  The fix itself was unchanged by the review,
1475:> which is the finding worth keeping about the review: eleven passes on the detector and none on the fix, and
1476:> the first pass on the fix approved it block by block while finding the detector's enumeration wrong.*
1477:>
1478:> **What the debt costs when it has a measured warrant, and it is cheap:** the measuring seat built the
1479:> disclosed `&& !(p2 && (idata & ICLR))` and it escaped the 203-row instrument, as three reading seats
1480:> had predicted -- so it was repaired in the GENERATING-SET form (grok), and GRADED **L2**, a floor breach
1481:> repaired in-round, by the pass-11 disposition in the CHANGELOG (the cell was inside the closure by the
1482:> round's own pass-3 sentence).  *This clause said "L1 construction" until 2026-09-04, when the GPT-6 Astra
1483:> fix review found the carrier and the CHANGELOG grading ONE finding two ways; the CHANGELOG's is the ruling
1484:> and this is the correction.*  The form: at every
1485:> rowed pending cell, write each tested idata bit both clear and set, so any single-axis extra conjunct on
1486:> one of them lands a write and reds a readback.  Twelve wide writes appended to three existing sessions;
1487:> no new session, no new load sensitivity.  Multi-axis idata joints stay disclosed; INT-clear-with-a-count-
1488:> pending is disclosed as UNREACHABLE with its derivation (the tick increments only under IEN and reassert
1489:> sets INT before any guard runs), which is the honest form of a cell nobody rows.
1490:>
1491:> *Two records lessons from the same pass.  A CORRECTION IS NOT SAFER THAN THE THING IT CORRECTS: pass 10
1492:> accepted "the store mask is a writer of ICLR" and made a true sentence false -- `0x17 & 0x08 == 0`, one
1493:> AND that nobody ran.  And a PREDICTION COPIED INTO A MEASURED COLUMN is the frozen-number class in a new
1494:> coat: a table said (0x13) where the log said 0x33; the score matched by coincidence and the value was a
1495:> wrong record until a seat read the log.*
1496:>
1497:> *Count the arguments, not the seats: seven of eight landed seats put the pair inside the closure,
1498:> and the one that did not (minimax) was reading the CONTRACT's per-lvalue wording rather than the
1499:> clause -- the wording the discriminator sentence now replaces.*
1500:
1501:> ### *** THE PRE-TABLE CHECK -- three greps, ~30 min, and it protects the ASK-AGAIN'S
1502:> ### CAP rather than the clock. Adopted 2026-08-24; two seats derived it independently. ***
1503:>
1504:> > ## *** MANDATORY as of 2026-08-28, by flagship adjudication. It was RECOMMENDED prose for
1505:> > ## four days and TWO rounds skipped it -- which is this file's own structure-first trigger,
1506:> > ## met exactly. ***
1507:> >
1508:> > **SCOPE, and this is what answers the cost objection: it binds EXACTLY the population the
1509:> > vacuity floor already binds** -- any round shipping or EXTENDING a kill-table-backed
1510:> > detector. **Not every round.** Records-only, documented-only and table-less harness rounds
1511:> > are untouched.
1512:> >
1513:> > **FORM: recorded OUTPUT, never assertion** -- the two-part-clearance precedent, *run it, do
1514:> > not assert it*. One `PRETABLE.out` in the round's kill-table artifacts dir (or a probe-header
1515:> > section), **written BEFORE table 1 runs so its own mtime proves the order** -- the same
1516:> > order discriminator the oracle-correction rule uses. It carries: the sibling arms compared
1517:> > and any asymmetry; each enumerated claim sentence mapped to a row or an owed sentence; the
1518:> > guard-field/body-field lists and any disagreeing-state row owed; and per row, the mutant it
1519:> > kills and the observable that differs.
1520:> >
1521:> > **Any check may discharge `N/A` WITH ITS REASON RECORDED. An unrun check and an inapplicable
1522:> > one must not render the same** -- this project's oldest rule about blanks.
1523:> >
1524:> > ### *** AND A THIRD FAILURE MODE, MEASURED 2026-08-28: THE BLOCK WAS RUN, ITS ROWS WERE
1525:> > ### PRESCRIBED, AND THEY WERE LOST WHEN THE ROW LIST WAS REWRITTEN THREE HOURS LATER. ***
1526:> >
1527:> > `#490`'s `PRETABLE.out` named two mutants at **16:05** -- a latch-bit collision and a brace
1528:> > slip -- and prescribed a row for each (`crossbit`, `latch cond`). At **18:45** a nine-seat
1529:> > panel found a DIFFERENT defect (the row set was site-asymmetric), the addition list was
1530:> > rewritten to carry the panel's findings, and **both earlier rows silently vanished.** The
1531:> > measuring seat then BUILT both mutants and both escaped the committed 13-row detector --
1532:> > two clause-(d) floor breaches that the round's own artifact had predicted and specified.
1533:> >
1534:> > **Nothing was forgotten in the sense of never known.** The knowledge sat in the file that
1535:> > exists precisely to be consulted at row-writing time, and did not survive a rewrite in a
1536:> > DIFFERENT artifact. The new list was not careless -- it correctly added everything the
1537:> > panel had just found.
1538:> >
1539:> > > **A ROW LIST REWRITTEN FROM A PANEL'S FINDINGS IS DIFFED AGAINST THE PRE-TABLE BLOCK'S
1540:> > > OWN PRESCRIBED ROWS, NOT MERELY EXTENDED WITH THE NEW ONES.** The block PRESCRIBES rows;
1541:> > > a later list that does not contain them has dropped an obligation, and the diff is the
1542:> > > only thing that says so.
1543:> >
1544:> > *This is the block's own lesson turned on the block: it exists because a recommended check
1545:> > is memory and memory does not fire. Running it once does not make its OUTPUT durable --
1546:> > the output has to be carried forward, and nothing was checking that it was.*
1547:> >
1548:> > **AND THE GUARD-FIELD CHECK COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT EITHER**, which is worth knowing before
1549:> > relying on it: it asks where the guard field and the body field DIFFER. For the latch
1550:> > collision they are the SAME field (`d->warned`). **For a shared bitfield the question is
1551:> > *"who else writes this field, and can they set my bit?"*** -- measured here as
1552:> > `grep -c 'd->warned |='` = 8 sites, **6 of them inside `DEVICE_ACCESS`**, so most bits are
1553:> > set by ordinary guest register reads and a collision would be silenced by benign traffic.
1554:>
1555:> > **THE TRIGGER WAS MET TWICE IN FOUR DAYS, and the value at each was specific rather than
1556:> > general:** at `#485` the GUARD-FIELD grep enumerates `D1..D5` mechanically, because the
1557:> > `default:` arm's `fatal()` arguments ARE the guarded body's field reads; at `#489` the
1558:> > CHANNEL line names the one shape all four escapes shared -- **and the probe header carried
1559:> > the governing sentence before three of the four were found.** Knowledge present, unconsulted
1560:> > at row-writing time. *A recommended check is memory, and this file's finding is that memory
1561:> > does not fire.*
1562:> >
1563:> > **ENFORCEMENT is existence-only and FILED, not built:** a commit adding a killtable artifacts
1564:> > dir must add `PRETABLE.out` beside it, forward-only from a cutoff. That is a tooling change
1565:> > owing two negative controls, so it ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider.
1566:> > Until it lands the mandate lives HERE, in lifecycle steps 4-5, and in `/round`.
1567:> >
1568:> > *** AND THE FIRST MANDATED APPLICATION MUST **CHECK** THE CHANNEL LINE, NOT MERELY OBEY IT.
1569:> > *** CHANNEL is one day old, and this file's freshly-minted-exemplar rule says a new rule's
1570:> > example is a HYPOTHESIS. Its claim is that a row's channel-under-its-mutant is knowable at
1571:> > WRITING time, before any table runs. One sentence in the first `PRETABLE.out` says whether
1572:> > that held.
1573:> >
1574:> > *Provenance note, because the ruling corrected my own citation: the twice-skipped rounds are
1575:> > `#485` (wdcnoirq) and `#489`, **not `#486`** -- `#486` is wdcfloodstale's non-discriminating
1576:> > gate row. The substance was intact and the number was wrong, and a wrong number in a
1577:> > mandate's provenance gets reused.*
1578:>
1579:> *** THE DIAGNOSIS THAT PROMPTED IT WAS WRONG, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART. ***
1580:> `#462` ran a 12-mutant table clean, then one capped ask-again found escapes needing five
1581:> rows -- and adding rows changed other mutants' scores, forcing a **second four-hour
1582:> table**. The adjudicator blamed obligations caught too late. **A seat then checked the
1583:> preserved logs and refuted that: both "late" rows were in the FIRST table already.** They
1584:> cost nothing. What cost four hours was that **the probe cannot score one row** -- 39
1585:> `session()` calls, no row filter -- so re-measuring five rows meant re-running everything.
1586:>
1587:> **So the checklist does NOT save the table.** Its real value is narrower and it is worth
1588:> having: **the ask-again is capped at ONE, and every hole it spends itself naming is a
1589:> query not spent on coverage debt.** A detector arriving with visible holes wastes the cap.
1590:>
1591:> | check | how | catches |
1592:> |---|---|---|
1593:> | **SIBLING PARITY** | for each arm the diff de-fatalises, compare its observable channels against its nearest sibling arm. An asymmetry is a row you owe or a sentence you owe | a de-fatalised arm with NO completion-event row while its sibling had one |
1594:> | **CLAIM -> ROW** | every ADDED comment sentence asserting an OBSERVABLE CONSEQUENCE owes a row or an explicit no-row sentence. Claim verbs (*observes, controls, pins, proves, the X control*) are EXAMPLES, not the test | a "device-signature control" claimed in prose and implemented by nothing |
1595:>
1596:> > *** THE VERB LIST WAS THE TEST UNTIL 2026-08-28, AND IT COULD NOT FIRE ON THE INCIDENT THAT
1597:> > MADE THIS CHECK MANDATORY. Caught by the adjudicating seat while ruling on the mandate. ***
1598:> >
1599:> > `#489`'s load-bearing added comment reads *"An early return would skip that event and hang the
1600:> > guest."* **It contains not one of the five verbs** -- it is a COUNTERFACTUAL-CONSEQUENCE claim
1601:> > about a hypothetical edit, and the grep form walks straight past it. Mandating the block
1602:> > unrepaired would have mandated a check that cannot fail on its own motivating case, which is
1603:> > this file's check-that-cannot-fail shape arriving inside the repair for it.
1604:> >
1605:> > **So the trigger is SEMANTIC: does this sentence assert something a row could observe?** A
1606:> > *"would &lt;consequence&gt;"* clause qualifies -- it names a channel (the event, the hang) and
1607:> > predicts a difference -- and `#489` shipped exactly that channel's row only after a panel
1608:> > found the escape three times.
1609:> | **GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD** | list fields the PREDICATE reads and fields the guarded BODY reads; where they differ, grep whether they are written at the same sites. If not, the detector owes a state where they DISAGREE | the guard's operand swapped for a field whose third state no row built |
1610:> | **CHANNEL** | for each row, name **the mutant it kills** and **the observable that differs under that mutant**. A row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and that is knowable at writing time, before any table runs | a survival oracle at a REFUSAL-EFFECT site: guarded and unguarded code both survive |
1611:>
1612:> ### *** THE CHANNEL LINE IS THE FOURTH CHECK, ADDED 2026-08-28 AFTER `#489` SHIPPED A
1613:> ### DETECTOR THREE TIMES AND A PANEL MEASURED FOUR ESCAPES OF ONE SHAPE. ***
1614:>
1615:> `#489` de-fatalised a guard, and **four separate mutants left the host ALIVE while the guard
1616:> was wrong** -- `> 408`, exempt-zero, `< 408`, and a latch-state collapse. Each was caught
1617:> only by a row reading a DECLINE channel (COND_DATA_AVAIL, the zeroed count, the fired event).
1618:> Each was predicted by a seat from the packet alone, and **each escaped a table that had
1619:> already come back CLEAN.** The round's own probe header carried the governing sentence before
1620:> three of the four were found.
1621:>
1622:> > #### *** "EACH ESCAPED A CLEAN TABLE" IS FALSE OF `> 408`.  Corrected 2026-09-06 from the
1623:> > #### retro re-check (batch 8b), verified against the artifacts before this edit. ***
1624:> >
1625:> > `regress/artifacts/gdromexitsix/killtable_20260828/M2_gt408.raw` -- TABLE 1, the 13-row instrument
1626:> > -- reads `TOCLEN_RESULT=10/13`: the `> 408` mutant was KILLED from the first table and never escaped
1627:> > anything (11/15 in table 2, 13/19 in table 4).  The clean-table escape of that shape was the EARLY
1628:> > RETURN, `break;` -> `return;`: 13/13 on the 13-row table, then `M7_return.raw` 17/19 once `bad cnt`
1629:> > and `bad event` were rowed.  Exempt-zero (`M8` 18/19), `< 408` (`M9` 18/19) and the latch collapse
1630:> > (`M10` 18/19) stand as written.  **The rule above is untouched -- a row reading a DECLINE channel is
1631:> > what caught every one of them -- and the exemplar's count is what was wrong.**  A wrong exemplar in
1632:> > the paragraph that mints a check gets reused as the check's warrant, which is why it is corrected
1633:> > here rather than left to the CHANGELOG (`#489`'s block carries the same correction).
1634:>
1635:> **THE COST WAS NOT MISSING KNOWLEDGE.  IT WAS KNOWLEDGE LIVING WHERE NOBODY CONSULTS IT AT
1636:> ROW-WRITING TIME** -- this file's trigger-versus-memory finding, again.
1637:>
1638:> #### AND THE OBVIOUS GENERALISATION IS UNBOUNDED, SO IT IS NOT THE ONE ADOPTED
1639:>
1640:> The tempting sentence is *"every VALUE and every STATE in which a guard refuses needs a
1641:> decline-channel row."*  **That licenses unbounded row growth -- the `#392` shape** -- and the
1642:> adjudication that produced this line rejected it for exactly that reason. The distinction:
1643:>
1644:> * **ROW DEMAND** -- *which* refusals need pinning -- is ALREADY bounded and is not touched:
1645:>   the floor clauses, the (d) DEPENDS-ON closure, one accept row per conditioned predicate,
1646:>   one anchor row per position claim, one ask-again. **Rows are owed PER ENUMERATED MUTANT,
1647:>   never per refusing state**; two states no enumerated mutant distinguishes collapse into one
1648:>   row. That enumeration is diff-closed and finite.
1649:> * **ROW VALIDITY** -- what `#489` actually measured -- is the CHANNEL line above, and it is a
1650:>   WRITE-TIME check on rows that already exist.
1651:>
1652:> > **A FILTER CAN REJECT OR REPAIR A ROW; IT CAN NEVER DEMAND A NEW ONE -- SO IT CANNOT GROW
1653:> > WITHOUT BOUND.**  That is the stop condition, and it is structural rather than a cap: the
1654:> > generator side is untouched.
1655:>
1656:> *Mechanising it -- a `# kills: <mutant> via <channel>` comment per row -- is FILED, not
1657:> adopted: it changes what every probe in the tree must carry, and that is an owner decision
1658:> rather than a round's.*
1659:>
1660:> **The third is the one that looked impossible and is the cheapest.** It only seemed
1661:> unreachable because it was approached from the mutant side -- *"what edit escapes?"*,
1662:> which is unbounded -- instead of the field side -- *"what states can these two fields be
1663:> in?"*, which is a grep. In `#462` that was three lines of output: `alloc_data` sets both
1664:> fields; two `free` sites clear only one.
1665:>
1666:> **DO NOT move the ask-again earlier.** It works BECAUSE a clean table redirects seats away
1667:> from shapes the author already covered; measured yield says so. And do not raise the cap
1668:> -- the cap is what stands between this and `#392`.
1669:
1670:> **(e) THE CONTRACT.** The probe header names which edits this detector is designed
1671:> to catch. *A detector that says so is falsifiable; one claiming completeness is
1672:> not* -- and a later escape becomes "outside the contract" or "a breach of it",
1673:> which are different dispositions, rather than an argument about overclaiming.
1674:> Two lines in a header.
1675:>
1676:> **IT IS A FLOOR CLAUSE, so a breach is L2 — replace IN-ROUND however late it is found.**
1677:> That is deliberate and it is the whole point: an escaped (d) mutant means the round's
1678:> claim to have "shipped a detector with the fix" is false about the fix's own repair.
1679:>
1680:> ### *** (f) THE ANCHOR RULE -- adopted 2026-08-25 after a CLEAN 12/12 detector was escaped
1681:> ### by a ONE-TERM translation that three purpose-built rows could not see BY CONSTRUCTION. ***
1682:>
1683:> **Rows that assert only PAIRWISE DISTINCTNESS, or a same-path round-trip, test the decode
1684:> against ITSELF.** They are therefore invariant under any edit that re-maps addresses
1685:> INJECTIVELY over the probed set -- translations first among them -- and they catch a re-map
1686:> only where it COLLIDES on that set.
1687:>
1688:> `#463` shipped three rows built specifically to pin an index bound: `alias`, `alias2` and
1689:> `crossarr`. All three read back `+0x10` and together assert exactly
1690:> `cell(+0x10) != cell(+0x14), cell(+0x18), cell(+0x24)`. **They never assert WHICH cell any
1691:> offset names.** So `r = (relative_addr / 4) & 3` -> `((relative_addr / 4) + 1) & 3` slid the
1692:> whole window one word, every pair stayed distinct, and the detector scored a CLEAN SWEEP --
1693:> while every `+0x?8` register moved one past a `[3]` array and a documented register's store
1694:> landed on `int asserted_13`, making the device drop a legitimately pending interrupt.
1695:>
1696:> > ### *** THE SAME RULE ON THE ACCEPT SIDE: AN ACCEPT ROW MUST USE THE WINDOW'S **LAST**
1697:> ### SLOT. Measured 2026-08-31, where a one-hex-digit edit scored a TOTAL ESCAPE. ***
1698:>
1699:> A detector for `sgireunmapped` carried an accept row per window, each writing to the
1700:> window's **BASE**. Shrinking a window bound -- `+ 0x80` -> `+ 0x40`, one hex digit --
1701:> **swept that 9-row detector CLEAN**, because moving the FAR bound is invisible from the
1702:> base by construction. The accept rows proved the window still *starts* where it did and
1703:> said nothing about where it *ends*.
1704:>
1705:> > **AN ACCEPT ROW WRITTEN AT A WINDOW'S BASE TESTS THE NEAR BOUND TWICE AND THE FAR BOUND
1706:> > NEVER.**
1707:>
1708:> > #### *** THE SECOND HALF OF THAT SENTENCE -- "put it at the LAST legal slot, WHERE BOTH
1709:> > #### BOUNDS ARE LOAD-BEARING" -- IS MEASURED **FALSE**, and it was falsified the same day
1710:> > #### it was written, by the measuring seat working on the very row it was minted from. ***
1711:> >
1712:> > **`M5`: move the NEAR bound instead.**  `>= CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A` -> `>= CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A +
1713:> > 0x40`, **one token**, against the 14-row detector whose accept rows all sit at their
1714:> > window's TOP slot: **14/14, a total escape** -- while a control row storing at the window
1715:> > BASE reds on that mutant and is serviced normally on the fix.
1716:> >
1717:> > **SO MOVING THE ROWS FROM BASE TO TOP SWAPPED THE BLINDNESS RATHER THAN REMOVING IT.**  A
1718:> > single accept row pins exactly ONE bound -- the one it is near.  There is no slot from
1719:> > which both bounds are observable, because an accept row's oracle is *this access was
1720:> > serviced*, and servicing is a single bit that one bound can explain on its own.
1721:> >
1722:> > **THE REPAIR THAT ACTUALLY WORKS, and it costs no extra sessions: ANCHOR THE ORACLE
1723:> > INSTEAD OF MOVING THE ROW.**  The store's own `debug()` already prints the destination --
1724:> > `[ d->re_linear_b[30] = 0x... ]`.  Assert **the array NAME and the INDEX**, not merely
1725:> > that the catch-all was missed.  That converts each relative row into an ANCHORED one in
1726:> > clause (f)'s sense, pins BOTH bounds from one access, and additionally catches
1727:> > TRANSLATIONS, which no base-or-top accept row can see at all.
1728:> >
1729:> > *Two rows (base AND top) is the fallback where no such channel exists.  It is strictly
1730:> > weaker: it pins the two bounds and still cannot see a re-map that keeps both.*
1731:>
1732:> It is clause (f) arriving from the other direction: (f) says a distinctness suite cannot
1733:> tell you WHERE an access lands, and this says an accept suite cannot tell you HOW FAR a
1734:> window reaches. Same blindness, opposite polarity -- and the same one-row repair.
1735:>
1736:> *Cheap reviewer check, beside the existing one:* ***"if this window got smaller, which row
1737:> reds?"*** If the answer is none, the far bound is unpinned however many accept rows exist.
1738:>
1739:> **AND WATCH FOR MIXED IDIOMS IN ONE CASCADE:** the same file bounds TLB_A and TLB_B by *the
1740:> next window's constant* and the other four by *base + size*.
1741:>
1742:> > #### *** THIS CLAUSE ONCE ADDED "a shrink edit to the first kind moves a boundary two
1743:> > #### windows share; to the second it opens a gap -- two different blast radii".  ALSO
1744:> > #### MEASURED FALSE, in the same table. ***
1745:> >
1746:> > **Editing the `<` EXPRESSION in the TLB_A arm opens a gap and does not touch TLB_B's arm
1747:> > at all.**  `M1`, `M2` and `M3` each reddened EXACTLY their own accept row -- 13/14 apiece,
1748:> > the same one-row signature as the three base+size siblings already discharged.  No shrink
1749:> > perturbed a neighbour.
1750:> >
1751:> > The shared-boundary hazard is real only for editing **`CRIME_RE_TLB_B` in `crmfbreg.h`**,
1752:> > which is a different edit on a different file.  *The idiom governs which EDITS are
1753:> > available, not how a given edit propagates -- and the original wording conflated them,
1754:> > which is what left three accept rows filed as needing "a different mutant shape" when they
1755:> > needed the ordinary one.*
1756:
1757:> **A DETECTOR CLAIMING TO PIN *WHERE* AN ACCESS LANDS MUST CARRY AT LEAST ONE ANCHORED ROW:
1758:> > an oracle tied to a SPECIFIC CELL by a channel OUTSIDE the decode under test** -- a side
1759:> > effect (a tick that zeroes the cell), an architectural reset value, or a second access path
1760:> > to the same cell. **One anchored row converts a whole distinctness suite from relative to
1761:> > absolute.**
1762:>
1763:> **THE REVIEWER CHECK, and it costs two seconds** -- it belongs beside *"does this diff delete
1764:> `exit(` from a device? then look for accept rows"*:
1765:>
1766:> > ***"If every address in this device shifted one word, which row reds?"*** If the answer is
1767:> > NONE, position is unpinned, whatever the row count.
1768:>
1769:> **This is not a new species.** It is the witness ladder's DEVICE-SIGNATURE control one level
1770:> down: RAM-vs-device needed a signature because a value-only read cannot tell them apart;
1771:> cell-vs-cell needs one for the same reason.
1772:>
1773:> Two cautions the same round measured, both worth carrying: a row that catches a re-map by
1774:> COLLISION is **layout-dependent** -- `crossarr` catches `-1` only because the probed cells
1775:> collide there, and a struct REORDER would silently unpin it.
1776:>
1777:> #### *** THE CLAUSE ABOVE ONCE ENDED "where an anchored row would not". THAT WAS MEASURED
1778:> #### FALSE 2026-08-25, AND IT WAS FALSE IN THE CARRIER, THE PROBE AND THE QUEUE FILE AT ONCE. ***
1779:>
1780:> The exemplar anchored row, `mask9 rt`, was described as *"anchored to a cell by a side effect,
1781:> **not to a neighbour by distance**"*. **It is anchored to a neighbour by distance.** The struct
1782:> declares `pending_irq[3], mask_13[3], mask_11[3], mask_9[3], int asserted_13` consecutively, so
1783:> `mask_9[3]` lands on `asserted_13` at byte 48 -- verified by `offsetof`, and the probe says so
1784:> itself two hundred lines below the clause that denies it. A struct reorder moving
1785:> `asserted_13` off that slot **can let** the `+1` store round-trip so that `mask9 rt` goes GREEN,
1786:> which is the identical failure the clause attributes only to `crossarr`. *Existential, not
1787:> universal, and the distinction matters because a wrong reason gets reused: a reorder that puts
1788:> some OTHER tick-zeroed field at byte 48 keeps the kill. What is unsafe is relying on the
1789:> arrangement, not any particular rearrangement of it.*
1790:>
1791:> > **THE CORRECT STATEMENT: anchoring makes an oracle absolute with respect to the DECODE --
1792:> > which is real, and is exactly what clause (f) buys -- but it does NOT make it independent of
1793:> > struct LAYOUT, because which cell an out-of-bounds index lands on is itself a layout fact.**
1794:>
1795:> *So the two cautions are not a contrast between a weak row and a strong one. Both rows are
1796:> layout-dependent; the anchored one is additionally decode-absolute. Stating it as a contrast
1797:> made the anchor rule sound like it bought more than it does -- in the carrier, where future
1798:> rounds apply it.*
1799:> And **translations beyond the measured ones are not covered by having measured +/-1**: a
1800:> derivation says `+2` escapes both the distinctness rows and that round's anchored row. A
1801:> contract must claim the translations it MEASURED and disclose the rest.
1802:
1803:> ### *** THE LATE-DISSENT RULE -- adopted 2026-08-25, after a panel stage fired AFTER a round
1804:> ### had committed returned 4-2 AGAINST the shipped fix. ***
1805:>
1806:> `#463` closed with its research stage at 3 of 9. An exemption was offered to the owner and
1807:> **DECLINED in favour of firing the missing seats** -- and the filled stage dissented on code
1808:> that had already shipped. There was no rule for that, so:
1809:>
1810:> > **A panel stage fired after a round closes adjudicates like any other finding. Closure
1811:> > changes the LICENSE, never the truth-value.** Three dispositions, exhaustive:
1812:> >
1813:> > 1. **WRONG RECORD -> L4, correct in place.** Closure is no shield for false prose.
1814:> > 2. **MEASURED FALSE PASS** against the shipped detector's floor, contract or obligations
1815:> >    -> L2/L3 per the existing rules, in a follow-up commit.
1816:> > 3. **EVERYTHING ELSE, design dissents included -> FILES AS A NEW QUEUE ITEM**, carrying the
1817:> >    dissent VERBATIM as provenance and ranked by severity like any filed item. The closed
1818:> >    round's record gains one line naming the dissent and where it went, **so a future reader
1819:> >    never sees false unanimity.**
1820:> >
1821:> > **Never amend shipped code under a closed round's number**, and never let "the round is
1822:> > closed" reduce a dissent to a footnote no queue item carries.
1823:>
1824:> **THE DISCRIMINATOR for whether a filed dissent earns a round is the universal one: CAN IT
1825:> STATE A MEASURABLE CONSEQUENCE?** `#463b` could -- a `+2` translation predicted to sweep the
1826:> shipped detector clean, and an added-`case` scenario that passes the de-escalation membership
1827:> test. A design dissent that can name no measurement files as documented-only.
1828:>
1829:> *** AND COUNT THE ARGUMENTS, NOT THE SEATS. *** That stage read 3-2 and then 4-2, and the
1830:> count was never the reason. One defending headline was **contradicted by the round's own L4
1831:> correction**; another's objections aimed at options the dissent had not proposed. Meanwhile
1832:> three DISSENTING seats proposed a one-line `return` that would have skipped the function's
1833:> common tail and handed a guest an untouched read buffer -- **none of them read nine lines
1834:> down**. What settled it was neither tally but asking *what the proposed line does to the
1835:> already-built mutants and rows*, which had empirical content the moment it was phrased that
1836:> way.
1837:>
1838:> **A corollary worth its own line, because it cost this round its best evidence:** a detector
1839:> can be green under EVERY candidate fix and therefore settle nothing between them. `#463`'s
1840:> thirteen rows leave the shipped fix, a bare decline, a decline-plus-latch and a latched
1841:> diagnostic all at 13/13, because every row touching the disputed offsets is SURVIVE-ONLY.
1842:> **"The gate is green" is not evidence for a disposition the gate cannot observe** -- which is
1843:> why a research panel cannot be replaced by re-running the battery.
1844:
1845:> ### *** THE SKETCH RULE -- adopted 2026-08-25, after FOUR independent readers wrote the same
1846:> ### broken line into a function none of them knew had a tail. ***
1847:>
1848:> Three panel seats and the ADJUDICATOR each proposed `if (r == 3) return;` as a defensive
1849:> decline. The function's last three lines are:
1850:>
1851:> ```c
1852:> 	if (writeflag == MEM_READ)
1853:> 		memory_writemax64(cpu, data, len, odata);
1854:> 	return 1;
1855:> ```
1856:>
1857:> An early `return` **skips that**, handing a guest read an untouched buffer -- a defect the
1858:> shipped code does not have. Four readers, nine lines, nobody looked.
1859:>
1860:> > **A PROPOSED EDIT IS A SKETCH, NOT A FIX, UNTIL IT HAS BEEN RUN AGAINST THE ENCLOSING
1861:> > FUNCTION'S REAL CONTROL FLOW -- and a brief that solicits or evaluates edits must INLINE THE
1862:> > FULL ENCLOSING FUNCTION: every exit path, and the common tail included.**
1863:>
1864:> **This is the dual of the mutant-table rule.** That one says a brief must inline the exact
1865:> EDIT, never just its label. This one says it must inline the exact CONTEXT the edit lands in.
1866:> Both failures look like seat error and are brief defects: the packet showed the guard and the
1867:> switch, the visible shape invited a per-case-return idiom, and four independent readers
1868:> produced the same wrong line. **When every reader makes the same mistake, suspect the brief.**
1869:>
1870:> Corollary, measured the same day and worth its own sentence: **a handler's RETURN VALUE is
1871:> itself a contract** (`return 1` = handled). What a `0` does to the caller was checked by
1872:> nobody in that entire exchange -- not the three seats, not the adjudicator, not me. An edit
1873:> that changes a return path owes that check.
1874:
1875:> ### *** THE THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET -- adopted 2026-08-25, after a round chose between two
1876:> ### options while the third was already shipping ONE FILE UP ITS OWN CALL PATH. ***
1877:>
1878:> `#463` de-fatalised a device and framed the choice as *keep `fatal()` unlatched* versus
1879:> *delete it*. It deleted, and recorded honestly that this cost the unconditional diagnostic.
1880:> **But `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523` -- in the file that DISPATCHES to that very device --
1881:> already bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions via `n_trunc_msgs`**, reasoning that
1882:> *"probing at the end of a device's mapped range is common and benign."* Same phenomenon, and
1883:> the fork's own answer was BOUND THE COUNT, KEEP THE MESSAGE.
1884:>
1885:> Worse: **this carrier already said so.** *"A guest-drivable `fatal()` needs a latch and not a
1886:> verbosity flag"* was standing rule before that round, and the two-membered framing never
1887:> consulted it.
1888:>
1889:> > *** THE SET HAS SIX MEMBERS, NOT THREE.  Grown twice, and BOTH TIMES BY A SEAT THAT WENT
1890:> > LOOKING IN THE TREE RATHER THAN REASONING -- which is the instruction below working. ***
1891:> >
1892:> > **1 DELETE  ·  2 DECLINE (silent)  ·  3 DECLINE + BOUNDED (LATCHED) ANNOUNCE  ·
1893:> > 4 ACCEPT  ·  5 FAULT  ·  6 BACKED ACCEPT  ·  7 IMPLEMENT**
1894:>
1895:> > ### *** GROWN A THIRD TIME, 2026-09-06, AND AGAIN BY A SEAT THAT WENT LOOKING IN THE TREE. ***
1896:> >
1897:> > * **7 IMPLEMENT** -- write the code.  **All six members above presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION**,
1898:> >   and that presumption fails for an UNIMPLEMENTED PATH.  `dev_lca.c`'s `lca_interrupt_assert` and
1899:> >   `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal("...TODO"); exit(1)` with **no guard at all**: there is no value
1900:> >   being refused and nothing to decline, accept or fault.  In the codex seat's words, *"declining an
1901:> >   interrupt leaves the machine incomplete."*  The tell that member 7 is the right one is a site with NO
1902:> >   PREDICATE -- a whole path stubbed out, rather than a guard rejecting a value.
1903:> >
1904:> > *** AND THE SAME PASS FOUND THAT **MEMBER 5 IS NOT UNIVERSALLY AVAILABLE**, which no earlier statement of
1905:> > this set said. ***  FAULT works because `memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access --
1906:> > **for MIPS and M88K.  There is no Alpha equivalent.**  So `dev_unreadable.c`, which `machine_sgi.c`
1907:> > installs ten times, does not transfer to an Alpha machine at all, and a round reaching for FAULT there owes
1908:> > Alpha exception delivery first.
1909:> >
1910:> > > **BEFORE CHOOSING A MEMBER, CHECK IT IS AVAILABLE ON THIS CPU.**  The set is a menu of dispositions, not
1911:> > > a menu of things every machine can do -- and the option-set rule's own advice (grep the fork for the
1912:> > > idiom) finds the idiom without telling you whether the machinery under it exists on your target.  That is
1913:> > > the warrant-transfer rule one level down: a precedent real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby
1914:> > > transferable.
1915:> >
1916:> > * **4 ACCEPT** -- the model ALREADY computes the right value and then discards it.  Delete
1917:> >   the kill, keep the value, NO latch: there is no event to announce, and a bounded announce
1918:> >   would ship a wrong record in product code (*"unimplemented; ignored"* about a read that
1919:> >   is implemented and is not ignored).  Found 2026-08-27 when the DC21285 datasheet showed
1920:> >   `+0x188` is IRQEnable, a documented read-only register the model answers correctly.
1921:> > * **5 FAULT** -- deliver the architectural bus error, by MODELLING THE SPACE AS UNMAPPED.
1922:> >   `src/devices/dev_unreadable.c` is a device whose entire access function is `return 0;`
1923:> >   ("returns an error for all reads/writes"), and `machine_sgi.c` installs it TEN times so
1924:> >   absent hardware faults, because the PROM and NetBSD probe those regions and RELY on the
1925:> >   failure.  **This is why *`return 0` is not a decline* and *FAULT is a member* are both
1926:> >   true: it is not a refusal, it is a positive statement that nothing is there.**
1927:> > * **6 BACKED ACCEPT** -- invent storage and accept, the `mte_reg[]`/`de_reg[]` idiom.
1928:> >   Distinct from 4: nothing computes a value, so this FABRICATES one.  Governed by the
1929:> >   ADDITION LICENCE -- a measured delta or a transferred warrant, never "cannot hurt".
1930:> >
1931:> > *(A seventh, ALIAS/mirror -- partial address decode folding a high window onto a low one,
1932:> > which real hardware often does -- was named and dismissed for want of evidence.  An
1933:> > unmapped-space arm's option set generically contains it; name it before dismissing it.)*
1934:> >
1935:> > **Before choosing, GREP THE FORK FOR AN EXISTING IDIOM -- the tree's own precedents are
1936:> > part of the design space.**  Members 4, 5 and 6 were ALL found that way and none of them
1937:> > by argument.  **And grep for the SIBLING HANDLERS' disposition in the same file**: on
1938:> > `sgireunmapped` that showed the three siblings already declined without `exit(1)` and
1939:> > pristine upstream did too, making the arm under review the file's own outlier -- upstream
1940:> > residue rather than a policy.  *That is a far stronger warrant than "it is a host kill",
1941:> > and it is one `grep` away.*
1942:>
1943:> This is the premise-audit discipline aimed at **option-space completeness** instead of at
1944:> factual premises. A brief that lists two options gets a panel arguing about two options;
1945:> nobody proposes the member that was never named. *Five seats and an adjudicator debated the
1946:> two-membered set; the seat that found the third had gone looking in the tree.*
1947:> #### *** AND THE FOUNDING STORY WAS WRONG. THE THIRD MEMBER *WAS* NAMED -- ON PASS 1, IN A
1948:> #### VERDICT LINE, BY A SEAT `panel.sh` HAD STOPPED FIRING. Discovered 2026-08-25, one day
1949:> #### after this rule was minted, by teaching a checker about that seat. ***
1950:>
1951:> The sentence above says *nobody proposes the member that was never named*. In the round this
1952:> rule was minted from, somebody did. `_scratchpad/panel_20260824_191634/ollama_kimi.txt`,
1953:> written at **19:36 on the FIRST pass**, says in its verdict line:
1954:>
1955:> > *"the latent index protection should be carried as a **point-of-use decline**, not only a
1956:> > comment."*
1957:>
1958:> **That is the SECOND member -- and an earlier version of this block called it the third,
1959:> which overcorrected the very story it was correcting.** Checked across all 70 lines of that
1960:> answer: what it proposes is `if (r > 2) break;`, described as *"decline-not-die, so if the
1961:> forbidden `case 0x0c:` ever arrives it fails safe instead of killing the host"* -- a SILENT
1962:> decline. It never proposes a bounded or latched announce; its only two mentions of `fatal()`
1963:> are to escalate the flood problem and to request a measurement about rate limiting.
1964:>
1965:> **The 17.7 KB answer was recorded nowhere.** It surfaced only when `check_seats_read.py` was
1966:> taught that `ollama_kimi.txt` maps to the kimi seat -- until then a substitute-transport
1967:> answer was invisible to the very check that exists to catch unread reviews.
1968:>
1969:> > **SO THE DIAGNOSIS IS BOTH, ONE MEMBER EACH.** Member 2 WAS proposed on pass 1 and went
1970:> > unread -- that half is an UNREAD REVIEW and the fix is to read what the seats already sent.
1971:> > Member 3, the bounded announce, was genuinely never named by anyone until a seat went
1972:> > looking in the tree -- that half is real OPTION-SPACE BLINDNESS and the founding story
1973:> > survives for the member it was actually about.
1974:>
1975:> *An earlier draft replaced one wrong diagnosis with another wrong diagnosis, and it was
1976:> caught by a seat that read all seventy lines instead of the verdict line. That is the
1977:> read-the-body rule this file already states -- **a verdict line is written against the
1978:> brief's framing and is the LEAST likely part of an answer to carry a correction** -- turned
1979:> on a correction of my own.*
1980:>
1981:> The same answer also named `asicindexcase` on pass 1 -- *"post-switch `r`-use audit before
1982:> the edit lands; absence admits converting a host kill into a silent OOB write on declined
1983:> offsets"* -- which the round later filed as a discovery.
1984:>
1985:> *Two rounds of design argument, one filed row and one carrier rule, all of which existed in
1986:> a file on disk the whole time. This is the third recorded instance of the unread-review
1987:> class and by some distance the most expensive.*
1988:>
1989:> And it pays across a whole filed queue, not one device: every remaining `exit(` de-fatalisation
1990:> faces the same choice, and `floodclass` already records guest-drivable log floods as a CLASS.
1991:>
1992:> #### *** THE RULE'S ADVICE WAS RIGHT AND ITS EXEMPLAR WAS THE TREE'S ONE OUTLIER.
1993:> #### Corrected 2026-08-25 -- and it was corrected BY FOLLOWING THE RULE. ***
1994:>
1995:> The rule says *grep the fork for the existing idiom rather than reason from first
1996:> principles*. The first round to obey it did exactly that, on `wdcflood`, and the census
1997:> came back against the rule's own example.
1998:>
1999:> **`memory_rw.c:495-523` is a bounded diagnostic, but it is the ONLY counter-style bound in
2000:> the tree, and every property that makes it a bad model was verified by reading it:**
2001:>
2002:> * it is **not a `fatal()`** -- `debugmsg_cpu(..., VERBOSITY_DEBUG, ...)`;
2003:> * it is **DOUBLE-GATED**: `ENOUGH_VERBOSITY(SUBSYS_MEMORY, VERBOSITY_DEBUG) && n_trunc_msgs < 8`,
2004:>   and because `&&` short-circuits, **the counter never increments at default verbosity**. The
2005:>   bound almost never engages, because the message is already invisible;
2006:> * `static int n_trunc_msgs` is function-local -- **GLOBAL, shared by every device and CPU**,
2007:>   not per-instance;
2008:> * it prints **no give-up notice**.
2009:>
2010:> > **THE HOUSE IDIOM IS NOT "FIRST N THEN QUIET". IT IS `N = 1`, PER DEVICE INSTANCE, PLUS AN
2011:> > EXPLICIT SUPPRESSION MARKER IN THE MESSAGE TEXT** -- roughly 34 latch sites across 11 device
2012:> > files. So member 3 should be read as **DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE**, and the counter form is
2013:> > the exception that needs a warrant, not the template.
2014:> >
2015:> > *** THE MARKER'S SPELLING WAS NAMED WRONG HERE: this clause used to say the idiom is
2016:> > `(further occurrences suppressed)`, which is the MINORITY form. Censused 2026-08-26 across
2017:> > `src/devices/*.c`, by message text: ***
2018:> >
2019:> > *** ALL THREE NUMBERS WENT STALE AND WERE RE-DERIVED 2026-08-30, after the kimi seat
2020:> > challenged the `(once)` figure in a brief that quoted this block.  It counted 24 where
2021:> > this said 9, and it was right.  The fable5 seat independently confirmed 24 and supplied
2022:> > the reason: *the count has grown past 9 because `#497` itself added six sites.* ***
2023:> >
2024:> > **THE DERIVER, so the next reader re-runs it instead of trusting a literal:**
2025:> >
2026:> >     for m in '(once)' 'further occurrences suppressed' 'reported once'; do
2027:> >         printf '%-34s %3s occ %3s files' "$m" \
2028:> >             "$(grep -o "$m" src/devices/*.c | wc -l)" \
2029:> >             "$(grep -l "$m" src/devices/*.c | wc -l)"; echo; done
2030:> >
2031:> > On 2026-08-30 that printed:
2032:> >
2033:> >     (once)                            24 occ   4 files
2034:> >     (further occurrences suppressed)   4 occ   2 files
2035:> >     (reported once)                    5 occ   3 files
2036:> >
2037:> > **THE ADVICE IS UNCHANGED AND STRENGTHENED: `(once)` is the plurality by a WIDER margin
2038:> > than this block claimed.**  Only the literals were wrong.  The growth is this project's
2039:> > own doing, which is exactly why a transcribed count in an auto-loaded file decays: the
2040:> > rounds that consult it are the rounds that change it.
2041:> >
2042:> > *(Superseded literals, kept only as the demoted record of what was measured on
2043:> > 2026-08-26: 9 / 3 / 2 = 14.  Never a current claim.)*
2044:> >
2045:> > #### *** AND ON 2026-08-31 A BRIEF OF MINE INLINED **THOSE VERY DEMOTED LITERALS** AS A
2046:> > #### SETTLED PREMISE.  Caught by the kimi seat, which measured the tree instead. ***
2047:> >
2048:> > The brief's settled-premises block gave `(once)` 9, `(further occurrences suppressed)` 3,
2049:> > `(reported once)` 2 -- the crossed-out row above, copied verbatim into a nine-seat panel
2050:> > packet.  Re-running the deriver the same day: **25 / 4 / 5**.
2051:> >
2052:> > **DEMOTING A NUMBER IN PLACE DOES NOT STOP IT BEING REUSED, BECAUSE THE REUSER IS
2053:> > SEARCHING FOR THE SUBJECT, NOT READING THE PARAGRAPH.**  A `grep` for the marker text
2054:> > lands on the demoted line as readily as on the live one -- and the demoted line is the one
2055:> > that still LOOKS like a tidy censused fact.  The live entry is a shell command, which does
2056:> > not.
2057:> >
2058:> > *So the demotion worked as a record and failed as a guard.*  The cheap defence is the one
2059:> > this file already mandates and I skipped: **a brief inlines the DERIVER'S OUTPUT, run at
2060:> > brief-writing time, never a literal from here.**  It costs one command, and it would also
2061:> > have caught the drift that makes the point -- 24 on 08-30, 25 on 08-31, because `#498`
2062:> > added a `(once)` site.  **The rounds that consult the number are the rounds that change
2063:> > it**, which is why no literal here survives contact with the week it is quoted in.
2064:> >
2065:> > *The seat's conclusion was also stronger than the brief's: `(once)` is not the plurality,
2066:> > it is the overwhelming majority.  Correcting the count strengthened the advice.*
2067:> >
2068:> > **A CAUTION FROM DERIVING IT: one of my four counting methods returned ZERO.**  A regex
2069:> > requiring `fatal(` and `(once)` on the SAME line finds nothing, because the marker sits on
2070:> > a continuation line of a wrapped string.  A single method would have produced a confident
2071:> > wrong answer in either direction; four methods disagreeing is what made the right one
2072:> > obvious.
2073:> >
2074:> > **`(once)` is the plurality and is what a new site should use.** The N=1 per-instance SHAPE
2075:> > is unaffected and unchanged -- only the words. Note the two quantities are different and the
2076:> > "roughly 34 sites" figure is probably a count of latch VARIABLES rather than of suppression
2077:> > markers; it is left standing because nobody has re-counted it, which is exactly the kind of
2078:> > unexamined number this file keeps having to correct.
2079:> >
2080:> > *Found while a seat was checking a DIFFERENT claim, which is how all four carrier
2081:> > corrections today were found. Nothing systematically re-derives these.*
2082:>
2083:> **And the fork has a recorded warrant for KEEPING `fatal()` rather than downgrading it**, at
2084:> `dev_asc.c:250-257`: under `-q` verbosity settles at `VERBOSITY_ERROR`, so a WARNING-level
2085:> `debugmsg` would be **invisible to the boot-log hygiene grep**, and an ERROR-level one would
2086:> trip `#261`'s break-on-ERROR. The chronology runs one way and is worth trusting: `#245` chose
2087:> verbosity-gating; `#265`/`#269`, `#337`, `#460` and `#461` all chose **latch + `fatal()`**.
2088:>
2089:> *`dev_wdc.c` is the tree's REFERENCE implementation -- `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:147` says its own
2090:> latch is "Modelled on `dev_wdc.c`'s `warned_badlen` (#337)". The file the rule was about to be
2091:> applied to was already the exemplar it should have cited.*
2092:>
2093:> **The general lesson is not about this idiom.** A rule minted from ONE instance carries that
2094:> instance's shape as if it were the class. The instruction survived contact and the example did
2095:> not, which is the right way round -- but it means **a freshly minted rule's exemplar is a
2096:> hypothesis, and the first round to apply the rule should be asked to CHECK it, not just obey
2097:> it.** Here that cost one census and corrected the rule in the same pass that used it.
2098:>
2099:> ### THE ADDITION LICENCE -- the discriminator against "one more line cannot hurt"
2100:>
2101:> > **Additions to product code in a hardening fork are licensed by MEASURED DELTAS or by a
2102:> > TRANSFERRED WARRANT -- never by "it cannot hurt."**
2103:>
2104:> The same round rejected three additions and each died on a measurement or a named defect:
2105:> sizing the arrays `[4]` was **measured to COST a mutant kill** (it makes `+1` escape `mask9 rt`,
2106:> because that row's kill works by a tick erasing the cell and `[4]` gives index 3 a real slot);
2107:> the `r = 0` clamp would have **defeated `crossarr`**; a disjointness assert **guarded the wrong
2108:> invariant**. The decline arrived with the opposite signature -- a measured delta (`+2`, which
2109:> escapes even `mask9 rt` today, reddens `safe 4` once the decline pins the refusal column) and a
2110:> transferred in-tree warrant. **"Cannot hurt" is not a licence; it is how dead guards accrete.**
2111:>
2112:> > #### *** THE `+2 reddens safe 4` DELTA WAS NEITHER MEASURED NOR TRUE.  Corrected 2026-09-06 from
2113:> > #### the retro re-check (batch 9), verified against the probe and the artifacts before this edit. ***
2114:> >
2115:> > `regress/asic_badaddr_probe.py`'s `safe 4` row is SURVIVE-ONLY -- `row("safe 4", "survived" if alive
2116:> > else "died", "survived", ...)` -- so it cannot redden under a decline: the guarded and the unguarded
2117:> > code both survive, which is exactly the CHANNEL-line vacuity this file names two sections up.
2118:> > Under a `+2` translation with the decline in place NO shipped row reds (`safe 4` and `safe 8` are
2119:> > survive-only; `alias`/`crossarr` read back `+0x10`, which `+2` does not move; nothing touches
2120:> > `+0x34`); only `-1` reds an existing row (`asic ok`).  And `+2` was never run: the only translation
2121:> > artifact under `regress/artifacts/asic463/` is `table5_13rows/probe_Mplus1.raw`, and the probe's own
2122:> > contract says "ONLY +/-1 IS MEASURED".  **The addition-licence rule is untouched; the decline's
2123:> > "measured delta" was a PREDICTION written in the measured column** -- the frozen-number class -- and
2124:> > the `+2` translation is an undischarged de-escalation obligation, filed as `asicplus2row`.
2125:
2126:> ### *** PROVISIONAL ADJUDICATION -- adopted 2026-08-25, MINTED BY THE ADJUDICATOR AGAINST
2127:> ### ITSELF after it issued a final-sounding ruling on 8 of 9 mandated seats. ***
2128:>
2129:> A round's research stage stood at 8/9. The ninth entry was a **HELD MARKER counted as an
2130:> answer** -- the gap-closes-itself shape -- so the adjudicator ruled as though the roster were
2131:> complete. The ninth seat then landed and **killed the ruling outright**, on a fact that
2132:> defeated its central warrant.
2133:>
2134:> > **AN ADJUDICATION NAMES ITS EVIDENCE BASIS -- "on N of M mandated seats" -- AND ANY RULING
2135:> > ISSUED SHORT OF THE ROSTER IS PROVISIONAL ON ITS FACE. The commit holds until the roster
2136:> > completes or the owner exempts.**
2137:>
2138:> This is section H's spirit applied to adjudication TIMING rather than to stages. And the
2139:> measured case is instructive about what waiting buys: three successive reversals landed
2140:> almost exactly where the position stood before the detour -- **but with the warrants
2141:> transformed**. The churn cost adjudication text and zero code, because nothing was committed
2142:> between reversals. *The hold discipline is what made the reversals free.*
2143:>
2144:> ### THE COMPANION, and it is the reason that ruling was defeatable at all
2145:>
2146:> The refuted warrant had three elements. The adjudicator checked the one it suspected
2147:> (*"is the announced event common and benign?"*), found it non-load-bearing, and shipped. The
2148:> vulnerable element was a different one (*"does the diagnostic announce something REAL?"*) --
2149:> **and the fact that defeated it was already in that round's own record, filed under another
2150:> heading four findings earlier.**
2151:>
2152:> > **BEFORE TRANSFERRING A PRECEDENT, GREP THE ROUND'S OWN FINDINGS AGAINST EACH WARRANT
2153:> > ELEMENT.** A warrant checked only against the facts you have in hand can be defeated by a
2154:> > fact you already hold under a different label.
2155:
2156:> **Above floor, measured escapes are COVERAGE DEBT: FILE them**, with the escape list recorded,
2157:> ranked by what they would admit — and ranked BELOW live defects in the same file.
2158:
2159:The floor is not invented: it is what four earlier rounds already did. `sh4bcr`'s first detector
2160:passed a mutant reinstating the very host kill its round removed; `sh4pcicexit`'s passed the
2161:original kill verbatim; `pitflip`'s rewind had zero coverage; #446's passed a **217-byte
2162:comment-only file**. All four strengthened or replaced IN-ROUND, and all four were below this
2163:floor. #447's detector is above it, checked row by row — so filing its nine escapes was right.
2164:
2165:**Reading "MEASURED FALSE PASS" as "any measured mutant escape" would reproduce #392**: the
2166:measuring seat finds escapes essentially every round and the mutation space is unbounded. The
2167:severity rule settles the ordering — #447's escapes are *hypothetical edits in uncovered product
2168:cells*, while `sh4chcr` is a **measured live host death in the shipped tree**.
2169:
2170:> ### *** THE DE-ESCALATION CLAUSE — the one genuinely new rule, and it names a hazard this
2171:> project created for itself four times. ***
2172:>
2173:> **A fatal→survive fix DELETES THE ACCIDENTAL TRIPWIRE that made guard-mask growth
2174:> self-announcing.** Before #447, adding `| TCR_UNIE` to a guard mask meant `exit(1)` on the
2175:> first legal write — unmissable. After it, the same edit means a **silently refused write** and
2176:> nothing anywhere notices.
2177:>
2178:> So: **when a fix removes a host-fatal guard, enumerate from the diff every predicate that used
2179:> to gate death, and the detector shipping with that fix must pin the ACCEPT SIDE of each** — a
2180:> row per adjacent modelled bit or value, proving legal writes still land. Those rows are part
2181:> of the FIX'S COST, not future strengthening, because the fix itself destroyed the old
2182:> (accidental) detector for that class.
2183:
2184:It is bounded (the class is exactly "edits the old `exit(1)` would have announced"), and it is
2185:checkable at review time by one question: **does this diff delete `exit(` from a device? then
2186:look for accept rows.** Deliberately NOT mechanised — the enumeration needs judgement about
2187:adjacency, and the existing "what is the smallest edit that breaks this and still passes?" seat
2188:invocation already FINDS the instances. What was missing was never detection; it was
2189:DISPOSITION.
2190:
2191:> ### *** ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY — the companion rule, adopted 2026-08-21 after #449's accept
2192:> row was MEASURED VACUOUS. ***
2193:>
2194:> An accept-side row is **not discharged by existing** — it is discharged by a **MEASURED
2195:> KILL**. For each predicate the clause enumerates from the diff, build the smallest
2196:> **realistic JOINT** guard-growth mutant — widen the predicate **and** its diagnostic/latch
2197:> operand together, the way adjacent code actually evolves — and the accept row must REDDEN
2198:> under it. A row green under its own growth mutant is vacuous whatever the chosen bit's
2199:> legality.
2200:>
2201:> Choose the pinning value so a guest store can leave it **in the predicate's OPERAND**: read
2202:> the store arm, and cite its line in the row's comment. **Write-1-to-keep, write-to-clear,
2203:> read-only and never-set-by-device bits cannot carry an accept row.**
2204:
2205:**"Reaches the predicate's operand" is the checkable notion, and "the guest can produce it" is
2206:one level too shallow** — #449's guest *did* write CMF=1 at the interface; the store arm filtered
2207:it before the predicate ever saw it. The measured kill subsumes reachability, and it catches
2208:wrong-regex, wrong-register and wrong-width vacuities in the same row for free.
2209:
2210:*** "JOINT" WAS THE FIRST MEASURED INSTANCE OF THE RULE, NOT THE RULE — restated 2026-08-22
2211:by the flagship seat after #452 measured the opposite case. *** #449's guard-only widening was
2212:measured **EQUIVALENT** (the latch argument still masked to the original bits, so nothing
2213:observable happened) and A1's kill only appears when guard and latch widen together. But at
2214:#451's SCI site the latch operand is a compile-time constant the guard cannot feed, so
2215:guard-only and joint redden the accept rows byte for byte — and guard-only ADDITIONALLY
2216:silences its own growth, printing one diagnostic where joint prints two. **There, guard-only is
2217:the HARDER mutant, and building only the joint form is the WEAKER test.**
2218:
2219:> **THE RULE, BY ITS DISCRIMINATOR:** the accept row must redden under the **least-observable
2220:> growth form that is measured NON-EQUIVALENT at that site**. Find it by the reading the rule
2221:> already mandates — read the latch/store arm and cite its line. Where the latch operand CAN
2222:> mask the guard, guard-only is equivalent and JOINT is that form. Where the latch operand is a
2223:> constant the guard cannot feed, guard-only is non-equivalent AND quieter, so it is that form.
2224:> **"Build the form the latch operand does not mask"** is the generalisation, and it is not a
2225:> new rule — it is this rule's own reason made explicit.
2226:
2227:Cost stays one mutant per de-fatalised predicate, not per row (#448 = 4 builds, #449 = 1).
2228:**Where the reading is uncertain, build BOTH** — #451 proved both directions measurable in
2229:minutes, and a spare build is cheaper than a wrong reading.
2230:
2231:> ### *** THE ASK-AGAIN, ADOPTED 2026-08-22, AND ITS CAP IS THE WHOLE SAFETY. ***
2232:>
2233:> **Once a detector's kill table is CLEAN, ask the panel once more for another mutant.**
2234:> Measured yield: 2 of the 5 rows #451 added, and 6 of the 15 in #452, came only from that
2235:> second ask — after the first table was already green. The mechanism is real: a clean table
2236:> redirects seats away from the shapes the author already thought of.
2237:>
2238:> **CAPPED AT ONE ASK-AGAIN PER DETECTOR PER ROUND.** This is not the #392 chain — #392 was
2239:> unbounded fix-of-fix passes with no stop condition; this is a single extra adversarial query
2240:> against a clean table with a measured stop: nothing found, done; something found, row added
2241:> under L1, done. **The cap IS the anti-#392 clause.** A second ask-again after an empty first
2242:> is exactly where the chain starts, and anything found past the cap is FILED, never fixed.
2243:>
2244:> > ### *** THE CAP COUNTS **ASKS**, NEVER **FINDINGS**. Adjudicated 2026-08-28, because the
2245:> > ### singular in the sentence above ("row added", "something found") invited the opposite
2246:> > ### reading and that reading NEARLY FILED A FLOOR BREACH. ***
2247:> >
2248:> > Everything ONE ask returns is disposed of INDIVIDUALLY under L1–L4 and the floor — and **a
2249:> > floor breach among the findings is L2, repaired in-round however late it is found.** A
2250:> > second ASK is where the #392 chain starts; the number of rows one ask yields is not.
2251:> >
2252:> > **The founding measurement already said so and the wording contradicted it:** this block's
2253:> > own text records *"6 of the 15 in #452 came only from that second ask."* A reading under
2254:> > which #452 should have taken one row and filed five is refuted by the paragraph that mints
2255:> > the rule.
2256:> >
2257:> > **The live cost of the misreading, from `#488`:** three escapes were found by one ask; the
2258:> > author took one and filed two, reasoning "the cap says I may take only one". `E1` was
2259:> > `if (X)` -> `if (X && Y)` on the DOMINATING predicate — the canonical clause-(d) form, i.e.
2260:> > a floor breach, which the floor says must be repaired in-round. **So one rule's cap was
2261:> > about to silently override another rule's MUST.** A reading that produces that collision is
2262:> > wrong on its face.
2263:> >
2264:> > The anti-runaway property is untouched: the bounded resource was always the adversarial
2265:> > QUERY, and what it returns is bounded by being the findings of ONE query against ONE clean
2266:> > table.
2267:
2268:> ### *** THE ORACLE-CORRECTION RULE — adopted 2026-08-22, and it is TEST-FIRST restated
2269:> for the case test-first did not cover. ***
2270:>
2271:> Sometimes the DETECTOR is the thing that is wrong: a shipped row pins the opposite of the
2272:> architecture, so correcting the code turns the row red. Re-pinning it is then necessary —
2273:> and it is also **observationally identical to fitting the test to the binary**, which is
2274:> the classic way to fake a green detector.
2275:>
2276:> **THE DISCRIMINATOR IS ORDER, AND IT IS MECHANICAL:**
2277:>
2278:> > **Re-pin the oracle FIRST. Measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new expectations.
2279:> > THEN change the code.** Same scripts, opposite colour. If you cannot show that red, you
2280:> > do not have test-first, and the re-pin collapses into laundering.
2281:>
2282:> Three further obligations, each from a seat that reached it independently:
2283:> * **PROVENANCE MUST SHOW.** The rewritten row comment quotes the source VERBATIM with
2284:>   file/line, so a future seat can re-derive it without trusting this round. The old pin's
2285:>   authority is the thing being overturned, so it cannot also be the warrant.
2286:> * **CORRECT THE FALSE RECORD IN PLACE**, never silently supersede it. A re-pinned oracle
2287:>   sitting next to an uncorrected claim of provenance is exactly what laundering looks like
2288:>   from outside.
2289:> * **A RE-PIN THAT REMOVES THE LAST FAILING ROW IS NOT FINISHED.** Ask what still faults
2290:>   after the correction. If the answer is "one row, and it observes the same value either
2291:>   way", the round owes a row on the OTHER observable channel before it may ship.
2292:>
2293:> *Provenance of the rule itself: the `sqreadtype` pass-1 panel. The brief proposed the
2294:> re-pin in the WRONG ORDER and no seat let it pass; one named the order fix, a second
2295:> supplied the provenance half, a third put it strongest — the re-pin **is** laundering
2296:> unless the new-channel row ships with it.*
2297:
2298:> ### *** A MUTANT THAT WILL NOT DIE IS A RESULT, NOT NOISE. Adopted 2026-08-23 by the
2299:> regress seat, after one round produced three of them with three DIFFERENT causes. ***
2300:>
2301:> A mutant that cannot be made to fail has **exactly three explanations**, and silently
2302:> re-aiming it picks one without measuring which:
2303:>
2304:> 1. **The mutant is mis-built.** A single edit meaning to restore a historical defect
2305:>    measured GREEN because `re.I` supplied the case-insensitivity the edit targeted --
2306:>    the defect needed two edits, and the one-edit form reported a row as defended when
2307:>    nothing defended it.
2308:> 2. **The target is dead.** A class the regex no longer needed; deleting it changed
2309:>    nothing, which is a finding about the code, not the test.
2310:> 3. **The row is mis-cut.** Four rows were rejected by a newly-added anchor BEFORE
2311:>    their own guard was reached, so four live guards looked dead. Each row was re-cut
2312:>    to start with the label so only the guard under test can reject it.
2313:>
2314:> This is not a generalisation from two cases: the carrier already treats non-dying
2315:> mutants as results twice — #449's guard-only widening measured EQUIVALENT, #451's
2316:> measured the opposite — and equivalence-disposition is this same question one level
2317:> down.
2318:>
2319:> #### *** AN ABSENCE RECORD IS TIME-INDEXED, AND THIS ONE WENT FALSE WITHIN ONE
2320:> COMMIT. ***
2321:>
2322:> Case 2 above was recorded honestly: the class was measured dead, deleted, and its
2323:> absence written down **with its warrant** ("`re.search` is unanchored, so the match
2324:> starts at the label and the TRAILING class consumes the closing `**`").
2325:>
2326:> The very next commit **anchored the label and introduced a new leading class** —
2327:> broader than the one that died — and nothing re-ran the death measurement. A regress
2328:> seat checked out the earlier revision to verify the original warrant, then measured
2329:> HEAD: dropping the new class reddens a row. The class was alive, load-bearing, and
2330:> covered by no mutant at all.
2331:>
2332:> > **THE RULE: an absence record must NAME ITS WARRANT, and any edit touching that
2333:> > warrant RE-RUNS THE DEATH MEASUREMENT.**
2334:>
2335:> Naming the warrant is what made the record falsifiable — it is the only reason the
2336:> claim could be re-tested at all. *"Deleted, absence recorded as the result" was the
2337:> right disposition; the missing half was the expiry check.*
2338:>
2339:> #### MULTI-PART MUTANTS ARE LICENSED, NOT THE DEFAULT
2340:>
2341:> The main loop proposed making multi-part the default form. **The adjudicating seat
2342:> said no**, and gave the boundary: the default stays the SMALLEST SINGLE EDIT, with
2343:> multi-part licensed in exactly two shapes:
2344:>
2345:> * **(i) verbatim reinstatement of what shipped** — floor clause (b) applied to a
2346:>   harness tool, taking however many edits the historical diff took;
2347:> * **(ii) reach-the-guard composites where EVERY added edit is first measured GREEN
2348:>   ALONE** — e.g. a guard unreachable under the shipped regex, where the other edits
2349:>   are the only road to it.
2350:>
2351:> **That second condition is what answers the unfalsifiability objection**, which the
2352:> main loop had raised against its own proposal: an unconstrained composite can be
2353:> steered until it fails for an unrelated reason, while a composite whose parts are
2354:> proven inert can only fail through the interaction being claimed.
2355:>
2356:> Bonus property worth knowing: a driver that treats MUTANT-TEXT-NOT-FOUND as a FAIL
2357:> turns every find-text into a **presence pin**, so a multi-part mutant additionally
2358:> pins the existence of each feature it edits.
2359:
2360:> ### THE FOUR REASONS THAT LICENSE AN IN-ROUND FIX OF DETECTOR MATERIAL
2361:>
2362:> Everything else is FILED. Named so no round has to re-argue it:
2363:>
2364:> * **L1 — pre-ship construction.** The row is part of the deliverable still being built. A row
2365:>   measured vacuous before commit is unfinished, not a reopening. **The stopping rule governs
2366:>   findings against SHIPPED material only.**
2367:> * **L2 — floor breach.** The shipped detector fails vacuity-floor clause (a), (b) or (c) —
2368:>   replace in-round however late it is found.
2369:> * **L3 — undischarged obligation.** The row is the sole carrier of something the round OWES
2370:>   (a de-escalation accept row; the "shipped a detector" claim itself). A vacuous sole carrier
2371:>   makes the round's record false.
2372:> * **L4 — wrong record.** Prose or comments claiming coverage a measurement refutes.
2373:
2374:This resolves an apparent inconsistency: #447's escapes were FILED while #448's and #449's were
2375:fixed in-round. #447's were measured against a **shipped** detector sitting above the floor —
2376:coverage debt. #448's and #449's were caught **inside their own construction** (L1), and #448's
2377:`return`→`break` variant additionally re-reached the removed `exit(1)`, which is L2.
2378:
2379:**A disjointness assertion over the latch classes was REJECTED, on three measured grounds** — the
2380:class bit-domains overlap pervasively today, so the general form is unsatisfiable; separation
2381:rests on the array INDICES rather than on the constants, so the assert would guard the wrong
2382:invariant; and for the one disjoint pair the values are hardware register layouts that cannot
2383:legitimately change, so it could never fire. **The real hazard is a duplicate class ID**, and its
2384:zero-cost structural fix is converting the `SH4_VAL_*` defines to a C99 enum ending in
2385:`_NCLASS`, where duplicates are impossible by construction.
2386:
2387:*** DISCHARGED -- DO NOT REDO IT. Verified 2026-08-23 by opening the file. *** The trigger
2388:was "when a tenth class arrives"; `SH4_VAL_SCICMD` was the tenth, the conversion happened,
2389:and `dev_sh4.c:124` is now `enum {` ending in `SH4_VAL_NCLASS`, with the file's own comment
2390:at `:119-122` recording that the pre-set trigger fired. An eleventh class is therefore free.
2391:This paragraph is kept for its reasoning; its instruction is complete.
2392:
2393:This applies to the whole #438 / #441 / #443 / #447 family, and #447's own probe already *stated*
2394:the hazard in its constants block ("a mask pinned only from BELOW … cannot see a guard that
2395:GREW") while shipping no row for it. **The file knew. The rows just did not exist.**
2396:
2397:**THE STOPPING RULE, and it is the one that was missing.** #392 ran SIX follow-up passes,
2398:each finding defects in the previous pass's fixes, and the chain did not converge.
2399:> A review finding gets FIXED IN THIS ROUND only if it is a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG
2400:> RECORD. Everything else gets FILED.
2401:That rule alone would have ended #392 at pass-2c instead of 2f.
2402:
2403:> ### *** THE REPAIR CHAIN -- a repair made in response to a pass 2 OWES ITS OWN PASS 2.
2404:> ### Adjudicated 2026-08-31, then CORRECTED TWICE by the adjudicating seat in the same day,
2405:> ### and both corrections came from the chain that minted it. ***
2406:>
2407:> **THE OBLIGATION FOLLOWS THE MATERIAL, NOT THE NARRATIVE.** A round that repairs a detector
2408:> in response to a panel has produced NEW floor-bound material, and "the round already had its
2409:> pass 2" is a fact about the narrative. `#499` ran four passes and **the first three each
2410:> found a real defect, two of them MEASURED CLEAN SWEEPS of a detector that had just come back
2411:> green.**
2412:>
2413:> **THE STOP CONDITION: one pass per floor-bound diff, closing on the FIRST PASS THAT FINDS
2414:> ZERO BREACHES** -- subject to the two corrections below, without which it closes early on a
2415:> live escape.
2416:>
2417:> #### CORRECTION 1 -- A ZERO NEEDS A VALIDITY CONDITION ON THE PASS, AND A DENOMINATOR
2418:>
2419:> As first written, "zero breaches" counted a pass's OUTPUT with no condition on the pass
2420:> itself. **Three briefs that day went out with the diff missing** -- a `git show | head -N`
2421:> inlined the commit message instead -- and a panel answering "no breaches" against an empty
2422:> packet would have CLOSED THE CHAIN.
2423:>
2424:> > **A closing pass must be a VALID pass: the diff actually delivered; the roster complete or
2425:> > its misses named per the seat ledger; and the zero carried WITH ITS DENOMINATOR -- which
2426:> > cells, which mutant shapes, were swept.** *"Zero found"* and *"zero looked for"* must not
2427:> > render the same, which is this project's oldest rule about blanks turned on its own stop
2428:> > condition.
2429:>
2430:> That also converts the rule's finishing incentive into a checkable artifact: **a bare zero
2431:> does not close; a zero with an enumeration does.**
2432:>
2433:> #### CORRECTION 2 -- SCOPE: EFFORT ON THE DIFF, THE FLOOR QUESTION ON THE COMPOSITE
2434:>
2435:> *"Scoped to that diff"* is ambiguous and **the dangerous reading would have closed `#499`
2436:> over a live total escape.** The breach that pass 3 found was not in that pass's two hunks --
2437:> the hunks were fine. It lived in the SHIPPED COMPOSITE detector's coverage of the ORIGINAL
2438:> fix's mutant space.
2439:>
2440:> > **Review EFFORT is scoped to the repair diff, so settled design is not re-litigated. The
2441:> > FLOOR QUESTION -- does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) enumeration -- is asked of the
2442:> > SHIPPED COMPOSITE on every pass.**
2443:>
2444:> #### THE BOUND IS IMPORTED, NOT INTRINSIC -- state the dependency or the rule overclaims
2445:>
2446:> The stop condition does not bound the chain and must not pretend to. The bound comes from
2447:> **the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration being diff-closed and finite**, and from *zero breaches*
2448:> meaning **zero SURVIVING ADJUDICATION** rather than zero raised -- otherwise one stubborn
2449:> seat blocks closure forever, against the count-arguments-not-seats rule. With those imports
2450:> the chain is infinite only if every repair is defective, in which case every pass is buying a
2451:> real defect off the ship.
2452:>
2453:> #### *** THE AMENDMENT THAT ACTUALLY ENDS CHAINS, and it is the part worth carrying. ***
2454:>
2455:> `#499`'s three breaches were **THE SAME CLASS THREE TIMES** -- a cell of one state space left
2456:> empty, repaired at exactly the address the mutant had used, leaving the symmetric cell open
2457:> for the next pass. Pass 1 found *width* unpinned; pass 2 found *component*; pass 3 found
2458:> *direction* AND *position*. **Pass-per-instance converges LINEARLY IN CELLS**, and 28 -> 34
2459:> -> 36 rows bought one cell each.
2460:>
2461:> > **WHEN TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FIND BREACHES OF ONE CLASS, THE NEXT REPAIR OWES THE CLASS
2462:> > CLOSURE: enumerate the full space from the function's own inputs, close every cell in ONE
2463:> > diff, and write into the CONTRACT which cells are covered and which are deliberately not.
2464:> > The closing pass then VERIFIES THE ENUMERATION rather than hunting cells.**
2465:>
2466:> That is the reading-seat-buys-the-class lesson wearing the repair hat. It also converts a
2467:> future escape from *"another breach"* into *"outside the contract, as stated"* -- which is
2468:> exactly what clause (e) is for, and which a cell-by-cell chain can never reach.
2469:>
2470:> *Provenance: proposed by the main loop, adjudicated by the flagship seat AGAINST ITSELF --
2471:> it was asked to be harder on a rule that favours finishing, and returned two defects and an
2472:> amendment. The measuring seat independently reached the same amendment from the other end:
2473:> "adding two more rows at the corner I just named will produce a fourth pass."*
2474:
2475:**TWO TIERS OF GATE — "green gate" was ambiguous and cost a day.** 24 commits shipped on
2476:2026-08-13 with the full battery never run once; a red battery could not have been attributed
2477:to any of them.
2478:* every commit: its own TARGETED gate (~1 min), in the same commit as the fix;
2479:* WEEKLY (owner directive 2026-08-15: small unit tests any time, the full battery holds
2480:  off to weekly -- **SUNDAYS 18:00**, scheduled task `GXemul-weekly-battery`; it was
2481:  moved there from Saturday 03:00 and this line said Saturday until 2026-08-26.
2482:  *`nightly_check.sh:45-52` had ALREADY RECORDED that a doc "still said ... Saturdays
2483:  03:00 afterwards" -- the tool noticed the drift and the carrier kept it, which is the
2484:  structure-knew-and-prose-did-not shape again*), alone,
2485:  nothing else running: the FULL battery (~45 min, and `gate_ab` carries a load-sensitive
2486:  wall-clock oracle, so it genuinely needs the machine to itself).
2487:
2488:**FIX-TO-TEST RATIO: ship the detector WITH the fix, then stop.** ~1:1 in commits, ~30-40% of
2489:effort — not the 100% that 2026-08-13 spent. More verification is NOT what prevents mistakes;
2490:today was ~100% harness work and produced more errors than a normal fixing round. What caught
2491:them was EXECUTION and ADVERSARIAL attempts, not additional checks: three rounds of tightening
2492:a regex left it evadable, and one seat asked to break it found two evasions in a single pass.
2493:**For each fix, ask one quota-free measure-seat: "what is the smallest edit that breaks this
2494:property and still passes?"** One extra invocation per round, higher yield than any ratio change.
2495:
2496:**FIVE MECHANICAL RULES, all from real 2026-08-13 errors, all near-zero cost.** These would
2497:have prevented roughly six mistakes outright:
2498:1. Write commit messages **from the diff**, never from intent (a message claimed a citation
2499:   fix that no hunk made).
2500:2. When correcting a claim, **grep for its siblings** before calling it fixed (an "un-fakeable"
2501:   claim was corrected in one place and survived in three).
2502:3. **Regenerate before grepping a generated file** — build-tree residue is not evidence about
2503:   the generator (four rounds of #88 read a stale `src/Makefile` as configure's output).
2504:4. Every reproduction must answer **"did it fail for the reason under test?"** — a guard that
2505:   says "FAILED FOR SOME OTHER REASON, do not claim it" stopped two false results.
2506:5. **Read the line before citing it** (one echo got cited four different ways across three
2507:   passes, each believing it was fixing the last).
2508:
2509:**The goal is not "no mistakes" — that is unreachable and chasing it produced the six-pass
2510:chain. The goal is NO MISTAKE SURVIVES UNCHALLENGED**, which is achievable and which the
2511:panel already delivers: every error made on 2026-08-13 was caught, usually within one pass.
2512:What failed was scope control, not correctness.
2513:
2514:## THE WITNESS LADDER — what "reproduce" means (adopted by adjudication, 2026-08-19)
2515:
2516:The old rule asked "can this MACHINE boot?"; the right question is "can this CODE PATH be
2517:executed?" — with `armbdt` as the proof (no ARM rig exists, yet gate 14 runs **358** real-decode
2518:checks across `testarm`, `barearm` and `cats` -- this said "261 ... on `testarm`" until
2519:2026-08-26; commit `6014b7e`'s own message records "PASS 358 checks (was 261)", and
2520:`gate_arm.sh:561` carried the same stale number in its own comment). But only executions that preserve the committed hardware selection and
2521:routing count. The ladder, and what each rung licenses:
2522:
2523:1. **`#include`/direct-call harness — NEVER a reproduction.** The discriminator is mechanical:
2524:   *if it still compiles and still fails after the machine description and CPU/device dispatch
2525:   are removed, leaving only a direct call, it was never a reproduction* — it is a restatement
2526:   of the source. (It measurably distorts, too: m8820x's offline repro reported 1013/1015/11
2527:   where the true numbers were 1007/1009/17, a NULL-callback stub artefact.) Such harnesses are
2528:   DETECTORS and still ship as regression rows — graded by the vacuity taxonomy (must fail on a
2529:   mutant), never by the witness clauses.
2530:2. **Machine construction** (gate 9's mechanism, **41 types / 58 subtypes**; this said
2531:   "23 types" until 2026-08-26, and `gate_asan_sweep.sh:11` keeps the 23 only as history
2532:   while `:26` reasons about "15 of the 41") — a reproduction ONLY for defects in
2533:   code construction itself executes (init-time, e.g. the macppc heap OOB #23). Never for
2534:   access-path defects: registration proves presence, not reachability.
2535:3. **Cold-debugger probe** — a real guest instruction through real address decode and real
2536:   `memory_rw`, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description. EQUAL to a boot in admissibility.
2537:   Exemplar: `regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py`.
2538:4. **Boot** — a real driver reaches the path unaided. SUPERIOR for ranking: a probe proves a
2539:   path CAN be reached; a boot proves it IS travelled (#437's SYNCHRONIZE CACHE is boot-only
2540:   knowledge).
2541:
2542:**A rung-2–4 witness is INVALID unless all of:** (i) it shows the defect symptom on the pre-fix
2543:committed HEAD and goes green post-fix with the same script — this is test-first itself, restated
2544:per-probe; (ii) **for ACCESS witnesses (rungs 3–4)** it carries a LIVENESS control (a known value
2545:returns through the same decode) AND a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control (the observed value could only
2546:have come from the device — RAM would answer differently; **the footbridge probe's first draft
2547:returned 0x0 everywhere WITH ITS RAM CONTROL GREEN**, so one control is not enough); (iii) the
2548:machine description and dispatch are committed and unmodified — a machine or `device_add`
2549:introduced IN ORDER TO reach the site is
2550:laundering, not witnessing.
2551:
2552:**Witness vs detector — the timing line:** a REPRODUCTION licenses a fix and exists BEFORE the
2553:edit. A DETECTOR defends the fix and may ship with or after it (`m8820x_sites_probe.py` shipped
2554:three commits post-fix; **its own commit title says "detector"**). Grading a detector by the
2555:witness clauses is a category error in either direction. Round prose saying "reproduced" without
2556:naming the artefact is a records defect; the ledger `witness` field is the fix.
2557:
2558:**Ranking — and the first version of this clause was WRONG, corrected 2026-08-19 by the
2559:same adjudication that wrote it.** It said *order items by the strongest witness that
2560:exists today*. **RUNG MEASURES REACHABILITY-PROOF STRENGTH, NOT HARM**, and using it to
2561:schedule promotes the benign item over the damaging one. The case that proved it: `b120r`
2562:has a rung-4 boot witness but a guest-visible symptom measured NIL **on the pair that was
2563:compared**, while `armbdt` has only a rung-3 probe and CORRUPTS GUEST DATA. So:
2564:
2565:> #### *** THE EXEMPLAR OVERSTATED ITS OWN EVIDENCE, corrected 2026-08-26 by the codex seat
2566:> #### answering `b120r`'s held stage -- and the refutation was sitting in `b120r`'s own
2567:> #### ledger row the whole time. ***
2568:>
2569:> This clause used to read *"a MEASURED guest-visible symptom of NIL (two transcripts
2570:> byte-identical)"*. The row says something narrower: `told-98` vs `told-35/11` give ORDERED
2571:> TRANSCRIPTS IDENTICAL, 0 diff lines across 97 lines each -- **but `told-0` FLIPS CONTROL
2572:> FLOW, 32 MODE SENSE commands instead of 11, including MODE SENSE(10) fallbacks and page 5,
2573:> because OpenBSD 7.7 branches on that byte.**
2574:>
2575:> **So NIL was measured for a PAIR, and restated as a property of the PARAMETER.** That is the
2576:> dropped-qualifier class again, in the file that teaches it -- and note the qualifier was never
2577:> missing from the evidence, only from the summary, which is exactly how `#464`'s "floods
2578:> identically" went wrong on the same day.
2579:>
2580:> **THE RULE IS UNCHANGED AND THE EXEMPLAR IS WEAKER.** Rung still measures reachability-proof
2581:> strength and not harm; `armbdt` still corrupts guest data and still outranks `b120r`. What
2582:> changes is that the contrast is severity-versus-*small* severity rather than
2583:> severity-versus-nothing -- so this pair no longer makes the point as sharply as it reads, and
2584:> a future round wanting a clean illustration should find a better one rather than lean on this.
2585:>
2586:> *This is the anchor rule's lesson recurring: a rule minted from ONE instance carries that
2587:> instance's shape as if it were the class, and **a freshly minted rule's exemplar is a
2588:> hypothesis**. The instruction survived contact; the example did not.*
2589:**a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE; SEVERITY and gate blind-spots order the queue.** Rung
2590:is a floor on admissibility, never a priority. Strongest witness today — boot > probe >
2591:construction **"No witness of either kind" is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked, not
2592:asserted.**
2593:
2594:> #### *** THE EXEMPLAR THAT USED TO SIT HERE WAS WRONG, corrected 2026-08-26. The RULE is
2595:> #### untouched -- it is one of the most useful lines in this file -- but `dfreq` is not an
2596:> #### instance of it. ***
2597:>
2598:> This clause read *"dfreq's was measured false by building the witness in one session."*
2599:> Checked against the row and the probe: **`dfreq`'s `witness` field is `None`.** Its closure
2600:> names *"the MIPS compare write ... converts the distance to a deadline into a frequency"*,
2601:> i.e. MIPS CP0 Compare -- while the row mentions ARM Footbridge four times and MIPS CP0 not
2602:> once. **The probe that was actually built (`footbridge_sites_probe.py:111`) execs `-E cats`,
2603:> an ARM/SA110 machine that executes no MIPS instruction and cannot reach CP0 Compare.** It
2604:> returned entirely CORRECT values, and its own cited line has since gone stale
2605:> (`dev_footbridge.c:548` is `console_putchar`; the timer mask is at `:640`).
2606:>
2607:> So a witness was built and it witnesses a DIFFERENT PATH from the one the row's closure
2608:> defines. The row is internally conflated, which is the finding -- not the rule's failure.
2609:> Found by the codex seat answering `dfreq`'s held stage; every element re-verified here
2610:> before the edit.
2611:>
2612:> ### *** AND THIS IS THE THIRD EXEMPLAR IN THIS FILE TO FAIL IN A SINGLE DAY WHILE ITS RULE
2613:> ### HELD. That ratio is itself a finding about this file. ***
2614:>
2615:> The anchor rule's `mask9 rt` ("anchored to a cell, not to a neighbour by distance" -- it is
2616:> anchored by distance); `b120r`'s "MEASURED symptom of NIL" (NIL for the pair compared, and
2617:> `told-0` flips control flow); and now `dfreq`. **Three rules, three intact instructions,
2618:> three broken examples.**
2619:>
2620:> The reason is structural rather than careless. **A rule is a claim about a CLASS and ages
2621:> well; an exemplar is a frozen claim about ONE INSTANCE, and instances move** -- code is
2622:> edited, rows are re-scoped, line numbers drift, and a row can be silently conflated with a
2623:> second defect. Nothing re-checks an example once it is written down, because it reads as
2624:> illustration rather than as an assertion.
2625:>
2626:> **So: an exemplar in this file is a DATED MEASUREMENT wearing the clothes of an
2627:> illustration.** When a round leans on one, re-derive it rather than cite it -- and if it has
2628:> decayed, correct it in place and keep the rule. All three today were caught that way, and in
2629:> all three the instruction survived contact while the example did not.
2630:
2631:**Round scope (the reopening rule):** a witness makes a site ELIGIBLE, never mandatory. A round
2632:takes ONE site; a second joins only if it is witnessed, in the SAME device and SAME semantic class
2633:under the third doctrine arm (so one fix design covers both), and covered by the SAME detector
2634:with no new oracle. **More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.**
2635:
2636:### *** TWO CORRECTIONS TO THIS LADDER, 2026-08-26, from `reprowitness`'s held stage. The
2637:### ladder failed its OWN exemplar, and its own checker had already written down the second gap. ***
2638:
2639:**(a) CLAUSE (ii) IS SCOPED TO ACCESS WITNESSES, RUNGS 3–4 — it was written as rung-2–4 and is
2640:UNSATISFIABLE AT RUNG 2.** Rung 2 admits machine construction for init-time defects and names the
2641:macppc heap OOB (#23). A construction-time OOB **performs no address decode and returns no device
2642:value**, so it cannot carry a control of the form "a known value returns through the same decode".
2643:The ladder's own exemplar could not satisfy the ladder's own clause, and had not been able to
2644:since the day both were written.
2645:
2646:> **A RUNG-2 WITNESS OWES CONSTRUCTION-SPECIFIC CONTROLS INSTEAD, and they are the same idea
2647:> aimed at a different channel:** a LIVENESS control that the construction under test actually
2648:> ran (the gate's own object count, or the machine type appearing in its list), and an
2649:> ATTRIBUTION control that the observed failure came from THIS construction rather than from the
2650:> harness or a neighbour (rebuild it alone; show the symptom follows the machine type). Same
2651:> two-control discipline; different observable, because there is no decode to route through.
2652:
2653:**(b) THERE IS A FIFTH WITNESS KIND, `harness:`, AND THIS FILE NEVER SAID SO WHILE ITS OWN
2654:CHECKER ENFORCED IT.** `check_witness.py:56` has accepted
2655:`^(boot:|probe:|construction:|harness:|none)` since its first run, and its header at `:24-28`
2656:states the finding outright: **none of the four rungs fits a defect in the TEST APPARATUS
2657:ITSELF**. `precommit_check.sh:678` repeats it. Meanwhile the prose above still said "no witness
2658:at rung 2–4 → document instead", with no exception.
2659:
2660:> **`harness:<what proved it>` is a valid witness EXACTLY WHEN THE ROUND'S SUBJECT IS THE TEST
2661:> APPARATUS** — a gate, a probe, a checker — and the proof is normally a MUTATION: the tool fails
2662:> on a mutant it should catch. **It never licenses an `#include`/direct-call witness for emulator
2663:> code**, which rung 1 rejects and which stays rejected; that boundary is what keeps this from
2664:> becoming a hole.
2665:
2666:*This is the structure-versus-prose failure INVERTED, and worth noticing in that form: the usual
2667:shape is a rule living in prose that never fires. Here the STRUCTURE knew — the checker had the
2668:category, enforced it, and documented why — and the PROSE never absorbed it. Auto-loaded text can
2669:be stale in both directions, and a tool's own header is a primary source about the tool.*
2670:
2671:**Ledger:** a row that closes with commits names its witness: `boot:<rig/gate>`, `probe:<path>`,
2672:`construction:<gate>`, `harness:<what proved it>`, or `none` (documented-only).
2673:
2674:### *** CLOSING A ROW AS **OVERTAKEN** -- THE THREE CONDITIONS. Adjudicated 2026-08-28. ***
2675:
2676:A row closed because SOMEONE ELSE'S fix removed the defect adds **no row of its own**, and that
2677:is correct rather than lazy: row demand is bounded PER ENUMERATED MUTANT, never per attribution
2678:need, and a row added so that coverage "is attributable" is the **addition licence's "cannot
2679:hurt" wearing detector clothes**. `#462` measured the real cost -- added rows perturb other
2680:mutants' scores and force a full re-table.
2681:
2682:> **But the disposition is only available on three conditions, and a copier must meet all
2683:> three:**
2684:> 1. **A DISCRIMINATING CONTROL, MEASURED AT HEAD** -- neutralise the overtaking fix and this
2685:>    row's OWN symptom figures come back.
2686:> 2. **THE CARRYING ROWS NAMED** by label and probe path, in the witness or closure field.
2687:> 3. **THE CONTROL MEASUREMENT PRESERVED AT A DURABLE PATH** -- not `_scratchpad/`.
2688:>
2689:> **A candidate that cannot meet (1) or (2) is NOT overtaken** -- it is either still live, or it
2690:> owes a carrier of its own. That sentence is the whole discriminator.
2691:
2692:**Attribution is a RECORDS property and lives in the witness field**, which is why no row is
2693:owed: `wdcoddstep`'s reads `detector:regress/diff_wdc_identify.c -- the two 'odd chunk:' rows
2694:shipped by #475 pin both halves`, and its control sits in `regress/artifacts/wdcoddstep/`.
2695:
2696:*Condition 3 is not decoration and the exemplar nearly failed it: that row's own `ev` field
2697:cited `_scratchpad/odd/out` and `_scratchpad/oddm/out`, **both since deleted**, while the durable
2698:copies had existed the whole time. The precedent-setting row was one `ls` away from being
2699:unverifiable.*
2700:
2701:### *** A SIXTH TERM, `detector:<path>` -- OWNER DECISION 2026-08-27, WITH A BOUND THAT IS
2702:### PART OF THE ADOPTION AND NOT A FOOTNOTE TO IT. ***
2703:
2704:**The gap, and it took three rounds to become visible.** `b115` (#472),
2705:`wdcringambig`/`wdcrearmzero` (#473) and `fbclearread` (#474) each shipped a REAL FIX to
2706:emulator code whose only evidence is an offline differential plus a measured kill table.
2707:**None of the five terms fitted.** `boot:`/`construction:` are false. **`probe:` is actively
2708:DANGEROUS** -- `check_witness.py`'s shape regex scores it rung-3, which that file's own header
2709:calls laundering. `harness:` is admitted only when the round's SUBJECT is the test apparatus,
2710:and all three fix emulator code. And `none` is defined as DOCUMENTED-ONLY, so on a round that
2711:shipped a fix **it asserts that no code changed.**
2712:
2713:So each round parked at `held` carrying a prose note -- **the note doing work a schema value
2714:should do.** Survivable once; at three it is a pattern, and a fourth would have copied
2715:whichever note it found.
2716:
2717:> **THE BOUND: `detector:` IS NOT A RUNG.** It records that a fix shipped with a MEASURED
2718:> DETECTOR and no guest-path witness. It ranks BELOW every access witness, and it does NOT
2719:> satisfy the reproduce-before-you-edit step: **a round using it still owes the rung-3 ATTEMPT**
2720:> the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires, and must record that the attempt was made and what
2721:> it found. The one thing it may never mean is *we did not think to ask*.
2722:
2723:It exists so the checker can tell FIXED-WITH-A-DETECTOR from DOCUMENTED-ONLY, which it could
2724:not do before -- both had to be written `none`, and that conflation is what made three honest
2725:rounds look like three rounds that shipped nothing.
2726:
2727:*The alternative put to the owner was a per-round exemption. It was declined for a reason worth
2728:keeping: an exemption is a NOTE, and a note is exactly what had already failed three times. The
2729:cost the owner accepted is real and is written here rather than glossed -- **adding a term
2730:changes what every future round may claim** -- which is why the bound above is part of the
2731:adoption.*
2732:
2733:Controlled in both directions on the day it landed: `detector:<path>` passes, a bare
2734:`detector:` with no path is REFUSED, the near-miss `detectorish:` is REFUSED, and all five
2735:pre-existing terms still pass unchanged.
2736: Forward-only from
2737:2026-08-19.
2738:
2739:## Round lifecycle, with the end-of-round parallel step
2740:
2741:1. Claim the top TaskList item (or top of OUTSTANDING_BUGS "OPEN LIST").
2742:2. Reproduce on the committed build per the WITNESS LADDER. No witness at rung
2743:   2–4 → document instead, stop.
2744:   > ### *** BEFORE RECORDING A ROUND AS RUNG-1, ASK ONE SEAT TO TRY A RUNG-3 PROBE.
2745:   > ### Owner ruling 2026-08-27, and the evidence is that nobody had asked. ***
2746:   >
2747:   > Three rounds shipped on offline differential fixtures with `witness: none`, and
2748:   > the ladder was blamed for having no term that fits. Then a seat was asked, once,
2749:   > and **built a rung-3 witness for `wdcdataoutdrain` in a SINGLE invocation** — a
2750:   > guest arming WRITE SECTORS and issuing one `sd` on unmodified `evbmips -e malta`,
2751:   > with the sector clobbered pre-fix, clean post-fix, and an accept control.
2752:   >
2753:   > **So the gap was HABIT, not a hole in the ladder**, and the ruling is deliberately
2754:   > NOT a new ladder term: admitting differentials as their own rung would legitimise
2755:   > the shortcut the ladder exists to discourage. The ladder was always fine.
2756:   >
2757:   > **The ask costs one invocation and is the cheapest thing in this file.** A seat
2758:   > that reports it cannot build one has given a real answer and the round proceeds
2759:   > as documented-only — with the ATTEMPT recorded, so `none` means "tried and could
2760:   > not" rather than "did not think to". Those are different states and must not
2761:   > render the same, which is this project's oldest rule about blanks.
2762:   >
2763:   > *It sits HERE, in the lifecycle step, rather than in the ladder — the carrier's
2764:   > own finding is that the one piece of prose which demonstrably fires is the
2765:   > premise-audit line, because it lives in the artifact present at the moment of
2766:   > decision. This is that moment.*
2767:3. Design brief → **panel pass 1** → converge (empirically where split).
2768:   > ### *** START FROM `GXEMUL-SEC/tools/BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md`.  DO NOT WRITE A BRIEF FROM
2769:   > ### MEMORY. *** Adopted 2026-08-28 by flagship adjudication.
2770:   >
2771:   > It carries the premise-audit opener, the tree-naming line, the **SETTLED PREMISES**
2772:   > section, the full-enclosing-function rule, and the three-question set — each with the
2773:   > measured incident that bought it.
2774:   >
2775:   > **THE RULING THAT CREATED IT, because the evidence is unusually clean:** the settled-
2776:   > premises block was invented on 2026-08-27 from `#488`'s loss of three seat-answers,
2777:   > **applied correctly that same evening at 22:21, and dropped at 11:55 the next morning by
2778:   > its own author in the next brief of the same campaign.**  Fourteen hours from minting to
2779:   > breaking, and nobody had forgotten it.  Two packet-fed seats then spent their entire
2780:   > answers re-deriving `fatal()` returns.
2781:   >
2782:   > That is this file's own structure-first test met exactly — *"a rule that earns its SECOND
2783:   > INSTANCE while sitting in this file has thereby proven it was mis-filed as prose"* — and
2784:   > it earned the second instance **before it was even filed**, against the person who
2785:   > proposed it.
2786:   >
2787:   > **The obligation is PER-PACKET and BROAD:** any packet-fed brief re-inlines every settled
2788:   > premise its own questions depend on, whatever pass or round settled it.  The narrow
2789:   > "pass 2 re-inlines pass 1" form was considered and **refuted by its own second instance**,
2790:   > which ran pass-1-to-pass-1 across two rounds with no pass 2 in the story.
2791:   >
2792:   > Stop condition, so it cannot become "inline everything": settled means a RECORDED
2793:   > resolution citable in a line or two with its warrant; a premise enters only if a question
2794:   > in THIS brief depends on it (test: could a seat build an "if X then A, else B" answer on
2795:   > it?); and scope is the CAMPAIGN, with cross-campaign premises escalating to the template
2796:   > skeleton — **never into `panel_ollama.py`, whose preamble stays semantic-free.**
2797:4. Implement (byte-identical in both trees for shared files); propagate to build trees.
2798:5. **Panel pass 2** on the real diff.
2799:5b. **THE ADJUDICATION PASS — the panel SHARES its findings.** *** STANDING OWNER DIRECTIVE,
2800:   2026-08-30: "always adjudicate findings and make sure the full panel shares findings." ***
2801:   Consolidate every seat's pass-2 findings into ONE numbered packet with YOUR DISPOSITION on
2802:   each, list the DISAGREEMENTS separately with both sides in their own words, and re-fire all
2803:   nine. **Template section `§F2` carries the shape; do not write it from memory.**
2804:
2805:   **Why it is a pass and not a tidy-up:** until this directive every seat answered blind to
2806:   the other eight and the main loop silently arbitrated — so a seat could not correct another
2807:   seat's error, and *a disagreement was resolved by whoever wrote it up*. That is the
2808:   seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape moved into the arbitration step, where nothing in
2809:   the harness looks for it.
2810:
2811:   **Ask the parties to adjudicate their OWN disputes, and ask a seat reviewing its own
2812:   finding to be HARDER on it** — including whether the FIX is right, not merely whether the
2813:   finding was. Those are different claims and the second does not imply the first. Same for a
2814:   ruling that ADOPTS a seat's position: that seat scrutinises it MORE, because a ruling in
2815:   your favour is the one you are least likely to test.
2816:
2817:   **The load-bearing question is "WHICH OF MY ACCEPTED DISPOSITIONS IS WRONG?"**, asked with
2818:   the count named. A round that accepted everything has either had a very good panel or has
2819:   stopped arguing, and only a seat can tell you which.
2820:6. `GXEMUL-SEC/regress/run.sh` → `REGRESS_PASS`. **Never run two harness/gate invocations
2821:   concurrently** — but *** THE REASON GIVEN HERE FOR FOUR MONTHS WAS WRONG, corrected by
2822:   measurement 2026-08-14: `selftest_mutation.sh` does NOT `rm -rf` the shared workdir. It is
2823:   `T=$LOGDIR/mutation` and `rm -rf "$T"` -- scoped to one subdirectory. *(Cited as
2824:   `:27`/`:41` until 2026-08-26; they are `:40`/`:54` today. Re-keyed to the code itself:
2825:   `grep -n 'LOGDIR/mutation' selftest_mutation.sh` resolves it at any commit.)* *** Nor do
2826:   the rig images serialise: both consumers open them read-only with `R:` and the overlay is
2827:   pid-unique. **The real serialisers are three producer/consumer orderings and a load-sensitive
2828:   oracle**, and they are worse than the wrong reason was: `gate_build.sh:64-65` WITHDRAWS
2829:   `/tmp/gxsec-gxemul` and `$RIG/gxsec-gxemul` at gate start and republishes only at `:302-305`,
2830:   while `gate_mips.sh:30` `need_exec`s it — concurrent runs make gate 4 `gate_skip` on a missing
2831:   binary, which is **silent coverage loss scored as SKIP**; `gate_hygiene.sh` grades pty
2832:   logs that gates 4 and 5 produce (the block cited as `:198-258` has moved; its own header
2833:   comment says it "READS THE RAW PTY LOGS ON PURPOSE", which is the durable key), so concurrency grades a partial log and yields **a wrong
2834:   answer, not a crash**; `gate_ab.sh`'s `rm -rf "$dir"; mkdir -p "$dir"` (cited as `:42`, now `:43` --
2835:   grep the statement, not the line) removes `/tmp/gx-pristine` and `/tmp/gx-prebatch`,
2836:   which `gate_upstream.sh:46-47` reads. A per-gate `LOGDIR` would NOT isolate them, because
2837:   the ORDERINGS above are what serialise the gates — the driver's own hardcode is gone (#422:
2838:   `drive_guest.py` honours `$LOGDIR` -- `grep -n 'honour .LOGDIR' drive_guest.py`, at
2839:   `:239` today and cited as `:207-211` until 2026-08-26 -- and `lib.sh` exports it), and it changed nothing
2840:   about them. **47 wall-clock oracles by direct census 2026-08-26** -- `while time.time() - t < ...`
2841:   loops across **27** python files in `regress/`, where this line said 33 loops in 18
2842:   files. *(An auditing seat counting slightly differently got 45; the number moves with
2843:   the regex, so what matters is that it GREW rather than the exact value -- the
2844:   concurrency warning is more strongly supported now, not less.)* Plus
2845:   `gate_asan_sweep.sh:60,62`, `gate_upstream.sh:74`, and `run_emu`), so converting one gate does
2846:   not license concurrency anywhere.
2847:7. Docs (CHANGELOG round block, REVIEW_FINDINGS, OUTSTANDING_BUGS), commit, push.
2848:8. **End-of-round triage — always, before calling the task done:**
2849:   a. Harvest every seat's residuals/dissents into OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md — after-pass
2850:      findings have repeatedly become the next rounds (#340's after-panel found three).
2851:   b. Sync the harness TaskList: mark done, TaskCreate anything new, re-rank.
2852:   c. **Start the parallel work:** while gates run (~45 min for the full battery), prep
2853:      the NEXT item — reproduction probe, brief, panel pass 1. Reading, brief-writing and
2854:      panel seats parallelize freely; the SERIAL resources are the build trees, the rigs
2855:      and the gate workdir (single writer, one gate run at a time).
2856:9. Convene the panel on the next item as soon as its brief exists — the panel is never
2857:   idle while a build or gate runs.
