2864:
2865:*** AND `dc21285.pdf` / `dc21285.txt` (Intel 278115-001, the DC21285 'Footbridge' core logic
2866:controller) HAVE BEEN PRESENT SINCE 2026-08-15 AND THIS LIST DID NOT SAY SO UNTIL 2026-08-27. ***
2867:
2868:It was found by a seat that went looking for it rather than by this list, and it immediately
2869:settled a round that was about to transfer the wrong warrant: the `+0x188` read arm in
2870:`dev_footbridge.c` was being treated as an unimplemented register needing a decline, and Table
2871:7-10 (`dc21285.txt:6826`) plus the prose at `:6911-6915` say it is **IRQEnable, a documented
2872:read-only register** -- *"The value of this register can only be changed by writing to the
2873:EnableSet and EnableClear registers"*, reset value 0. The model already returns the right value
2874:and then calls `exit(1)` on it.
2875:
2876:**That produced a FOURTH member of the de-fatalisation option set, which the three-membered rule
2877:above does not contain: ACCEPT.** delete / decline / decline+bounded-announce all presume the
2878:access is unserviceable. Where the model already computes the correct answer and then discards
2879:it, the disposition is to delete the kill and KEEP THE VALUE -- no latch, no message, because
2880:there is no event to announce. *A bounded-announce here would ship a wrong record in product
2881:code: printing "unimplemented; ignored" about a read that is implemented and is not ignored.*
2882:
2883:**The general lesson is about this list, not about footbridge: an absent-source note is
2884:load-bearing and this one was wrong for twelve days.** The same failure is already recorded
2885:above for the SH manual, where 'there is no SH documentation' was load-bearing for four rounds.
2886:**Before reasoning from first principles about a device, `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` -- do not trust
2887:this paragraph to be complete.**
