2987:Renesas SH7750/SH7750S/SH7750R Hardware Manual Rev.7.00, REJ09B0366-0700, 1074 pages) and
2988:`_scratchpad/sh4_programming.txt` (661 KB, from `sh4_programming.pdf`). Extracted with
2989:git-bash `/mingw64/bin/pdftotext -layout` — the same tool that made `ddi0100i.txt`; note
2990:WSL Gentoo has NO poppler, so run the extractor from git-bash.
2991:
2992:**The old note said there was none, and that note was load-bearing for four SH rounds.**
2993:It is kept below in substance because its CAUTION still holds even though its FACT does
2994:not: cite only what you have READ in these files. `#357`/`#362`/`#365` shipped DDI 0100I
2995:page numbers that came from panel LLM output rather than a local manual, and the same
2996:temptation exists here — a web SEARCH RESULT summarising a manual is not the manual, and
2997:on 2026-08-22 a search summary and the real page agreed only by luck.
2998:
2999:**WHAT IT SETTLED IMMEDIATELY** (`#452`, and it went AGAINST the main loop's reasoning):
3000:the loop had argued that `dev_sh4.c`'s `aa` handlers ignoring address bit 23 while the
3001:`da1` handlers treat it as selecting a second array meant "one of those is wrong". The
3002:manual says both are right — P4 gives `H'F200 0000`–`H'F2FF FFFF` to the instruction TLB
3003:ADDRESS array as ONE undivided 16 MB region, while `H'F300 0000`–`H'F3FF FFFF` holds
3004:instruction TLB data arrays **1 AND 2**, split at `H'F380 0000`. Same for `H'F600 0000`
3005:(UTLB address, undivided) versus `H'F700 0000` (UTLB data 1 and 2, split). §3.7.3 also
3006:confirms the entry is "selected by bits [9:8]", matching `SH4_ITLB_E_MASK`, and that array
3007:2 holds SA and TC — **different fields from array 1**, which is why `#452` declines those
3008:accesses rather than aliasing them onto array 1.
3009:
3010:*** THE EXTRACTED TABLES STAGGER NAMES AGAINST VALUES, AND THE STAGGER READS AS A
3011:CONTRADICTION. Decode it against a block the tree ALREADY PINS -- do not "correct" code
3012:from a raw table row. (2026-08-23, #458.) ***
3013:
3014:`pdftotext -layout` renders a vertically-centered table cell on a different line from its
3015:row's value. Table 19.4 comes out as `RTC ATI H'480` / `H'4A0` / `PRI H'4C0` / `CUI`,
3016:which reads as *PRI = H'4C0* and would have condemned three correct constants.
3017:
3018:**The decoder is the SCI block four rows below it**, because `sh4_exception.h:93-96`
3019:already pins SCI ERI/RXI/TXI/TEI at `0x4e0/0x500/0x520/0x540` in shipped, working code.
3020:That extraction shows `SCI ERI H'4E0` / `H'500` / `RXI H'520` / `H'540` / `TXI` / `TEI` --
3021:which reconciles with the header ONLY if names and values pair **strictly in order down
3022:each column**, the printed adjacency being an artefact. Apply that to RTC and ATI/PRI/CUI
3023:are `0x480/0x4a0/0x4c0`, which is what the code says.
3024:
3025:**The general rule: before citing a value from an extracted table, find a row in the SAME
3026:table whose answer this tree independently knows, and use it to calibrate the layout.** A
3027:second, unstaggered table elsewhere in the manual (here `:32655`, DMTE0/DMAE) is the other
3028:confirmation and is worth grepping for -- these manuals list most codes twice.
3029:
3030:> ### *** ONE CALIBRATION POINT IS NOT ENOUGH, AND THE RULE ABOVE WOULD HAVE CERTIFIED A
3031:> ### TABLE THAT WAS WRONG FROM ITS FOURTH ROW DOWN. Measured 2026-08-26 on `sh4tlbda2`. ***
3032:>
3033:> Table 19.4's stagger was UNIFORM -- every name one row off from its value -- and that is
3034:> the only shape this rule was minted from. **Figure 3.4 at
3035:> `_scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt:9014-9029` is NOT uniform: it has a MISSING NAME CELL,
3036:> so the stagger BEGINS PARTWAY DOWN.**
3037:>
3038:> ```
3039:> 9017| H'F000 0000    Instruction cache address array     <- correct
3040:> 9018| H'F100 0000      Instruction cache data array      <- correct
3041:> 9019| H'F200 0000     Instruction TLB address array      <- correct
3042:> 9020| H'F300 0000                                        <- THE GAP: address, NO name
3043:> 9021| H'F400 0000  Instruction TLB data arrays 1 and 2   <- really F300 0000
3044:> 9025| H'F800 0000    Unified TLB data arrays 1 and 2     <- really F700 0000
3045:> ```
3046:>
3047:> **The first three rows pair CORRECTLY.** So a calibration point taken from above the gap
3048:> reports the table as sound, and everything below it is wrong by one. A reader trusting the
3049:> figure puts ITLB data at `F4000000` and UTLB data at `F8000000` -- and **`F8000000` is
3050:> RESERVED**; the prose jumps `F7000000` straight to `FF000000`. *The figure would have
3051:> condemned both shipped constants, and the calibration rule as written would have blessed
3052:> the figure.*
3053:>
3054:> > **THE REFINEMENT: calibrate on a row BELOW the value you are about to cite, or on TWO
3055:> > rows STRADDLING it. A single calibration point is only valid for a UNIFORM stagger, and
3056:> > you cannot tell which kind you have from one point.**
3057:>
3058:> **And prefer the PROSE.** The unstaggered section paragraphs at `:9052-9065` and the
3059:> per-array section heads gave the right answer at every one of the four windows this round
3060:> needed. Extracted FIGURES are the hazard; extracted PROSE is not.
3061:>
3062:> *This is the exemplar problem again, one level down: the rule was right about the class and
3063:> its single instance carried a property -- uniformity -- that the class does not have.*
3064:
3065:**AND THE TOOL YOU CHECK WITH DECIDES WHETHER YOU CATCH IT.** `sed -n 'A,Bp'` prints the
3066:lines WITHOUT their numbers, so the number has to be counted by hand off the top of the
3067:range -- and on 2026-08-23 that produced a citation two lines off in a comment that had
3068:just been written to FIX a citation error. **Use `grep -n` or
3069:`awk 'NR>=A && NR<=B {print NR": "$0}'`**, which carry the number with the line. Three of
3070:that round's four wrong citations were found by a seat re-deriving them with `grep -n`.
3071:
3072:*** AND A PATTERN THAT MATCHED NOTHING REPORTS ZERO FOR EVERYTHING. Twice in one
3073:session, 2026-08-24, in OPPOSITE directions -- and both looked like results. ***
3074:
3075:* An **unanchored** `grep FAIL` over probe output matched the word FAILED inside a row's
3076:  own DESCRIPTION, on a GREEN run -- **a phantom failure**. The tell was that the row
3077:  printed `survived` against a wanted `survived`.
3078:* An **`awk` range whose start pattern did not match** returned 0 for every query, and the
3079:  0 was read as "the summary never mentions this" -- **a phantom absence**, which then had
3080:  a whole narrative built on it before one grep at the right anchor refuted it.
3081:* *** A FIFTH, 2026-08-26, AND IT GETS WORSE AS THIS FILE GETS MORE HONEST. *** A grep
3082:  built to find STALE CLAIMS reported a defect that was really **an honest correction
3083:  quoting the text it refutes** -- the exact form this project mandates. It cannot tell an
3084:  ASSERTION from a QUOTATION OF ONE.
3085:  **The working form: search BACKWARD from the hit for a correction signal** (`said the
3086:  opposite`, `WAS FALSE`, `Corrected 20..`, `used to read`) before scoring it stale. With
3087:  that discriminator, seven audit items resolved to six gone, one quoted-by-its-correction,
3088:  **zero genuinely stale**; without it, one honest correction read as a live defect.
3089:  *The trend matters more than the instance: every correction added here quotes what it
3090:  refutes, so a naive stale-text grep gets steadily MORE wrong as the file improves.*
3091:
3092:  > *** AND THE SAME COLLISION HAS A SECOND, WORSE FORM: A QUOTED MARKER RE-ARMS THE TOOL
3093:  > THAT READS IT. Measured 2026-08-28, on a defect I created while fixing another. ***
3094:  >
3095:  > `gen_codex_wall.py` keys on the literal text `[HELD AWAITING SEAT: <seat>]`. A stale held
3096:  > marker was cleared -- and the clearing note **preserved the question verbatim**, exactly as
3097:  > the quote-what-you-refute rule requires. **The quote contained the marker, so the generator
3098:  > still counted the stage as held**, and the "cleared" edit changed nothing. The count sat at
3099:  > 27 through two regenerations and I read it as a stable number rather than a stuck one.
3100:  >
3101:  > **The grep case makes a READER wrong; this one makes a TOOL wrong**, silently and durably,
3102:  > because the record now *says* the thing was answered while the machinery keeps reporting it
3103:  > owed. That is the gap-closes-itself shape with the two halves disagreeing.
3104:  >
3105:  > > **QUOTING A MACHINE-READ MARKER REQUIRES DEFANGING IT.** Keep it human-legible and break
3106:  > > the pattern -- `(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT fable5 -- defanged)`. The rule generalises past this
3107:  > > one generator: **before quoting any string a tool matches on, ask what that tool will do
3108:  > > with your copy of it.**
3109:  >
3110:  > *Bounded, and the hunt is recorded: all 263 rows were scanned for answered entries still
3111:  > carrying a live marker. **Exactly one, the one made here.** The class is real and its
3112:  > population today is zero.*
3113:  >
3114:  > > ### *** THAT LAST SENTENCE WAS MEASURED **FALSE** ON 2026-08-29: THE POPULATION WAS
3115:  > > ### **TWENTY**, ACROSS SEVENTEEN ROWS, THE OLDEST NINE DAYS STALE. ***
3116:  > >
3117:  > > **The hunt was right about the class and wrong about its size, and the reason is the
3118:  > > CRITERION.**  Looking for *answered entries still carrying a live marker* asks whether
3119:  > > the entry HOLDING the marker was answered.  That finds one.  The question that finds
3120:  > > twenty is different and is the one that matters to the tools:
3121:  > >
3122:  > > > *** SUPERSEDED THE DAY AFTER IT WAS WRITTEN, AND THE CARRIER KEPT THE OLD WORDING
3123:  > > > FOR ONE MORE DAY AND SIX MORE MIS-CLEARS.  Corrected 2026-08-30. ***
3124:  > > >
3125:  > > > **THE RULE: does the marker name a seat that has answered in THE SAME STAGE, at a
3126:  > > > STRICTLY LATER date?**  Mechanically: marker names seat S in phase P at date D, and S
3127:  > > > has an entry on the same row IN PHASE P, dated STRICTLY AFTER D, of real length, that
3128:  > > > is not itself a marker.
3129:  > > >
3130:  > > > **BOTH QUALIFIERS WERE PAID FOR IN BLOOD, AND NEITHER WAS IN THE ORIGINAL WORDING:**
3131:  > > >
3132:  > > > * *ANYWHERE ON THIS ROW* let a RESEARCH answer discharge a REGRESS hold.  `regress` is
3133:  > > >   FABLE-ONLY by standing directive, so cross-phase credit is wrong there by
3134:  > > >   construction.  **This was diagnosed and corrected on 2026-08-29 IN THE LEDGER** --
3135:  > > >   `wdccdbmidxfer` entry [28] says in its own words *"My second sweep used same-stage
3136:  > > >   matching and did not repeat it"* -- and THIS FILE was never updated, so the next
3137:  > > >   round read the superseded wording here and repeated the documented failure at SIX
3138:  > > >   TIMES the scale.
3139:  > > > * *a LATER entry* was implemented as `>=`.  All six 2026-08-30 mis-clears were
3140:  > > >   discharged by SAME-DATE siblings written by the very batch that created the marker
3141:  > > >   to record the stage as NOT done.  A strictly-later test keeps all six.
3142:  > > >
3143:  > > > **AND THE FAILURE IS WORSE THAN INVISIBILITY.**  `check_stage_panels.py` keys on the
3144:  > > > live literal, so a wrongly-defanged `seat=fable5, phase=regress` entry stops being a
3145:  > > > marker and falls through into the SEAT TALLY as a regress ANSWER.  Defanging does not
3146:  > > > merely hide an obligation; it MANUFACTURES a seat answer that never happened -- the
3147:  > > > seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape, inverted.
3148:  > > >
3149:  > > > *This is this file's own structure-first thesis, demonstrated against itself: the
3150:  > > > correction lived in the LEDGER, where it was written by the person who found it, and
3151:  > > > the auto-loaded carrier kept the superseded verdict.  The file already says "when a
3152:  > > > rule is superseded, delete its conclusion" -- and then did not.*
3153:  > >
3154:  > > **MEASURED COST, and it is not cosmetic: `gen_codex_wall.py` reported 27 HELD STAGES
3155:  > > and 15 of them were PHANTOM.**  Defanging the twenty took it to 12.  `check_stage_panels`
3156:  > > was telling the operator `wdccdbmidxfer` awaited *fable5, opus5* when fable5 had answered
3157:  > > that morning.  THREE tools read this literal -- `gen_codex_wall.py`,
3158:  > > `check_fable_queue.py`, `check_stage_panels.py` -- so a stale marker misleads all three.
3159:  > >
3160:  > > *And I read the stuck 27 as a STABLE number across two regenerations before asking why
3161:  > > it never moved.  A count that does not change is not thereby correct.*
3162:  > >
3163:  > > **Five markers were deliberately KEPT** -- `ieeeupgrade`, `pitclobber`, `proberowsubset`,
3164:  > > `probetimebased`, and `wdccdbmidxfer`/opus5 -- because those seats genuinely have not
3165:  > > answered.  The `wdccdbmidxfer` row is the discriminator working: its fable5 marker went
3166:  > > and its opus5 marker stayed, on the same row, in the same pass.
3167:  >
3168:  > *Two of the three greps in this same repair also silently matched nothing --
3169:  > `"gdromwarnbits ("` (the list renders the id in BACKTICKS) and an `entries`-vs-`stages`
3170:  > field-name error that reported a volatile-path defect as absent when the adjudicating seat
3171:  > had correctly found it. **Both were caught only because a seat's answer disagreed with my
3172:  > check and I opened the artifact instead of keeping the check that agreed with me.***
3173:
3174:* *** A SIXTH, 2026-08-26, AND IT IS THE FIRST WHERE TWO APPARATUS ERRORS STACKED INTO A
3175:  CONVINCING FALSE RESULT. *** A 30-minute gate was launched as
3176:  `timeout 1800 bash gate_sh_rounding.sh | grep -E ...`. Both halves were wrong and they
3177:  covered for each other:
3178:  * `timeout` killed the gate at exactly 30 minutes -- the last section finished at
3179:    `18:01:38` and the run died at `18:02`, **before reaching the section under test**;
3180:  * the **`| grep` made the reported exit status GREP's `0`**, not `timeout`'s `124`, so a
3181:    KILLED gate presented as a CLEAN COMPLETION.
3182:  The conclusion drawn was "my new gate section never ran, something is wrong with it." The
3183:  section was innocent and untouched by the failure.
3184:  > **NEVER PIPE A LONG-RUNNING GATE THROUGH A FILTER WHEN YOU NEED ITS EXIT STATUS**, and
3185:  > size the timeout from a measured run rather than a guess. `gate_sh_rounding.sh:615`
3186:  > already warns about the buffering half of this, one layer down: *"-u IS LOAD-BEARING ...
3187:  > with stdout redirected to a file it is block buffered, and a killed run then loses every
3188:  > row and looks like a run that produced nothing."*
3189:  **And the diagnosis itself produced two more phantoms before landing**, both caught only by
3190:  asking for a denominator: a `grep` for `gate_skip` that "found" `:618` -- an artifact of a
3191:  broken `awk` in the same pipeline, not a match -- and a log listing rendered with
3192:  `%H:%M:%S`, which **drops the date**, so logs from different days interleaved and read as
3193:  one chronological run. A story about an upstream skip was nearly built on each.
3194:
3195:> **CHECK THAT THE APPARATUS SELECTED SOMETHING BEFORE READING WHAT IT SAYS.** A range, a
3196:> regex or a filter that selects the empty set produces a clean, confident, wrong answer --
3197:> and unlike a crash it looks exactly like a measurement.
3198:
3199:> ### *** THREE MORE, ALL ONE SHAPE: A FAILURE LAUNDERED INTO A CLEAN COMPLETION.
3200:> ### 2026-08-28, two from a measuring seat and one of mine. ***
3201:>
3202:> * **A BUILD-AND-MEASURE SCRIPT MUST NOT END IN A COMPARISON.** A runner ended in `diff`,
3203:>   which exits 1 on differences; under `set -e` that killed the script **before its two
3204:>   probe runs**, and the wrapper still reported `rc=0`. No measurement happened and nothing
3205:>   said so.
3206:> * **A WINDOWS PATH PASSED TO `wsl -- bash` FAILS WHILE `wsl` ITSELF EXITS 0.** The error is
3207:>   `No such file or directory`; the exit status is success. (This is why the invocation rule
3208:>   is `MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 wsl -d Gentoo -- bash /mnt/c/...` -- the `/mnt/c` form, every time.)
3209:> * **PRESERVING A LOG FROM A STILL-RUNNING JOB CAPTURES A TRUNCATION, AND THE COPY LOOKS
3210:>   COMPLETE.** I copied a seat's `table2_targeted.txt` at 1402 bytes; the finished file is
3211:>   1529 ending `TARGETED_DONE`. The copy stopped at `SELECTED 1 of 8: S1 unkcmd` -- **so the
3212:>   last mutant read as a session that started and produced nothing, which is indistinguishable
3213:>   from a hang.** It was already staged for commit when the seat that wrote it noticed.
3214:>
3215:> > **THE RULE, and it costs one line: A PRESERVED LOG IS VERIFIED BY ITS COMPLETION SENTINEL,
3216:> > NOT BY ITS EXISTENCE.** Every probe in this tree emits one (`TPROBE2_DONE`, `TARGETED_DONE`,
3217:> > `FREEZE_DONE`, `REGRESS_PASS`). `tail -1` before citing, and re-copy if it is absent.
3218:>
3219:> *The volatile-path rule says copy the log before the record cites it. This is its missing
3220:> half: copy it AFTER the job finishes, or the durable artifact preserves a lie. The first rule
3221:> pushes you to copy EARLY and the second says not too early -- which is why the sentinel, not
3222:> the timing, is the test.*
3223:
3224:**The cheap defence is a DENOMINATOR.** Print how many lines the range selected, or how
3225:many rows the grep considered, next to the answer. `0 of 0` and `0 of 41` are different
3226:claims and only one of them is evidence. This is the same discipline as *a score claim
3227:names its log*, one level down: the log makes the number checkable, the denominator makes
3228:the SEARCH checkable.
3229:
3230:**ITS LIMIT, MEASURED 2026-08-25: A DENOMINATOR VALIDATES THE ARTIFACT, NOT THE PATTERN'S
3231:FITNESS FOR ITS FORMAT.** `grep -cE '^[+-][^+-]' nullfix.diff` returned **0** on a file
3232:holding exactly one added line -- in `diff` NORMAL format (`610a611` then `> ...`), not
3233:unified. The count was `0 of 2`, a nonzero denominator that looks like a real measurement
3234:of a real file. So `0 of 41` is NOT always evidence, and the sentence above overstates it:
3235:**the denominator catches selecting nothing FROM NOTHING, never selecting nothing from
3236:SOMETHING IN AN UNEXPECTED SHAPE.**
3237:
3238:> **WHEN A COUNT IS SURPRISING, OPEN THE ARTIFACT BEFORE BELIEVING THE COUNT.** "Zero
3239:> changed lines in a diff that exists" is a contradiction, not a result — and a
3240:> contradiction is the cheapest signal there is.
3241:
3242:*** AND A FOURTH, WITH THE OPPOSITE SHAPE: A CORRECT READING NEARLY DISCARDED BECAUSE A
3243:SECOND, BROKEN CHECK DISAGREED WITH IT. ***  `tasklist | grep` reported three panel CLI
3244:processes alive. `tasklist /v | grep` -- the follow-up meant to identify them -- reported
3245:none, because `/v` changes the column layout. **The three were real; the SECOND check was
3246:the broken one**, and I had already written "false positive" before looking at what
3247:matched.
3248:
3249:> **When two checks disagree, find out WHICH ONE IS BROKEN. Do not keep the one that
3250:> agrees with what you expected.** The first three incidents invented a failure or an
3251:> absence; this one nearly destroyed a true positive, and the disagreement itself was the
3252:> signal.
3253:
3254:*Both incidents were mine, in tooling I wrote that day, while applying rules about careful
3255:measurement. Neither was caught by a gate -- one by an impossible-looking row, one by a
3256:follow-up grep that happened to use a different anchor.*
3257:
3258:*** THE GENERAL RULE, after THREE incidents in one session (2026-08-23): ONE BACKSLASH LEVEL IS CONSUMED on the Bash -> heredoc -> Python path, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. ***
3259:
3260:Measured, same session, opposite failures:
3261:* `'...\\\n'` intended as backslash+newline produced the two characters `\n` in a
3262:  shell word list -- a bogus `for` word, and a RED GATE.
3263:* `'...\\n"'` intended as the two characters `\n` inside a C string literal produced a
3264:  REAL NEWLINE, splitting the literal and breaking the build.
3265:
3266:So do not reason about the level count -- **use `chr(92)` for any backslash that must
3267:survive into the file, and build the line by concatenation.** `BS = chr(92)` at the top
3268:of the script costs nothing and is immune in both directions. The same applies to shell
3269:regex arguments: `grep -n '\\n'` silently degrades to matching the letter `n` (it did,
3270:twice, once producing a false "the ledger is ascii-escaped" reading) -- use `grep -nF`.
3271:
3272:*** DO NOT BUILD A BACKSLASH-CONTINUED SHELL LINE THROUGH A NESTED HEREDOC. It mangled
3273:TWICE in one session (2026-08-23), and the second one shipped a RED GATE. ***
3274:
3275:Writing `'... "ipra rtc" ' + '\
3276:' + '  "scif eri" ...'` from inside
3277:`python - <<'PYEOF'` produced the two characters `
3278:` in the file instead of a backslash
3279:followed by a newline. In a `for v in ...` word list that adds a **bogus word `n`**, so the
3280:gate ran `check "  n"` against a row that does not exist and reported
3281:`FAIL n got=0 want=1` -- **1 of 111**.
3282:
3283:**Two lessons, and the second one is the reassuring half:**
3284:* **Write the word list on ONE line**, or edit line-by-line with `chr(92)` for the
3285:  backslash. Never assemble a continuation inside nested quoting.
3286:* **THE GATE CAUGHT IT, LOUDLY AND CORRECTLY.** A name list that gains a bogus entry fails
3287:  rather than silently skipping a real row -- the opposite of the padded-column trap, where
3288:  a too-long name makes a check UNSATISFIABLE and the row quietly stops being tested. Both
3289:  failure modes are real; this one is the safe direction, and it is safe by design.
3290:
3291:*** AND A COUNT CHECK CANNOT SEE THE LIST LANDING IN THE WRONG SECTION. Measured
3292:2026-08-23, and MY OWN VERIFICATION PASSED ON IT. ***
3293:
3294:A 32-name row list was written into `gate_sh_rounding.sh` with
3295:`re.search(r'^    for v in ("live".*?); do$', ...)` — and **every section's list
3296:starts with `"live" "devsig"`**, so it matched the FIRST one, which was the #459
3297:pclock section. The gdrom names were written over the pclock names, the real gdrom
3298:list was left at its old 27, and the pclock gate would have looked for 32 gdrom rows
3299:in `$PCLOG`. **A red gate, and a destroyed list.**
3300:
3301:**The cross-check I ran to confirm the edit reported `VERDICT: MATCH`** — because it
3302:used the same first-match regex, so it compared the list I had just written against
3303:the probe I had written it from. *A verification that shares the defect of the thing
3304:it verifies always passes.*
3305:
3306:**THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE LOG VARIABLE ON THE NEXT LINE, and it is the only thing
3307:that identifies which section a row list belongs to:**
3308:
3309:```
3310:    for v in "live" "devsig" ... ; do
3311:        n=$(count "$GDDLOG" "^$v  .*ok$")     <-- THIS names the section
3312:```
3313:
3314:So: **anchor on `count "$XXLOG"`, never on the list's own first names**, and after
3315:editing, diff EVERY list in the file against `git show HEAD:` — expect exactly one to
3316:change. That check found it in seconds; the count check never could.
3317:
3318:*Related but distinct from the padded-column and bogus-word traps below: those make a
3319:row silently stop being tested. This one tests the right rows against the wrong log.*
3320:
3321:**Cheap check before running a 15-minute gate:** paste the `for v in ...` list into
3322:`bash -c '... echo "[$v]"; done' | nl` and count. It takes two seconds and it is the whole
3323:test.
3324:
3325:*** AND THE SAME RULE GOVERNS COUNTS, NOT ONLY SCORES. Adjudicated 2026-08-26 after an
3326:audit of this whole file found the transcribed count to be its single largest decayed class. ***
3327:
3328:> **A COUNT WRITTEN INTO A DURABLE RECORD NAMES ITS DERIVER** -- the command, script, manifest
3329:> or log that produces it -- **or carries its method and date inline. And a count being
3330:> CORRECTED may never land as a fresh bare literal: it lands as deriver-plus-demoted-prose.**
3331:>
3332:> **STOP CONDITION, in the same sentence because that is what keeps it from becoming `#392`:**
3333:> it fires only on NEW and CORRECTED counts, at writing time; it audits NOTHING retroactively;
3334:> it builds NO checker; and the ~70 legacy counts stay governed by the existing read-side rule
3335:> -- re-derive an exemplar before leaning on it, and FILE anything found decayed rather than
3336:> sweeping it.
3337:>
3338:> #### *** AND THE DERIVER MUST KEY ON WHAT DEFINES THE CLASS, NOT ON A CORRELATE OF IT.
3339:> #### Measured 2026-08-31 after THREE name-based censuses of one class all came back wrong,
3340:> #### in both directions -- and the third was mine, using the deriver I had just written
3341:> #### telling other people not to transcribe numbers. ***
3342:>
3343:> `staticlatchscope` counts function-static warn-once latches. Every census pattern keyed on
3344:> the VARIABLE BEING CALLED `w`/`warned`/`first`, and the population's names run **`warned` 13,
3345:> `w` 6, `first` 2, plus six singletons** (`warned2`, `warn_fs`, `warn`, `w_warned`,
3346:> `scc_oob_warned`, `r_warned`). Counts came back 10/3, then 23/7, then 26/10.
3347:>
3348:> **A latch is defined by its SCOPE and its USE, not by its spelling**, so a name pattern cannot
3349:> enumerate that class at all -- and every attempt undercounted while looking authoritative.
3350:> Keyed on scope (`grep -rc -e '^[[:space:]]\+static int ' -e '{[[:space:]]*static int '`) it is
3351:> **30 sites across 13 files, of which 28 across 11 are latches** -- the two exclusions being
3352:> periodic counters that the name patterns had also silently swept in.
3353:>
3354:> > **BEFORE WRITING A DERIVER, ASK WHAT MAKES A MEMBER A MEMBER.** If the answer is a property
3355:> > the regex does not test -- scope, position, use, type -- the deriver is measuring a
3356:> > correlate, and a correlate's error is invisible because the output is still a plausible
3357:> > number. **This is the check-that-cannot-fail family's counting cousin: it returns the same
3358:> > shape of answer whether or not it is enumerating the right set.**
3359:>
3360:> *Cheap tell, and it is the one that broke this open: when two derivers disagree, neither is
3361:> trustworthy until you look at what each MATCHED. Four methods gave four answers here, and
3362:> printing the per-file breakdown -- not the total -- was what showed three files missing
3363:> entirely and one file counted at double its real population.*
3364:
3365:**THE HONEST SEVERITY, because the ruling turned on it: NO STALE COUNT HAS EVER CHANGED A
3366:DECISION.** The adjudicator hunted for one and came back negative, checking every candidate
3367:where a count sat near a decision -- `exitsweep`'s census (re-derived by a seven-seat panel
3368:BEFORE the round acted, and its bound deliberately written *"as a condition rather than a
3369:number"*), gate 9's 23-vs-41 (the gate already refuses the class in its own words), and the
3370:house-idiom's wrong marker spelling (all three sites predate the clause that misnamed them).
3371:**Transcribed counts have cost audit expense and reader distrust; they have cost zero
3372:decisions.** That is written down so nobody re-argues this class as though it were urgent.
3373:
3374:*And the near-misses are NOT in this class, which is the sharpest thing the hunt produced:* the
3375:one measured case of a stale number changing a decision was an **absence claim** -- a five-seat
3376:panel voting to drop a row on a sentence derived at 7 differentials and quoted at 13, resolved
3377:by a targeted CHECK (`check_optpin.py`, whose header states the maxim: *"An audit is a claim
3378:with a shelf life; a check is not"*). The other decision-costing stale facts on record are
3379:EXPIRED MEASUREMENTS AND ROSTERS -- the ~20-minute Ollama spacing that rationed panels for a
3380:week, the kimi substitute left unfired, "no SH manual" load-bearing for four rounds.
3381:
3382:**WHY NO CHECKER, and it is a measurement rather than a preference.** This file carries **33**
3383:digit+noun counts and **67 WORD-number forms** (`ONE LESSON`, `TWO INSTANCES`, `nine seats`) --
3384:**the blind spot is twice the visible population**, and the word forms are overwhelmingly
3385:rhetorical structure rather than facts. A checker policing the minority of a class while
3386:alarming on prose is the switched-off-check shape this file already names, and section U's own
3387:header states the principle: *guessing would produce false positives, which is how a check gets
3388:ignored.*
3389:
3390:> **PRE-SPECIFIED ESCALATION, so nobody re-argues it later: the FIRST measured decision that
3391:> consumed a stale count converts this writing rule into the `check_cited_artifacts.py` idiom**
3392:> -- existence-only, forward-only from a cutoff, carrier files only. **Until that trigger
3393:> fires, no machinery.**
3394:
3395:*The rule is prose and that is deliberate, against this file's own structure-first default: the
3396:adjudicator rejected both mechanical options on measurements, and a prose rule with a named
3397:stop condition and a pre-specified escalation trigger is what the evidence supports. Recorded
3398:here rather than as a checker because the evidence says a checker would be switched off.*
3399:
3400:*** A SCORE CLAIM MUST NAME THE LOG THAT PRODUCED IT. Adopted 2026-08-23 after the
3401:FOURTH generation of the frozen-number defect appeared in one round -- inside the
3402:sentence memorialising the third. ***
3403:
3404:`#461` wrote `8/32 pre-fix ... both MEASURED at 32 rows` into a heading and a gate
3405:comment. The heading was written at 17:33; **the 32-row run started at 17:50.** The
3406:only pre-fix number on record was 8/31, and the 8 had been carried forward when the
3407:row count grew. A seat caught it by comparing mtimes.
3408:
3409:**Section U cannot catch this class and never will.** It checks DENOMINATORS against
3410:the gate pin, mechanically and well. The defect here is the NUMERATOR and the word
3411:*measured* -- and no comparison against a pin can tell a measured 8 from a carried 8.
3412:
3413:> **THE RULE: a score claim names its artifact — `8/32 pre-fix (kill461e.out)`.**
3414:>
3415:> ### *** AND THE ARTIFACT MUST BE IMMUTABLE, OR THE CITATION DECAYS SILENTLY.
3416:> ### Measured on my own harness, 2026-08-24. ***
3417:>
3418:> A driver that writes `probe_<mutant>.raw` and truncates its results file **reuses those
3419:> names on every run**. So a record citing them is true only until the next run — and
3420:> then it is worse than uncited, because the named log now says something ELSE.
3421:>
3422:> Concretely: `#462`'s round block cited `probe_base.raw` for **52/52**. A second table
3423:> at 57 rows overwrote that file, and the citation now points at a 57-row log. **The
3424:> claim did not become unverifiable; it became CONTRADICTED BY ITS OWN EVIDENCE** —
3425:> exactly the shape that made the mislabelled "PRE-FIX" caption detectable, arrived at
3426:> from the opposite direction.
3427:>
3428:> **Two rules, and the second is the one that costs nothing:**
3429:> * **A measurement cited in a durable record must live at a durable path.** Stamp the
3430:>   run (`table_<n>/`, or a timestamp) or copy the log beside the record. A pointer into
3431:>   a buffer that gets reused is not a citation.
3432:> * **Before any re-run that reuses log names, PRESERVE the prior run's logs first.**
3433:>   Ten of twelve were saved here only because the question was asked while the re-run
3434:>   was still early; `probe_base.raw` and `probe_Mb1.raw` were already gone.
3435:>
3436:> *The scores themselves survived — because they had been written into the records at the
3437:> time. **The transcription outlived the evidence**, which is precisely the situation the
3438:> names-its-log rule exists to prevent, reached by a route the rule did not cover.*
3439:> That makes the claim checkable BY EXISTENCE, which is the same witness discipline
3440:> the ledger already imposes on rows, and it is the only mechanical guard available
3441:> for a number that was never run.
3442:
3443:It is the oracle-correction rule's own order discriminator applied to prose: **a
3444:number written before the run that produces it cannot be told from a number fitted
3445:to the answer**, whatever it later turns out to be. Recording it as OWED costs
3446:nothing; asserting it costs the round's credibility on every other number it states.
3447:
3448:*** AND A CORRECTION IS NOT SAFER THAN THE THING IT CORRECTS. ***  In the same round
3449:a recount of "26 of 31 rows carry a measured kill" was itself wrong twice: the seat's
3450:recount gave 25 and six, and the draft correcting it listed `dma accept` among the
3451:unkilled rows *while also saying it had been discharged*. A correction carries more
3452:authority than the error, so it earns a second reading, not less of one -- which is
3453:what the existing "budget a second review pass for any records-correction round"
3454:note already says, now with a third instance.
3455:
3456:> ### *** THE CITATION-FORM RULE -- STANDING as of 2026-08-26, after the same remedy was
3457:> ### adopted FOUR SEPARATE TIMES round-locally and never carried, while the class bit EIGHT
3458:> ### times. Adjudicated, and the mechanical check it was competing with was REJECTED ON
3459:> ### MEASUREMENT. ***
3460:>
3461:> > **Line numbers only ACROSS files, with the qualifier ON the citation. SAME-FILE references
3462:> > POSITIONAL or by CONSTRUCT NAME. Durable cross-file references as VERIFIED-UNIQUE GREP
3463:> > ANCHORS.**
3464:>
3465:> **It removes the measured population: 7 of the 8 recorded instances were same-file or bare
3466:> forms the rule eliminates outright.** And it is structure in the sense this file means --
3467:> it lives where citations are WRITTEN and it fails at the point of USE, because a grep anchor
3468:> that matches nothing is loud, while a wrong number is silent.
3469:>
3470:> *** THE CHECKER WAS REJECTED, AND ITS NUMBERS ARE THE REASON THIS RULE IS PROSE. *** The
3471:> proposal was: warn when an added `file.ext:NNN` names a file the same diff also edits.
3472:> Measured over 429 commits -- **it fires on 57 commits / 263 citations (~11 a week) and its
3473:> recall on the eight recorded instances is ZERO**, because every recorded instance is a BARE
3474:> `:NNN` that section D's `[a-z0-9_]+\.(c|h):[0-9]+` cannot see. On the motivating round
3475:> it emits 15 warns, none of them the stale one. **A checker policing the visible minority of
3476:> a class while blind to its dominant form is the check-that-cannot-fail shape** -- which is
3477:> what the proposal had accused section D of being.
3478:>
3479:> **AND THE CLASS IS WEAKER THAN THE COUNTS CLASS THAT WAS ALREADY DECLINED, for a structural
3480:> reason worth knowing: NO GATE, PROBE OR SCRIPT IN THIS TREE READS A LINE CITATION
3481:> FUNCTIONALLY.** Gates key on grep anchors and content. A citation is consumed only by a
3482:> READER -- and a reader using one opens the file, so **a drifted number self-refutes at the
3483:> point of use.** A carried count does not. That is why all eight instances were caught by a
3484:> seat or a re-read, and why the hunt for a consumed decision came back empty across 135
3485:> "stale" mentions in the CHANGELOG.
3486:>
3487:> **PRE-SPECIFIED ESCALATION:** the first MEASURED decision consumed by a stale line citation
3488:> -- a fix mis-aimed by one, a gate passing falsely, a seat verifying green against wrong
3489:> content -- converts this into an anchor-verification check bounded to ADDED citations,
3490:> forward-only from a cutoff. **Until that trigger fires, no machinery.**
3491:
3492:**A LINE NUMBER IN A COMMENT IS INVALIDATED BY YOUR OWN EDITS TO THAT FILE.** The same
3493:round cited `dev_sh4.c:2379-2381` correctly, then inserted a seven-line comment ABOVE it
3494:in a later step, moving the target to `:2386-2388` and making its own fresh citation
3495:wrong. **Re-grep every same-file citation AFTER the last edit to that file**, not when
3496:first written.
3497:
3498:This is the `#458` case of the same class as the hand-assembled-encoding trap: **a wrong
3499:constant is invisible when the probe reads the same constant**, so the check has to come
3500:from outside the code entirely.
3501:
3502:A filed item (`sh4aaalias`) was withdrawn on that reading. **Obtaining the manual cost one
3503:download and refuted a wrong finding in the same session it was raised.**
3504:
3505:**The ARM manual was ABSENT until 2026-08-11.** Rounds #357/#362/#365 cite DDI 0100I page
3506:numbers that came from **panel LLM output, not a local manual** — the load-bearing ones were
3507:later checked against a genuine copy and held, but that is luck plus good seats, not process.
3508:Cite only what you have read in these files. Two things a real reading corrected: **A2.8.3
3509:does not exist** (A2.8 has only A2.8.1 and A2.8.2), and the endian-specific rotation statement
3510:is not in A2.8 at all — it is in **LDR's own Alignment note, p. A4-44**.
3511:
3512:## Build/measure quick facts
3513:
3514:- Build inside `wsl -d Gentoo` (default Ubuntu lacks make); `rm src/cpus/*.o` first.
3515:  *** THE STATED REASON FOR THAT `rm` WAS FALSE, and it has been repeated in every
3516:  round and every seat brief. Measured 2026-08-26. *** This said the `#include`d `.c`
3517:  files are "invisible to the dep rules". **They are not.** `src/cpus/Makefile:214`
3518:  -- the GENERATED makefile make actually reads -- contains
3519:  `cpu_sh.o: cpu_sh.c cpu_sh_instr.c cpu_dyntrans.c memory_rw.c tmp_sh_head.c
3520:  tmp_sh_tail.c`, and `Makefile.skel:194` is where it comes from. Make tracks them.
3521:
3522:  **The `rm` survives as a CONSERVATIVE CLEAN-BUILD precaution, not as a necessity**,
3523:  and nobody has measured whether it is needed at all. **That is worth measuring,
3524:  because it is not free:** a mutant table rebuilds per mutant (one round built NINE
3525:  binaries), and if incremental builds are sound there, mutant-table time -- the
3526:  dominant cost of a round -- drops substantially. Filed as `depsrm`.
3527:
3528:  Until it is measured, KEEP the `rm`: an unnecessary rebuild costs minutes, while a
3529:  stale object costs a wrong measurement, and this project has already paid that once.
3530:- est/ and GXEMUL-SEC differ by the paths declared in `tools/est_divergence.txt` -- *** COUNT DELIBERATELY NOT RESTATED HERE, and the reason is that this line has now been wrong TWICE. ***  It said **SEVEN**; the file holds **six** non-comment entries (`grep -vcE '^\s*(#|$)'`).  The paragraph diagnoses the failure, prescribes the right remedy -- *derive the allowlist rather than quoting a count* -- and then commits the failure in its own headline number.  **Run the deriver.**  *(For the record, this line was ALREADY a correction of an even earlier one, which said not "5 named files +
3531:  `dev_ne2000.c`" -- the omitted one is `src/devices/.index`. The list is
3532:  `tools/est_divergence.txt` and `precommit_check.sh` reads it, so **derive the
3533:  allowlist rather than quoting a count**.  So the instruction has been right all along and the number beside it has been wrong twice.)*
3534:  Any other shared file must land byte-identical in both. Commit only GXEMUL-SEC files.
3535:- Debugger `dump` reads device state through `memory_rw`, so **read SH4 device state with
3536:  guest loads via `step`** -- but the REASON given here was a blanket claim and it is false.
3537:  *** CORRECTED 2026-08-26. *** `debugger_cmds.c:716` calls `memory_rw`, and
3538:  `memory_rw.c:585` DOES dispatch to handlers registered
3539:  `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS`. What makes the advice true for SH4 is narrower and
3540:  better: **SH4 registers `DM_DEFAULT`** (`dev_sh4.c:2860`, `:2867`), so ITS handler is
3541:  bypassed. A device registered the other way WOULD be reached by `dump`. (There is also no
3542:  `examine` command in the tree today.)
3543:- *** A SINGLE-STEPPING PROBE CANNOT OBSERVE INTERRUPT DELIVERY, EVER. *** `cpu_dyntrans.c:204-206`
3544:  reads `/* Do not cause interrupts while single-stepping. It is so horribly annoying. */`
3545:  followed by `if (!single_step) {`. **Every SH probe in this tree single-stepped** when this was written, so none of
3546:  them could see an interrupt arrive -- *and that is no longer universally true, because the
3547:  tree then ADDED the free-running shape this very paragraph describes:*
3548:  `sh_intprio_probe.py` and `sh_iprbcd_probe.py` both breakpoint-and-`continue` today.
3549:  **The suppression rule below is unchanged and still governs any probe that DOES step**;
3550:  what expired is the claim that they all do — a step-based oracle reads "not delivered" on a correct
3551:  build and on a mutant alike, which is the vacuous shape in its purest form. This is why
3552:  `sh4dmacie` was mis-ranked for weeks as needing "a new capability": the EXPEVT/INTEVT
3553:  registers were readable by an ordinary guest load all along (`0xff000024`/`0xff000028`, served
3554:  by `dev_sh4.c`); what was missing was a probe SHAPE, not emulator support.
3555:  **The shape that works, measured:** free-running with `sr=0x40000000`, `vbr` pointed into
3556:  RAM, a `bra .` self-loop after the triggering store, a breakpoint at `VBR+0x600`, then
3557:  `continue`. Interrupt levels are hardcoded (IRQ10 is level 5 at `cpu_sh.c:255-258`), so
3558:  IMASK must be low enough to admit the one under test.
3559:  *** BUT "DELIVERY" IS THE EXACT WORD, AND READING THIS RULE MORE BROADLY COSTS A PROBE.
3560:  Refined 2026-08-22, verified by opening all three files. *** The guard suppresses the
3561:  EXCEPTION DISPATCH only. **The ASSERT path runs perfectly well while stepping**, by two
3562:  routes: a device handler asserts SYNCHRONOUSLY inside the guest's own store instruction.
3563:  *** THE PARENTHESIS HERE SAID "nothing in `memory_rw` or device dispatch consults
3564:  `single_step`", AND HALF OF IT IS FALSE. Corrected 2026-08-26. *** `memory_rw.c:284`
3565:  reads `!single_step && !about_to_enter_single_step &&` -- for WATCHPOINTS. **The
3566:  conclusion survives on the half that is true:** DEVICE DISPATCH carries no single-step
3567:  guard, which is what makes a synchronous assert visible while stepping. The watchpoint
3568:  path is a different mechanism and does not gate device access, and **tick functions
3569:  run under stepping too** — `machine.c:726-728` is
3570:  `ticks_till_next[te] -= single_step ? 1 : N_SAFE_DYNTRANS_LIMIT;`, so they still fire, just
3571:  one tick at a time.
3572:  *** AND THAT IS STILL NOT THE WHOLE RULE — corrected AGAIN the same day, by a panel seat,
3573:  after the paragraph above had already been written into the carrier and a witness designed
3574:  on it. VERIFY BEFORE REUSING EITHER HALF. *** A tick function RUNS under stepping but does
3575:  no work if what it consumes is produced by a HOST TIMER: `debugger.c:800` calls
3576:  `timer_stop()` "while interacting with the user", and the step command returns at
3577:  `:835-836` **before** `timer_start()` at `:840`, so `timer_add`/SIGALRM callbacks are
3578:  stopped for the entire `step N`. `sh4_timer_tick()` therefore never runs, `DEVICE_TICK`
3579:  finds `timer_interrupts_pending == 0`, and nothing asserts.
3580:  > **THE RULE, BY ITS DISCRIMINATOR:** a step-based probe sees an assert that is
3581:  > **SYNCHRONOUS** — a device handler reached inside the guest's own load or store. It does
3582:  > NOT see one that depends on a host-timer callback, however much the tick loop appears to
3583:  > run. Ask *"what produces the thing the tick consumes?"* — if the answer is `timer_add`,
3584:  > the probe must free-run.
3585:  The case that forced the first correction was `cpu_sh.c:312-316`, whose `exit(1)` sits in
3586:  the assert path; the case that forced the second was the WITNESS for that same defect,
3587:  where the TMU route is a host-timer route and only the `testsh` IPI route is synchronous.
3588:  **Three passes over one sentence, each narrower than the last — the general lesson is that
3589:  "runs under stepping" and "does its work under stepping" are different claims, and this
3590:  file conflated them twice.** Instruction-combination work needs FREE-RUNNING execution: the
3591:  #340 two-pass loop driver, breakpoint AFTER the sequence, instruction tracing OFF
3592:  (recipe in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md, 2026-08-03 entry).
3593:- Probe harness: `_scratchpad/gxprobe.py` (don't hand-roll pty plumbing). Reconstruct the
3594:  arc screen (`r49_screen.py` pattern) for BOOT-PROOF tokens.
3595:  *** "NEVER grep the raw arc pty log" IS TOO STRONG, and the two sources are
3596:  COMPLEMENTARY rather than one-right-one-wrong. Corrected 2026-08-26. ***
3597:  **Gate 4 obeys the rule and always did** -- `gate_mips.sh:62` rebuilds via
3598:  `r49_screen.py` and `:64`/`:66` grep the REBUILD, because arc's VGA console
3599:  repaints DIFFERENTIALLY and a raw grep misses tokens.
3600:  **Gate 6 deliberately accepts EITHER** (`gate_hygiene.sh:402`), and its own comment
3601:  at `:11` records the measured reason: `'{ asc: data in'` reads **0 from the screen
3602:  rebuild and 5 from the raw log**. *The rebuild is required to avoid
3603:  differential-repaint FALSE NEGATIVES; the raw log is required for content the
3604:  rebuild DROPS.* Choose by what you are looking for, not by a blanket ban.
3605:  *(An auditing seat reported that gate 4 greps the raw log. It does not -- it greps
3606:  `arc.screen`. The seat was right that "never" is false and wrong about which gate,
3607:  which is why this was re-derived rather than transcribed.)*
3608:*** AND THE OPPOSITE FAILURE ALSO EXISTS: A PLAIN `&` IN A BASH CALL DOES *NOT*
3609:SURVIVE, AND IT FAILS SILENTLY WITH A ZERO-BYTE LOG. Measured 2026-08-23. ***
3610:
3611:Two measurements were launched as `MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 wsl -d Gentoo -- bash ... &`
3612:inside a Bash tool call. Both produced **zero-byte output files**: the `wsl.exe`
3613:child is killed when that invocation's shell exits, so the run never happened.
3614:
3615:**It looks exactly like a run still in progress.** `ps aux` shows nothing, the log
3616:is empty, and the natural reading is "not finished yet" — which is how ~25 minutes
3617:went by twice before the zero byte count was noticed.
3618:
3619:*** THE TELL IS THE PROCESS, NOT THE SIZE -- and this clause said the opposite until
3620:2026-08-26. *** It read *"a live run's log grows, and a dead one is exactly 0"*.
3621:**The ledger holds the counter-example in its own records:** `agy` writes NOTHING until
3622:it finishes -- its file sat at **EXACTLY 0 bytes for minutes with a live `agy.exe`
3623:(pid 23548)**, then landed 8,044 bytes in one shot. So for agy, a zero-byte file is the
3624:NORMAL mid-run state, and the size rule would have declared a healthy seat dead.
3625:
3626:> **Zero bytes alone proves neither live nor dead. Check the PROCESS** (`ps aux` for the
3627:> child, or `tasklist` for a Windows CLI seat) **and the completion sentinel.** Size is
3628:> evidence only where the tool is known to stream -- `grok` does, agy does not, and the
3629:> two demand opposite readings of the same observation.
3630:
3631:*The original rule was minted from a real incident and its ADVICE survives: a plain `&`
3632:inside a Bash call dies silently. What failed is the DISCRIMINATOR it offered, which is
3633:the exemplar problem again -- the incident's own shape taken for the class.*
3634:
3635:**Use `run_in_background: true`** (the harness tracks it and notifies on exit).
3636:
3637:> ### *** THE PROCESS CHECK THIS FILE PRESCRIBES IS **ARGUMENT-BLIND ON THIS MACHINE**, SO IT
3638:> ### RETURNS "DEAD" FOR EVERY SCRIPT, ALWAYS.  Measured 2026-08-31 -- and I believed it, wrote
3639:> ### a false correction into this file on the strength of it, and had to take the rule back. ***
3640:>
3641:> git-bash `ps -ef` prints the **executable path and no arguments**:
3642:>
3643:>     User00    7854       1 ?  03:32:47 /c/Users/.../Python312/python
3644:>
3645:> So `ps -ef | grep -c 'panel_ollama'` returns **0** and `grep -c 'python'` returns **2**, on the
3646:> same processes, in the same second.  **Any pattern naming a SCRIPT -- `panel_ollama`,
3647:> `gate_arm.sh`, `make -j4` -- can never match, so the check answers "not running" whether or
3648:> not anything is running.**  That is this file's own check-that-cannot-fail shape, sitting
3649:> inside the remedy it recommends for the previous one: the bracket trick
3650:> (`ps -eo pid,args | grep -E "[m]ake"`) presumes an `args` column that MSYS `ps` does not have.
3651:>
3652:> **WHAT IT COST, because the damage was not the wasted check.**  Three cloud seats were
3653:> launched `nohup ... &`; the blind check said 0 alive; I concluded nohup had failed, relaunched
3654:> all three, and **ran two writers against the same three output files** -- `ollama_deepseek.txt`
3655:> was measurably truncated and rewritten, 3475 -> 2928 bytes, between two reads.  Then I wrote a
3656:> rule into this file saying *nohup is not a reliable fallback*.  **It is; the check was blind.**
3657:>
3658:> **THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT SETTLED IT, and it is available in the same command:** a python with
3659:> **PPID 1** and a start time matching the FIRST launch is an orphaned `nohup` survivor -- which
3660:> is exactly what `ps -ef` showed once I read the PPID column instead of grepping for a name.
3661:>
3662:> **SO, ON THIS MACHINE:**
3663:>
3664:>     ps -ef | grep <script-name>    -> ALWAYS 0.  Never use it.  It cannot fail.
3665:>     ps -ef, read PPID + START      -> works; PPID 1 means orphaned/nohup'd
3666:>     tasklist | grep -i '^python'   -> works (Windows-native; image name, still not args)
3667:>     the redirect target's SIZE     -> works, and is the cheapest of the three
3668:>
3669:> **And the standing advice is unchanged and is what avoids all of this: long jobs go to
3670:> `run_in_background`, which the harness tracks and which notifies on exit** -- no process check
3671:> needed, and no chance of a second launch racing the first.
3672:>
3673:> *Recorded in this shape because the near-miss is the instructive part: I ran a check, got a
3674:> confident answer, acted on it, and the action created a real defect (duplicate writers) that
3675:> nothing would have reported.  The file changing size between two of my own `ls` calls is what
3676:> gave it away -- a contradiction, which this file already names as the cheapest signal there is.*
3677:
3678:*Note this is the MIRROR of the hazard below, and the two want opposite reflexes: a
3679:TIMED-OUT foreground run keeps going invisibly, while a plain-`&` run dies
3680:invisibly. Check `ps aux` for the first; check the log's byte count for the second.*
3681:
3682:> ### *** AND ON 2026-08-27 THE RULE'S OWN TELL FAILED, WHILE THE FALLBACK CHECK LIED IN THE
3683:> ### OTHER DIRECTION. TWO APPARATUS ERRORS, STACKED, PRODUCING A CONFIDENT WRONG READING
3684:> ### THAT PERSISTED ACROSS THREE SEPARATE CHECKS. ***
3685:>
3686:> An ASAN rebuild was launched as `wsl -d Gentoo -- bash -c '... nohup make -j4 > /tmp/x.log
3687:> 2>&1 & echo started'`.  It printed `started`.  **It never ran at all.**
3688:>
3689:> * **The size tell did not apply: THERE WAS NO LOG FILE.**  The rule above says a dead run
3690:>   leaves a log of exactly 0 bytes.  That presumes the redirect happened.  Here the child was
3691:>   torn down with the invocation's shell before the redirect took effect, so `/tmp/x.log`
3692:>   **did not exist** -- and `tail` on a missing file prints nothing, which reads exactly like
3693:>   a build that has produced no output yet.
3694:>
3695:> * **`pgrep -f "make -j4"` MATCHED ITS OWN WRAPPER.**  The pattern appeared in the
3696:>   `bash -c` command line running the check, so it found itself and reported RUNNING.  Three
3697:>   consecutive polls said RUNNING with zero make processes alive.  `ps -eo pid,args | grep`
3698:>   showed the truth immediately: the only match was the checking shell.
3699:>
3700:> **Neither error is new as a class** -- the carrier already records the check-that-cannot-fail
3701:> and the pattern-that-matches-nothing.  What is new is that the FIRST rule's remedy was
3702:> defeated by the SECOND rule's failure mode, so every reading agreed with every other reading
3703:> and nothing looked wrong.
3704:>
3705:> > **THE CHEAP DISCRIMINATOR IS THE REDIRECT TARGET, NOT THE PROCESS AND NOT ITS SIZE.**  A
3706:> > shell creates the redirect file *before* it execs the command, so:
3707:> >
3708:> >     log MISSING   -> the command NEVER STARTED
3709:> >     log 0 bytes   -> started; either mid-run on a non-streaming tool, or dead
3710:> >     log growing   -> alive and streaming
3711:> >
3712:> > `ls -la <log>` separates "never started" from "started", which is the distinction both
3713:> > older rules assume you have already made and neither one gives you.
3714:>
3715:> **And when a process check is genuinely needed, it must not be able to match itself:** use
3716:> `ps -eo pid,args | grep -E "[m]ake"` (the bracket trick), or grep for something only the
3717:> real process has -- never a literal that also appears in the command you are typing.
3718:>
3719:> *The operational rule is unchanged and is the one that was ignored here: long jobs go to
3720:> `run_in_background`, which the harness tracks and which notifies on exit.  Both of these
3721:> checks exist only because a plain `&` was used in the first place.*
3722:
3723:*** A BASH-TOOL TIMEOUT DOES NOT KILL THE WSL-SIDE PROCESS, AND THE ORPHAN KEEPS
3724:WRITING THE TREE. Measured 2026-08-23, and it produced TWO CONCURRENT RUNS IN ONE
3725:MUTANT TREE. ***
3726:
3727:A kill-table script was launched in the foreground with `timeout 2400000`. **The Bash
3728:tool caps at 600000 ms**, so the wrapper died at ten minutes reporting exit 143 -- and
3729:the WSL-side script carried on building mutants. Re-launching it in the background then
3730:gave two scripts checking out different mutants into the SAME `/tmp/gx461`, each
3731:measuring a binary the other was overwriting. `ps aux` showed both, four minutes apart,
3732:and **neither measurement was worth anything**.
3733:
3734:It defeats the usual signal: the tool REPORTS a failure, so it does not look like a
3735:silent success -- but the failure it reports is *its own wrapper*, and nothing says the
3736:work is still running. **After any timed-out or interrupted WSL invocation, `ps aux`
3737:for the probe/emulator before starting anything else.** Never re-launch on the
3738:assumption that a timeout stopped the work.
3739:
3740:**Two consequences, both applied:**
3741:* Long measurements go to `run_in_background` from the start. 600000 ms is the ceiling,
3742:  and a six-probe kill table exceeds it by design, not by accident.
3743:* **THE SENTINEL RULE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND THIS SESSION'S OWN SCRIPT BROKE IT.**
3744:  `kill461b.sh` mutated `/tmp/gx461` with no `.MUTANT` file, so when it was killed
3745:  mid-mutant the tree was left mutated with nothing saying so. The dirt was found only
3746:  because a `cmp` against SEC was run by hand. A script that mutates a tree drops the
3747:  sentinel FIRST and clears it only after `cmp` against GXEMUL-SEC succeeds -- which is
3748:  what the carrier already said, and what the script did not do.
3749:
3750:*** EDITING A RUNNING SHELL SCRIPT CORRUPTS IT MID-EXECUTION. Documented for `panel.sh`
3751:at midday 2026-08-25, then committed by me against `killwdc.sh` the same evening. ***
3752:
3753:**Bash reads a script INCREMENTALLY, by byte offset.** Inserting lines into a file a shell is
3754:currently executing shifts every offset after the insertion point, and the shell resumes mid-
3755:token. The failure looks like a syntax error in a file that is perfectly valid:
3756:
3757:    killwdc.sh: line 90: syntax error near unexpected token `('
3758:    ...and `bash -n killwdc.sh` afterwards reports NO ERROR AT ALL.
3759:
3760:That disagreement is the diagnostic. **A script that fails to parse while running and parses
3761:clean afterwards was edited underneath itself** -- nothing else produces that signature.
3762:
3763:The cost here was an eight-mutant table killed on its first mutant, plus a tree left holding
3764:a sentinel. Cheap this time only because the sentinel discipline worked: the tree said what
3765:state it was in, and one restore-and-`cmp`-against-SEC settled it.
3766:
3767:> **LAUNCH LONG JOBS FROM A FROZEN COPY.** `cp script.sh script.run-<stamp>.sh` and run the
3768:> copy. Then the original is free to edit while the job runs, which is exactly when the urge
3769:> to edit it arrives -- because that is when you are watching it and thinking about it.
3770:
3771:> ### *** AND THE SAME HAZARD HAS A PYTHON FORM THAT THE BASH RULE DOES NOT COVER: A
3772:> ### MULTI-RUN SCRIPT RE-READS ITS PROBE BETWEEN RUNS. Noticed 2026-08-28, before it bit. ***
3773:>
3774:> Bash reads a script INCREMENTALLY, so editing it mid-execution corrupts THAT run -- which is
3775:> the rule above. **Python is the opposite and it is not safer: the interpreter reads the whole
3776:> file at startup, so the RUNNING invocation is immune -- and the NEXT invocation loads
3777:> whatever the file says then.**
3778:>
3779:> So a comparison driver shaped like
3780:>
3781:>     for tree in base mutant; do python3 -u probe.py $tree/gxemul "$IMG"; done
3782:>
3783:> **gives run 1 and run 2 DIFFERENT INSTRUMENTS if the probe is edited between them**, and the
3784:> difference lands in the very output being diffed. An edit to a row's NOTE text is enough:
3785:> the note is printed in the row line, so a records-only correction shows up as an apparent
3786:> behavioural difference between base and mutant.
3787:>
3788:> > **A COMPARISON RUN OWES ONE INSTRUMENT. Freeze the probe (`cp`) for the duration, or make
3789:> > every edit BEFORE the driver starts and say so.** The bash rule protects the run in flight;
3790:> > this protects the COMPARISON, which is the thing the measurement actually rests on.
3791:>
3792:> *Recorded because it did not bite, not because it did: the edits happened to precede the
3793:> launch. The near-miss is the useful part -- the failure would have presented as a real
3794:> difference between two trees, which is exactly what the run exists to detect, and nothing
3795:> in the output would have said the instrument changed.*
3796:
3797:*I knew this rule, had written it down hours earlier for a different script, correctly held
3798:off editing `wdc_flood_probe.py` for the same reason -- and then edited the driver. The
3799:probe was protected because the hazard was FRONT OF MIND FOR THE PROBE. Knowing a rule and
3800:having it fire on the right object are different things, which is the argument for making it
3801:STRUCTURAL (a frozen copy) rather than remembered.*
3802:
3803:*** AND `pkill -f <script>` KILLS THE SHELL RUNNING IT. ***  The cleanup command was
3804:itself a WSL bash invocation whose command line matched the pattern, so it terminated
3805:itself and reported exit 15 before printing its verification. Pattern-kill and then
3806:verify in a SEPARATE invocation; a cleanup that cannot report its own result is a
3807:cleanup you have to redo.
