18130:**Distinct from `shiftubcensus`**, which is the SIGNED-1 family (`1 << n` with a plain-`int` literal). This
18131:is the NEGATIVE-COUNT family: a different C99 clause and a different fix shape. Found by the GPT-6 Astra
18132:fix-design review on `armbfiwidth` while looking for siblings of that round's idiom outside ARM.
18133:
18134:### `mapledmaaddr` (HIGH) -- filed 2026-09-10
18135:
18136:`dev_dreamcast_maple.c`, grep anchor `case 0x04:	/*  MAPLE_DMAADDR  */`: **the read arm computes the
18137:correct value and then throws it away by terminating the process.** In order:
18138:
18139:```c
18140:fatal("[ dreamcast_maple: TODO: read from dmaaddr ]\n");
18141:odata = d->dmaaddr;
18142:exit(1);
18143:```
18144:
18145:The WRITE arm two lines above stores `d->dmaaddr = idata`, so the register is **fully modelled in both
18146:directions**. A guest that writes the DMA address and reads it back -- the ordinary way to confirm a
18147:controller programming step -- is killed on the read. `machine_dreamcast.c` adds `dreamcast_maple`
18148:unconditionally, and nothing has to start DMA or build a command list to get there.
18149:
18150:**This is option-set member 4 (ACCEPT) in its textbook shape**: the model already computes the right
18151:answer and then discards it, so the disposition is to delete the kill and KEEP THE VALUE, with **no
18152:latch** -- there is no event to announce, and a bounded announce would ship a wrong record in product code
18153:(*"unimplemented; ignored"* about a read that is implemented and is not ignored). **Two deleted lines.**
18154:FAULT is neither available nor appropriate: `memory_rw.c` converts a failed device callback into an
18155:architectural exception for MIPS and M88K only, and this device is on an SH4 machine -- but the access is
18156:implemented, so refusing it would be wrong even if the machinery existed.
18157:
18158:Test carrier already exists: `regress/gdrom_default_probe.py` supplies a Dreamcast session with guest
18159:load/store helpers. **The oracle must assert that a separately STORED value reads back EXACTLY and that
18160:execution continues** -- observing a zero, or a surviving debugger prompt, is vacuous, since both are true
18161:of several wrong implementations. Not measured, no witness built; the rung-3 attempt is owed.
18162:
18163:### `xscaleinten` (HIGH) -- filed 2026-09-10
18164:
18165:`cpu_arm_coproc.c`, grep anchor `void arm_coproc_i80321_6`, crm 0 / crn 0: the load arm reads
18166:
18167:```c
18168:cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;
18169:fatal("TODO: XScale read from inten?\n");
18170:exit(1);
18171:```
18172:
18173:The destination register receives the modelled value and the process then dies. The store arm immediately
18174:below writes the same field and the i80321 interrupt controller consumes it, so the register is fully
18175:modelled both ways.
18176:
18177:*** THE DECISIVE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING CASE IN THE SAME SWITCH, one screen away. *** `case 4:`
18178:(`i80321_isteer`) has the **identical structure** -- load arm assigns from the modelled field, store arm
18179:writes it -- and carries **no `fatal()` and no `exit()`**. So `crn 0`'s kill is an OUTLIER inside its own
18180:switch rather than a policy: the upstream-residue pattern this project has now found several times, where
18181:the author hit the exit he happened to reach while developing and not the sibling he did not. *That is a
18182:far stronger warrant than "it is a host kill", and it is one grep away* -- which is exactly what the
18183:option-set rule's sibling check is for.
18184:
18185:Reachability is explicit rather than inferred: `machine_iq80321.c` installs it with
18186:`cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = arm_coproc_i80321_6;`, and Iyonix does the same. Emulated firmware reading its
18187:own interrupt-enable register is the natural consumer, and the generic coprocessor dispatcher enforces no
18188:supervisor-only precondition.
18189:
18190:Disposition: **member 4 (ACCEPT)**, two lines deleted, no latch, for the same reason as `mapledmaaddr`.
18191:FAULT is unavailable regardless -- the failed-device exception path is MIPS and M88K only, and this is
18192:ARM. Not measured, no witness built. Note the witness needs a **coprocessor read from an ARM guest**,
18193:which is a different vehicle from the device probes this project mostly writes; `gate_arm.sh`'s decode
18194:checks are the nearest existing machinery.
18195:
18305:(`n6` holds eight footbridge sites, `alphavmkill` one, `sgirekills` five, `lcaisalane` and `lcabadval`
18306:eight between them, `m88kexit` two dead ones). A strict open/held filter leaves 83, seven of which belong
18307:to in-work LCA repairs, so **the unowned remainder is 76**.
18308:
18309:*** One of my own earlier qualifications was too aggressive and is corrected here: lacking a separate
18310:CHILD repair id does not mean lacking an open OWNING row. *** `sgirekills` explicitly retains its five
18311:command exits. Conversely, an incidental mention in census prose is not a repair row.
18312:
18313:**Six E1 predicates** -- a one-to-five-line change with an existing test carrier -- were identified and
18314:**all six are now filed as their own rows**: `mk48nvramkill`, `mapledmaaddr`, `xscaleinten`,
18315:`crimewidthkill`, `vmedefaultkill`, `fbctrlportkill`. This row therefore tracks the ~70 that remain, and
18316:they are E2/E3: real rounds, not quick wins. Heaviest concentrations are `dev_osiop.c` (14),
18317:`cpu_arm_coproc.c` (8, one filed), `dev_adb.c` (7), `dev_dreamcast_g2.c` (6), `dev_pvr.c` and
18318:`dev_igsfb.c` (5 each).
18319:
18320:**The one constraint governing every disposition in this body, and it is easy to forget:** `memory_rw.c`
18321:turns a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for **MIPS and M88K only**.
18322:Returning zero from an ARM or SH device callback gives no bus-error delivery, so the FAULT member is
18323:unavailable there and a round reaching for it owes the exception path first. CPU translation and
18324:instruction handlers have their own direct mechanisms, which is a separate question.
1322:> ### *** "THE PREDICATE THE FIX INTRODUCED" ENUMERATED THE EMPTY SET FOR MOST OF THIS
1323:> ### QUEUE, AND NOBODY NOTICED FOR THREE ROUNDS. Corrected 2026-08-24 -- clause (d) now
1324:> ### reads DEPENDS ON, not INTRODUCED. ***
1325:>
1326:> Clause (d) says *keep the guard, null its repair*. As written it enumerated only
1327:> predicates the diff ADDS -- and **the two commonest fix shapes here add none**:
1328:>
1329:> * a **pure deletion** introduces no predicate at all;
1330:> * a **DE-FATALISATION** introduces none either -- it changes what an EXISTING
1331:>   predicate's branch does. That is most of this project's queue.
1332:>
1333:> So for those rounds clause (d) enumerated **nothing**, and a round could satisfy the
1334:> floor while having built no (d) mutant whatever. `#461` measured (d) successfully only
1335:> because its fix happened to ADD a predicate (an alignment mask) for the mutant to weaken.
1336:>
1337:> **Reading it as DEPENDS ON fixes both cases at once**, and it is why this now replaces a
1338:> narrower deletion-only rule written earlier the same day: that rule was a special case of
1339:> this gap, and two overlapping statements of one rule is the accumulation this carrier is
1340:> supposed to refuse.
1341:>
1342:> **The two shapes, once the enumeration is right:**
1343:> * **fix ADDS a predicate** -> weaken the added one (unchanged; the original rule).
1344:> * **fix adds none** -> the (d) mutant reroutes an EXISTING guard's repair. For a
1345:>   de-fatalisation: **swap the guard's operand for a field the guarded BODY reads.**
1346:>   For a pure deletion: **re-introduce the removed behaviour under a DIFFERENT predicate**
1347:>   -- not the same one widened, which is (b) wearing (d)'s clothes.
1348:>
1349:> *** THE MEASURED CASE, and it is why this matters rather than being tidy: ***
1350:> `#462` de-fatalised two arms. Its guard reads `d->data`; the body it guards reads
1351:> `d->data` AND `d->data_len`; the two are set together in one place and cleared in two
1352:> places that touch only ONE of them. Swapping the guard's operand to `d->data_len == 0`
1353:> **scored a CLEAN SWEEP on a 52-row detector that had just passed twelve mutants**, and
1354:> restored a NULL-pointer read of 408 bytes. Under the old wording that was coverage debt;
1355:> under DEPENDS ON it is a **floor breach, L2, fix in-round** -- which is what the round
1356:> did, before the wording was corrected.
1357:>
1358:> #### *** THE BOUND, and it is not optional: "DEPENDS ON" INVITES TRANSITIVE DATA-FLOW
1359:> #### CHASING, which is unbounded and is how this becomes `#392`. Named 2026-08-24 by the
1360:> #### adjudicating seat, in the same breath as upholding the correction. ***
1361:>

